arXiv:2211.15970v2 [math.SG] 24 May 2023</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:173%;">Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds
<br class="ltx_break"> 
<br class="ltx_break"></span></h1>

<div id="id2" class="ltx_logical-block">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Michael Entov,
Misha Verbitsky<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          Partially supported by HSE University Basic Research Program,
FAPERJ E-26/202.912/2018 and CNPq - Process 310952/2021-2.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:144%;">Contents</span><span id="p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"><span id="p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Consider a symplectic embedding of a disjoint union of domains (lying in the standard symplectic <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>) into a symplectic manifold <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say that such
an embedding is Kähler-type, or respectively tame, if it is holomorphic with respect to some (not a priori fixed, Kähler-type)
integrable
complex structure on <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with the symplectic form, or respectively tamed by it. Assume that <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is either of the following: a complex projective space (with the standard symplectic form);
an even-dimensional torus, or a K3 surface, equipped with an irrational Kähler-type symplectic form. Then any two Kähler-type embeddings of a disjoint union of balls into <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> can be mapped into each other by a symplectomorphism acting trivially on homology. If the embeddings are holomorphic with respect to complex structures compatible with the symplectic form and lying in the same connected component of the space of Kähler-type complex structures on <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, then
the symplectomorphism can be chosen to be smoothly isotopic to the identity. For certain <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and certain disjoint unions of balls we describe precisely the obstructions to the existence of Kähler-type embeddings of the balls into <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, symplectic volume is the only obstruction for the existence of Kähler-type embeddings of <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> equal balls (for any <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>) into <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with the standard symplectic form and of any number of possibly different balls into a torus or a K3 surface, equipped with an irrational symplectic form.
We also show that symplectic volume is the only obstruction for the existence of tame embeddings of disjoint unions of equal balls, polydisks, or parallelepipeds, into a torus equipped with a generic Kähler-type symplectic form. For balls and parallelepipeds the same is true for K3 surfaces.</span></p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Symplectic embeddings of balls and other domains into symplectic manifolds have been studied since the early days of symplectic topology.
So far, the existence of the embeddings (the so-called symplectic packing problem) got most of attention and much less is known
about the connectedness of the spaces of such embeddings. The notable exception
is the classical result of McDuff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx67" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD3</a>]</cite> about the connectedness of the space of symplectic embeddings of disjoint unions of balls into certain 4-dimensional symplectic manifolds – in particular, into <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and their blow-ups (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD1</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx66" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD2</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lal</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir1</a>]</cite> for earlier partial results on the subject and
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx68" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD4</a>]</cite>,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLW</a>]</cite>,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cr</a>]</cite>
for more recent extensions of McDuff’s result).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, in an attempt to get more information about the connectedness question for symplectic manifolds of dimension higher than 4, we consider a more restrictive class of
symplectic embeddings of disjoint unions of domains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Namely, let <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a
manifold equipped with a
<span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kähler-type symplectic form</span> <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> – i.e., a symplectic form compatible with some (<span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">not a priori fixed</span>)
integrable complex structure on <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>; such complex structures will be called <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kähler-type complex structures</span>.
We will denote by <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the space of Kähler-type complex structures on <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We consider symplectic embeddings (of disjoint unions of domains) into <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> that are holomorphic with respect to some <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(not a priori fixed)</span> integrable (Kähler-type) complex structure <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> which is compatible with <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, or tamed by it.
We will say that such a symplectic embedding is of <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kähler type</span>, or, respectively, <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">tame</span>.
The existence of Kähler-type embeddings, <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">for a fixed isotopy class of the complex structure <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math></span>, has been previously studied – in a different guise –
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ec</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fl</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx64" title="" class="ltx_ref">LuWa</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tr</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx102" title="" class="ltx_ref">WN1</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx103" title="" class="ltx_ref">WN2</a>]</cite>, see <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Kähler-type and tame embeddings ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and part 3 of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">With this terminology, we have the following hierarchy of properties of the embeddings:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext='\textrm{K\"{a}hler-type}\ \Longrightarrow\ \textrm{tame}\Longrightarrow\ \textrm{symplectic}.' display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Kähler-type</mtext><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em" stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>tame</mtext><mo rspace="0.778em" stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>symplectic</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{K\"{a}hler-type}\ \Longrightarrow\ \textrm{tame}\Longrightarrow\ \textrm{symplectic}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">A Kähler-type embedding of a domain in <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> (equipped with the standard symplectic and complex structures) into <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> can be equivalently described as a Kähler-isometric, or just Kähler, embedding of the domain into <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with the Kähler metric <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega(\cdot,I\cdot)+\sqrt{-1}\omega(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(\cdot,I\cdot)+\sqrt{-1}\omega(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In other words, an embedding of a domain into <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is of Kähler type if and only if it is Kähler with respect to some Kähler metric whose imaginary part is <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. The restriction of the Kähler metric to the image of the embedding is the standard flat metric.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">There do exist symplectic embeddings of balls that are not of Kähler type (see below). We do not know whether these
symplectic embeddings are tame or whether there exist tame embeddings (of balls or any other domains) that are not of Kähler-type – see <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> and
<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. Let us note that for a disjoint union of starshaped domains (with a piecewise-smooth boundary)
its Kähler-type and tame
embeddings form <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant open subsets of the space of its symplectic embeddings into <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> (with respect to the <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-topology) – see Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 The Symp(𝑀,𝜔)∩Diff_0⁢(𝑀)-action on the space of Kähler-type embeddings ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">As far as the connectedness of the spaces of Kähler-type embeddings is concerned, let us note first that a Kähler embedding into <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> may be holomorphic with respect to different complex structures compatible with <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, and, second, that two Kähler-type embeddings lying in the same <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit have to be holomorphic with respect to complex structures lying in the same orbit of the <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-action on the space <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> of Kähler-type complex structures on <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>,
hence in the same connected component of the set of complex structures on <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Unfortunately, we know almost nothing about the latter connected components or about the space <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)/\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)/\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. Because of this, we cannot say much about the orbits of the <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-action on the space of Kähler-type embeddings of
given domains into <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, we can say more about the actions of <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
and of the symplectic Torelli group <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> (the group of symplectomorphisms of <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> acting trivially on the homology)
on the spaces of Kähler-type embeddings of balls into <math id="S1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Namely, assume that <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is either of the following:
<math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with the standard symplectic form; <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> or a K3 surface equipped with a Kähler-type symplectic form <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> which is irrational (i.e., the real cohomology class <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is not a real multiple of a rational one). Then
any two
Kähler-type embeddings of an arbitrary disjoint union of balls
into <math id="S1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> can be mapped into each other by
the action of <math id="S1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. If these embeddings are holomorphic
with respect to complex structures compatible with <math id="S1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and lying in the same connected component of
<math id="S1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, then these embeddings can be mapped into each other by
the action of <math id="S1.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, these two Kähler-type embeddings are holomorphic with respect to isotopic
complex structures compatible with <math id="S1.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Note that if <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a rational or ruled closed symplectic 4-manifold, or if <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from a rational or ruled closed symplectic 4-manifold by removing either a closed 2-dimensional symplectic submanifold or a Lagrangian submanifold diffeomorphic to <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (for instance, if <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard symplectic ball), then
any two symplectic
embeddings of an arbitrary disjoint union of closed balls
into <math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> can be mapped into each other by
the action of <math id="S1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> – see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx67" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD3</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLW</a>]</cite> (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD1</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx66" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD2</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lal</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir1</a>]</cite> for earlier partial results on the subject).
In particular, this is true for any two Kähler-type, or tame, embeddings and thus the space of such embeddings is connected (see <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> below).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Let us now discuss our results on the existence of Kähler-type and tame embeddings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Following the terminology in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>]</cite>, we say that Kähler-type,
or tame,
embeddings of a disjoint union <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of compact domains with piecewise boundary into <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are <span id="S1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unobstructed</span> if there are no obstructions for the existence of such embeddings
of <math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}\lambda W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}\lambda W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, into <math id="S1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> other than the symplectic volume. (The domains we consider
always lie in the standard symplectic <math id="S1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.) Note that if Kähler-type embeddings of a disjoint union of domains into <math id="S1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed, this means, in particular, that one can choose a complex structure <math id="S1.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S1.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> so that the Kähler metric <math id="S1.p12.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega(\cdot,I\cdot)+\sqrt{-1}\omega(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(\cdot,I\cdot)+\sqrt{-1}\omega(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is flat outside a set of arbitrarily small volume.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Since the classical work of McDuff-Polterovich <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>]</cite>, it has been known that proving the existence of symplectic embeddings of closed balls <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> of radii <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> into a closed symplectic manifold <math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to showing that an appropriate cohomology class of the complex blow-up <math id="S1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> points can be represented by a symplectic form with suitable properties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper we extend the results of McDuff and Polterovich <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>]</cite> to Kähler-type embeddings of <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Namely, assume <math id="S1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is closed. For each <math id="S1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, and each complex structure <math id="S1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by
<math id="S1.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> the complex blow-up of <math id="S1.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S1.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S1.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the lift of <math id="S1.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We show that <math id="S1.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> admits a Kähler-type embedding into <math id="S1.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
if and only if for some complex structure <math id="S1.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S1.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and some <math id="S1.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>
the cohomology class <math id="S1.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S1.p14.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., can be represented by a Kähler form on <math id="S1.p14.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}},{\widetilde{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}},{\widetilde{I}})</annotation></semantics></math>). In fact, if this cohomology class is Kähler, then <math id="S1.p14.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> admits a Kähler-type embedding into <math id="S1.p14.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S1.p14.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S1.p14.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and smoothly isotopic to <math id="S1.p14.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
Together with information on the Kähler cones of <math id="S1.p14.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}},{\widetilde{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}},{\widetilde{I}})</annotation></semantics></math> for various <math id="S1.p14.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> (compatible with <math id="S1.p14.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S1.p14.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> this allows us to show the existence of Kähler-type embeddings of <math id="S1.p14.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> into certain <math id="S1.p14.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> (see Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Here are some of the applications for particular symplectic manifolds (see Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is either the standard symplectic <math id="S1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> or a standard symplectic ball of real dimension <math id="S1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>, then for any <math id="S1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, Kähler-type embeddings of a disjoint union of <math id="S1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> equal balls are unobstructed. In particular, this implies that for the standard complex structure on <math id="S1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> equal balls of total
symplectic volume smaller than the volume of <math id="S1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a Kähler form on <math id="S1.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, isotopic to the standard Fubini-Study form, so that the resulting Kähler manifold admits a <span id="S1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">Kähler</span> (i.e., both holomorphic and symplectic) embedding of the <math id="S1.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> equal balls – meaning, in particular, that the Kähler metric is a standard flat metric on the image of the balls.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> we also describe exactly which disjoint unions of <math id="S1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math> (possibly different) balls admit Kähler-type embeddings into <math id="S1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
It then follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx67" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD3</a>]</cite> that any symplectic embedding of such a disjoint union of balls into <math id="S1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is, in fact, of Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">In the cases where <math id="S1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or a complex blow-up of <math id="S1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> at one point equipped with
any symplectic form compatible with the orientation, we describe exactly which disjoint unions of <math id="S1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq 7</annotation></semantics></math> (possibly different) balls admit Kähler-type embeddings into <math id="S1.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> that are holomorphic with respect to a complex structure isotopic to the “standard” one and compatible with the symplectic form.
It again follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx67" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD3</a>]</cite> that any symplectic embedding of such a disjoint union of balls into <math id="S1.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is, in fact, of Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is either <math id="S1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> or a K3 surface and the Kähler-type symplectic form <math id="S1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational (i.e., the real cohomology class <math id="S1.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is not a real multiple of a rational one), then Kähler-type embeddings of any disjoint union of balls into <math id="S1.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed. If <math id="S1.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is rational (i.e., <math id="S1.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a real multiple of a rational class),
then
there may be obstructions for the existence of Kähler-type embeddings of disjoint unions of balls into <math id="S1.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> that are independent of the symplectic volume. For instance, in the case <math id="S1.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{T}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{T}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> there are obstructions coming from the Seshadri constants (see <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>). In particular, this shows that there exist symplectic embeddings of disjoint unions of balls into <math id="S1.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{4},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{4},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> that are not Kähler-type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we show that tame embeddings of disjoint unions of equal balls, or equal polydisks, or equal parallelepipeds, into <math id="S1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
are unobstructed provided <math id="S1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> lies in a <math id="S1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant open dense set of Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> containing all the irrational forms (the set depends on the embedded domains).
By choosing possibly smaller <math id="S1.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant open dense sets of such <math id="S1.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> we make sure that the unobstructedness holds, in fact, for the tame embeddings into <math id="S1.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> which are holomorphic with respect to complex structures that are not only tamed by <math id="S1.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> but also “arbitrarily close” (in the sense of Hodge theory) to complex structures compatible with <math id="S1.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.
Kähler-type (not just tame) embeddings of disjoint unions of equal balls, or equal polydisks, or equal parallelepipeds, into <math id="S1.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed if <math id="S1.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>
is an irrational Kähler-type symplectic form lying in an appropriate dense <math id="S1.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit in the space of forms (the orbit depends on the embedded domains). Similar results concerning balls and parallelepipeds hold also for symplectic manifolds underlying Kähler K3 surfaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p21.1" class="ltx_p">We also discuss extensions of the results for K3 surfaces to a larger class of hyperkähler manifolds – see Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 The IHS-hyperkähler case ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth manifold of real dimension <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Diff</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the group of diffeomorphisms of <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and by <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> its identity component, and by <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{H}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{H}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the subgroup of <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Diff</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> formed by the diffeomorphisms acting trivially on the homology of <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For a symplectic form <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> denote by <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> the group of symplectomorphisms of <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, by <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
its identity component and by <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">symplectic Torelli group</span>, i.e. the group of symplectomorphisms of <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> acting trivially on homology. For a non-compact symplectic manifold <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> we denote by <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}^{c}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Symp</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}^{c}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
the group of compactly supported symplectomorphisms of <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and by <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> its identity component.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Kähler-type complex and symplectic structures</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic form and a complex structure on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. The form <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">tames</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(v,Iv)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(v,Iv)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for any non-zero <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in TM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in TM</annotation></semantics></math>, and is <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">compatible with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math></span> if it tames <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and is also <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant,
meaning that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega(\cdot,I\cdot)+i\omega(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(\cdot,I\cdot)+i\omega(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a Hermitian metric on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>. In the latter case the pair <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\omega,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\omega,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is called
a <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kähler structure</span> on <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A cohomology class in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is called <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kähler (with respect to a complex structure <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>, if it can be represented by a symplectic form on <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. The cohomology classes that are Kähler with respect to <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> form the <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kähler cone of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math></span> in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A symplectic/complex structure on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be of <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kähler type</span>, if it appears in <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">some</span> Kähler structure on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.1 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Any complex structure on <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is of Kähler type <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx105" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yau1</a>]</cite>. Whether this holds also for <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, is
a well-known open question – see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx63" title="" class="ltx_ref">LiWo</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tos</a>]</cite>, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx47" title="" class="ltx_ref">HuKP</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For a closed manifold <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{C}}M=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{C}}M=2</annotation></semantics></math>, with an even <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, any complex structure on <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is of Kähler type <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Buch</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lam</a>]</cite>. In particular,
any complex structure on <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">♯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is of Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The Kähler-type symplectic/complex structures on <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> are exactly the ones that can be mapped
by a diffeomorphism of <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> to a linear symplectic/complex structure – i.e., a symplectic/complex structure whose lift to <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>
has constant coefficients
(see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Prop. 6.1]</cite>).
There do exist complex structures on <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, for each <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, that are not of Kähler-type <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Som</a>, p.212]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">BSV</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth manifold underlying a complex K3 surface, then any
complex structure on <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is of Kähler-type <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Siu</a>]</cite> and
its first Chern class is zero (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bea2</a>]</cite>)<span id="footnote1a" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            In fact, all smooth manifolds <math id="footnote1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> underlying a complex K3 surface
are diffeomorphic. They all are compact and connected. All complex structures on such an <math id="footnote1a.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> define the
same orientation on <math id="footnote1a.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="footnote1a.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{+}(M)=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{+}(M)=3</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="footnote1a.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{-}(M)=19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{-}(M)=19</annotation></semantics></math> – see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bea2</a>]</cite>.
Further on, we will always equip a K3 surface with this
standard orientation. Alternatively, to fix the orientation,
one can use the fact that all complex K3 surfaces are deformation equivalent and, in
particular, oriented diffeomorphic – see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bea2</a>]</cite>.</span></span></span>.
It is well-known that any complex
structure on such an <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> appears in a hyperkähler
structure and so does any Kähler-type symplectic form
on <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> – see e.g.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV3</a>, Prop. 5.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">It is a well-known open question whether all symplectic forms on <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> and K3 surfaces (equipped with the standard smooth structures) are of Kähler type – see e.g.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Don</a>]</cite>. (There do exist exotic smooth structures on topological K3 surfaces that admit symplectic forms – see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">FiS</a>]</cite>, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ch</a>]</cite>; it follows from the classification of complex surfaces that these exotic smooth K3 surfaces do not admit complex structures and therefore the symplectic forms on them are not of Kähler type). Using the Kodaira-Spencer stability theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">KoS2</a>]</cite> and Torelli theorems for tori (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BaHPV</a>, Ch. I, Thm. 14.2]</cite>) and K3 surfaces (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">BurR</a>]</cite>, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bea2</a>, p.96]</cite>) one can show that the Kähler-type forms on these manifolds form an open subset of the space of all symplectic forms (with respect to the <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-topology – see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AmV1</a>]</cite>, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx84" title="" class="ltx_ref">StT</a>]</cite> for a more general result).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the space of all Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and by <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the space of all Kähler-type complex structures on <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We equip these spaces with the <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-topologies (the complex structures are viewed here as integrable almost-complex structures – i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-tensors).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The space <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is an open subspace of the space of all complex structures on <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>; both of these spaces are
locally <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-path connected – see <a href="#S13.SS2" title="13.2 Deformation families of complex structures ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S2.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in\mathit{SK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in\mathit{SK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)\subset\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)\subset\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the space of all (Kähler-type) complex structures on <math id="S2.SS1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S2.SS1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected component of <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> are <span id="S2.SS1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">compatible</span>, if some complex structure lying in <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>
– i.e., if the set <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}\cap{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}\cap{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is non-empty.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">With this terminology, every Kähler-type <math id="S2.SS1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with some connected component of <math id="S2.SS1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the space <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant under the natural action of <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Diff</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> (and hence of <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>) on the space of complex structures on <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, but the space
<math id="S2.SS1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is not invariant even under the <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p17" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Define the <span id="S2.SS1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Teichmüller space of Kähler-type complex structures</span> on <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}(M):=\mathit{CK}(M)/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}(M):=\mathit{CK}(M)/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p18.2" class="ltx_p">and if <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected component of <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M):={\mathcal{C}}_{0}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M):={\mathcal{C}}_{0}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p18.3" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected component of <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S2.SS1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the image of <math id="S2.SS1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> under the projection <math id="S2.SS1.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)\to{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)\to{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in\mathit{SK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in\mathit{SK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote the image of <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> under
the quotient projection <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)\to{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)\to{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}(M,\omega):=\frac{\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\cdot{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)}{\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)}\subset{\tt Teich}(M)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}(M,\omega):=\frac{\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\cdot{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)}{\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)}\subset{\tt Teich}(M).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p20.2" class="ltx_p">In other words, <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of points of <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> that can be represented by complex structures
on <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p21.1" class="ltx_p">Given a connected component <math id="S2.SS1.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, the image of <math id="S2.SS1.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)\cap{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)\cap{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> under
the quotient projection <math id="S2.SS1.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}\to{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}\to{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
will be denoted by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega):=\frac{\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\cdot\left({\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)\cap{\mathcal{C}}_{0}\right)}{\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)}=" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega):=\frac{\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\cdot\left({\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)\cap{\mathcal{C}}_{0}\right)}{\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="={\tt Teich}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">={\tt Teich}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the space <math id="S2.SS1.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> may not be Hausdorff – this is the case, for instance, when <math id="S2.SS1.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth manifold underlying a complex K3 surface, see <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Hodge decomposition</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is closed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A complex structure <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> defines a <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>-decomposition of any (complex-valued) differential <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-form <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,1,\ldots,2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,1,\ldots,2n</annotation></semantics></math> – we will denote it by <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\sum_{p+q=k}\eta_{I}^{p,q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\sum_{p+q=k}\eta_{I}^{p,q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is of Kähler type, then the <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>-decomposition on differential forms, defined by <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, induces a decomposition, called the Hodge decomposition<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            Although the proof of the
existence of the Hodge decomposition involves both the complex structure <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and a Kähler form on <math id="footnote2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, one can show (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voi</a>, Vol.
1, Prop. 6.11]</cite>) that, in fact, the Hodge
decomposition on the cohomology depends only on <math id="footnote2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span>, on the complex cohomology of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H^{k}(M;{\mathbb{C}})=\oplus_{p+q=k}H_{I}^{p,q}(M;{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(M;{\mathbb{C}})=\oplus_{p+q=k}H_{I}^{p,q}(M;{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a cohomology class <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math>, we will denote its <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>-component in the Hodge decomposition by <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{I}^{p,q}\in H_{I}^{p,q}(M;{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{I}^{p,q}\in H_{I}^{p,q}(M;{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We denote <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{p,q}_{I}(M;{\mathbb{R}}):=H^{p,q}_{I}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\cap H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{p,q}_{I}(M;{\mathbb{R}}):=H^{p,q}_{I}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\cap H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a real cohomology class <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in H^{2p}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in H^{2p}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> its <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,p)</annotation></semantics></math>-component is also real.
A differential form on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-form</span>, and a cohomology class of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-class</span> (with respect to <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>), if their <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>-component is their only non-zero component.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, then it is a <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-form with respect to <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, and, accordingly, its cohomology class is a <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-class.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If a cohomology class <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to a Kähler-type complex structure <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, then
for any Kähler-type complex structure <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> the class <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{I^{\prime}}^{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{I^{\prime}}^{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> – see <a href="#S13.SS2" title="13.2 Deformation families of complex structures ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Kähler-type and tame embeddings</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler-type symplectic form on <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The symplectic and complex structures on <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> are always assumed to be the standard ones and will be denoted, respectively, by <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1},\ldots,W_{k}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1},\ldots,W_{k}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> be compact domains with piecewise-smooth boundaries whose interiors contain the origin. Denote</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}\lambda W_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}\lambda W_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We say that <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\mathbb{W}}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\mathbb{W}}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth/symplectic/holomorphic embedding if it extends to such an embedding of the disjoint union of some open neighborhoods of <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>. We will denote by <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:W_{i}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:W_{i}\to M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, the restriction of such an extension to
a neighborhood of <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Clearly, if <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic embedding, then</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)=\langle[\omega]^{n},[M]\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)=\langle[\omega]^{n},[M]\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">(Recall that <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Vol</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}</annotation></semantics></math> stands for the symplectic volume and <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> stands for the natural pairing between cohomology and homology classes.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\mathbb{W}}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}\to(M^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\mathbb{W}}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}\to(M^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
be an embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Given a Kähler-type complex structure <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that the embedding <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is of <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-Kähler type</span>, if it is symplectic with respect to <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and holomorphic with respect to some <span id="S2.SS3.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(not a priori fixed)</span> complex structure <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and isotopic to <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J]=[I]\in{\tt Teich}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J]=[I]\in{\tt Teich}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. (Such a <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is tautologically of Kähler-type).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We say that the embedding <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is of <span id="S2.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kähler type</span>, if it is
symplectic with respect to <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and holomorphic with respect to some <span id="S2.SS3.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(not a priori fixed)</span> complex structure compatible with <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> –
or, in other words, if <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is of <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type for some <span id="S2.SS3.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(not a priori fixed, and possibly non-unique)</span> <math id="S2.SS3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Given a <span id="S2.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fixed Kähler structure <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\omega,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\omega,I)</annotation></semantics></math></span> on <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M^{2n},\omega,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M^{2n},\omega,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S2.SS3.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kähler</span> embedding if it is Kähler-isometric – i.e. both symplectic with respect to <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and holomorphic with respect to <math id="S2.SS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We say that the embedding <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.SS3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">tame</span> if it is symplectic with respect to <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and holomorphic with respect to some <span id="S2.SS3.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(not a priori fixed)</span> Kähler-type complex structure <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> tamed by <math id="S2.SS3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming that <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is closed
and <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that the embedding <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-tame</span> if it is symplectic with respect to <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and holomorphic with respect to some <span id="S2.SS3.p12.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(not a priori fixed)</span> Kähler-type complex structure <math id="S2.SS3.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> so that</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is tamed by <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the class
<math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]_{I}^{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]_{I}^{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\left\langle\left([\omega]_{I}^{2,0}+[\omega]_{I}^{0,2}\right)^{n},[M]\right\rangle\right|&lt;\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\left\langle\left([\omega]_{I}^{2,0}+[\omega]_{I}^{0,2}\right)^{n},[M]\right\rangle\right|&lt;\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">With the terminology as in <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Kähler-type and tame embeddings ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, for an embedding into a (closed) symplectic manifold
<math id="S2.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
we have the following equivalences and hierarchy of properties:</p>
<p id="S2.SS3.p14.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Kähler-type <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Longleftrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Longleftrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type for some (possibly non-unique!) <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<p id="S2.SS3.p14.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">tame <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Longleftrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Longleftrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame for some <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<p id="S2.SS3.p14.4" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Kähler-type <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Longrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Longrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame for every <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Longrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Longrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> tame <math id="S2.SS3.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Longrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Longrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> symplectic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p15.1" class="ltx_p">In general, there do exist symplectic embeddings of balls that are not of Kähler-type (see <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>), but we do not know whether there exist
symplectic embeddings of balls or any other domains into any <math id="S2.SS3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, equipped with a Kähler-type symplectic form, that are not tame, or tame embeddings that are not of Kähler-type – see
<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>
and
<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.4 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p17.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S2.SS3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> any finite disjoint union of sufficiently small balls (and hence of arbitrary sufficiently small domains in <math id="S2.SS3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{R}}^{2n},\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{R}}^{2n},\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>) admits an <math id="S2.SS3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding into <math id="S2.SS3.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> – this follows e.g. from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>, Prop. 5.5.A]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.5</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p19.1" class="ltx_p">If a Kähler manifold <math id="S2.SS3.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega,I)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a Kähler embedding of a domain in <math id="S2.SS3.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, its restriction to the image of the embedding is flat. This provides a strong obstruction to the existence of Kähler embeddings (if one fixes a Kähler structure on the manifold).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p20" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.6 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p21.1" class="ltx_p">The existence of <math id="S2.SS3.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embeddings has been previously studied – in a different guise – in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ec</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fl</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx64" title="" class="ltx_ref">LuWa</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tr</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx102" title="" class="ltx_ref">WN1</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx103" title="" class="ltx_ref">WN2</a>]</cite> in the following setup.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega,I)</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed Kähler manifold (in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ec</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fl</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tr</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx102" title="" class="ltx_ref">WN1</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx103" title="" class="ltx_ref">WN2</a>]</cite> it is assumed that the complex manifold <math id="S2.SS3.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and the cohomology class <math id="S2.SS3.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the first Chern class of an ample line bundle on <math id="S2.SS3.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the following question: Given a disjoint union <math id="S2.SS3.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of domains in <math id="S2.SS3.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
does there exist a Kähler form <math id="S2.SS3.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS3.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, isotopic to <math id="S2.SS3.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S2.SS3.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\eta,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\eta,I)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a Kähler embedding of <math id="S2.SS3.p22.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p23.1" class="ltx_p">One easily sees that the answer to the latter question is positive if and only if <math id="S2.SS3.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> admits an <math id="S2.SS3.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding of <math id="S2.SS3.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p24.1" class="ltx_p">Let us introduce additional terminology related to Kähler-type
and tame
embeddings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p25.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}\subset\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}\subset\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-empty set. If a Kähler-type, or tame,
embedding <math id="S2.SS3.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic with respect to some complex structure belonging to <math id="S2.SS3.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that <math id="S2.SS3.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.SS3.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">favors <math id="S2.SS3.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p26.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, if <math id="S2.SS3.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}={\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}={\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected component <math id="S2.SS3.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS3.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that such an <math id="S2.SS3.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.SS3.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">favors <math id="S2.SS3.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, or that <math id="S2.SS3.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S2.SS3.p26.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">favorite connected component of <math id="S2.SS3.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p27.1" class="ltx_p">With this terminology,
tautologically,</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p28" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, every <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding into <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> favors the <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every Kähler-type
embedding into <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> favors a connected component of <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, and consequently a connected component of <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p29.1" class="ltx_p">Note that, in principle, a Kähler-type embedding may be of <math id="S2.SS3.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type for several <math id="S2.SS3.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and may favor more than one connected component of <math id="S2.SS3.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> or of <math id="S2.SS3.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p30.1" class="ltx_p">The group <math id="S2.SS3.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> acts naturally on the space of Kähler-type, or tame, or <math id="S2.SS3.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame,
embeddings <math id="S2.SS3.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, so do the groups <math id="S2.SS3.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p31.1" class="ltx_p">By a standard result concerning extensions of symplectic isotopies (proved as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDS</a>, Thms. 3.3.1, 3.3.2]</cite>),
if the domains <math id="S2.SS3.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1},\ldots,W_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1},\ldots,W_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are starshaped, then
any two symplectic embeddings <math id="S2.SS3.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
can be connected by a (smooth) path of symplectic embeddings <math id="S2.SS3.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.SS3.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
lie in the same <math id="S2.SS3.p31.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit – i.e., there exists <math id="S2.SS3.p31.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS3.p31.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\circ f=f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\circ f=f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p32.1" class="ltx_p">Also note, that if <math id="S2.SS3.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\circ f=f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\circ f=f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S2.SS3.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Diff</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure <math id="S2.SS3.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS3.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS3.p32.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic with respect to the complex structure <math id="S2.SS3.p32.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{*}I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p33.1" class="ltx_p">These simple observations easily yield the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p34" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p34.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.7 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p35.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that the domains <math id="S2.SS3.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1},\ldots,W_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1},\ldots,W_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are starshaped.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p36.1" class="ltx_p">Then any two embeddings <math id="S2.SS3.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
lie in the same connected component of the space of Kähler-type
embeddings <math id="S2.SS3.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if they lie in the
same orbit of the <math id="S2.SS3.p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-action.
In this case <math id="S2.SS3.p36.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are holomorphic with respect to complex structures compatible
with <math id="S2.SS3.p36.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> that lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S2.SS3.p36.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S2.SS3.p36.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular,
such <math id="S2.SS3.p36.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> favor a common connected component of <math id="S2.SS3.p36.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
and, consequently, a common connected component of <math id="S2.SS3.p36.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p37.1" class="ltx_p">If Kähler-type
embeddings <math id="S2.SS3.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> lie in the same <math id="S2.SS3.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit, then they are holomorphic with respect to complex structures compatible
with <math id="S2.SS3.p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> that lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S2.SS3.p37.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S2.SS3.p37.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, there exists <math id="S2.SS3.p37.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt Teich}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt Teich}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
such that both <math id="S2.SS3.p37.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p37.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are of <math id="S2.SS3.p37.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type, and <math id="S2.SS3.p37.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> favor a common connected component of <math id="S2.SS3.p37.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S2.SS3.p37.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p38.1" class="ltx_p">Let us define a quantity that will be important for the study of the portion of the volume of a target closed symplectic manifold that can be filled out by Kähler-type and tame embeddings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p39" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p39.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p39.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.8 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p40.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S2.SS3.p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed manifold equipped with a Kähler-type symplectic form <math id="S2.SS3.p40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p41.1" class="ltx_p">Define the number <math id="S2.SS3.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}}):=\sup_{\lambda}\frac{{\rm{Vol}\,}(\lambda{\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})}{{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>λ</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}}):=\sup_{\lambda}\frac{{\rm{Vol}\,}(\lambda{\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})}{{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p41.2" class="ltx_p">where the supremum is taken over all <math id="S2.SS3.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS3.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a Kähler-type embedding into <math id="S2.SS3.p41.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p42" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p42.1" class="ltx_p">Given
<math id="S2.SS3.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, define the number <math id="S2.SS3.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}}):=\sup_{\lambda}\frac{{\rm{Vol}\,}(\lambda{\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})}{{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mi>λ</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}}):=\sup_{\lambda}\frac{{\rm{Vol}\,}(\lambda{\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})}{{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p42.2" class="ltx_p">where the supremum is taken over all <math id="S2.SS3.p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS3.p42.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> admits an <math id="S2.SS3.p42.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame embedding into <math id="S2.SS3.p42.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
(In view of <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Kähler-type and tame embeddings ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, the set of such <math id="S2.SS3.p42.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is always non-empty.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p43" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p43.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS3.p43.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.9</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p44" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p44.1" class="ltx_p">1. Clearly,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})\leq\nu_{T,\varepsilon_{1}}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})\leq\nu_{T,\varepsilon_{2}}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})\leq\nu_{T,\varepsilon_{1}}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})\leq\nu_{T,\varepsilon_{2}}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p44.2" class="ltx_p">for any <math id="S2.SS3.p44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\varepsilon_{1}\leq\varepsilon_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\varepsilon_{1}\leq\varepsilon_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p45" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p45.1" class="ltx_p">2. The equality <math id="S2.SS3.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively <math id="S2.SS3.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, is equivalent
to the claim that the Kähler-type, respectively <math id="S2.SS3.p45.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame, embeddings of <math id="S2.SS3.p45.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.SS3.p45.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S2.SS3.p45.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Main results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we state the main results of the paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we denote by <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math> the closed Euclidean <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional ball of radius <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> centered at the origin.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds
and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">dim</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}=2n</annotation></semantics></math>, is a closed connected manifold equipped with a Kähler-type symplectic form <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{r}}=(r_{1},\ldots,r_{k})\in({\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{r}}=(r_{1},\ldots,r_{k})\in({\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k}\subset M^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k}\subset M^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the set of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples of pairwise distinct points of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
and each complex structure <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
denote the complex blow-up of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> we will use the same notation for the following objects (suppressing the dependence on <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">- The lift of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> will be denoted by <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">- <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi:{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi:{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> will denote the natural projection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">- The cohomology classes Poincaré-dual to the fundamental homology classes of the exceptional divisors will be denoted by <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\ldots,e_{k}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\ldots,e_{k}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> the groups <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> can be canonically identified (preserving the numbering of the classes <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\ldots,e_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\ldots,e_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>) and we will use these identifications without further mention.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Define the set
<math id="S3.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}})\subset\mathit{CK}(M)\times{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}})\subset\mathit{CK}(M)\times{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}}):=\Big\{\ (I,{\mathbbm{x}})\ \big|\ I\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\cdot{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega),{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}\ \textit{and}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}}):=\Big\{\ (I,{\mathbbm{x}})\ \big|\ I\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\cdot{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega),{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}\ \textit{and}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext='\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})\ \textit{is K\"{a}hler with respect to}\ {\widetilde{I}}\ \Big\},' display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">is Kähler with respect to</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})\ \textit{is K\"{a}hler with respect to}\ {\widetilde{I}}\ \Big\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{pr}\,}:{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}})\to\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">pr</mtext><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{pr}\,}:{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}})\to\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> be the natural projection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Define the set <math id="S3.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}({\bar{r}})\subset{\tt Teich}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝙺</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}({\bar{r}})\subset{\tt Teich}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}({\bar{r}}):={\textit{pr}\,}({\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}}))/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)=" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝙺</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">pr</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}({\bar{r}}):={\textit{pr}\,}({\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}}))/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="=\Big\{\ \iota\in{\tt Teich}(M,\omega)\ \big|\ \exists I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega),[I]=\iota,\ \textit{and}\ {\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}\ \textit{s.t.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">s.t.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\Big\{\ \iota\in{\tt Teich}(M,\omega)\ \big|\ \exists I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega),[I]=\iota,\ \textit{and}\ {\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}\ \textit{s.t.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext='\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})\ \textit{is K\"{a}hler with respect to}\ {\widetilde{I}}\ \Big\}.' display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">is Kähler with respect to</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})\ \textit{is K\"{a}hler with respect to}\ {\widetilde{I}}\ \Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected component of <math id="S3.SS1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Define the set
<math id="S3.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})\subset{\mathcal{C}}_{0}\times{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})\subset{\mathcal{C}}_{0}\times{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}}):=({\mathcal{C}}_{0}\times{\hat{M}}^{k})\cap{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}})=" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}}):=({\mathcal{C}}_{0}\times{\hat{M}}^{k})\cap{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}})=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="=\Big\{\ (I,{\mathbbm{x}})\ \big|\ I\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\cdot\big({\mathcal{C}}_{0}\cap{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)\big),{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}\ \textit{and}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\Big\{\ (I,{\mathbbm{x}})\ \big|\ I\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\cdot\big({\mathcal{C}}_{0}\cap{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)\big),{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}\ \textit{and}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext='\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})\ \textit{is K\"{a}hler with respect to}\ {\widetilde{I}}\ \Big\}.' display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">is Kähler with respect to</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})\ \textit{is K\"{a}hler with respect to}\ {\widetilde{I}}\ \Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p14.2" class="ltx_p">Define the set <math id="S3.SS1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})\subset{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})\subset{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}}):={\tt{K}}({\bar{r}})\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)={\textit{pr}\,}\left({\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})\right)/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)=" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝙺</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">pr</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}}):={\tt{K}}({\bar{r}})\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)={\textit{pr}\,}\left({\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})\right)/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="=\Big\{\ \iota\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\ \big|\ \exists I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}\cap{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega),[I]=\iota,\ \textit{and}\ {\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}\ \textit{s.t.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">s.t.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\Big\{\ \iota\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\ \big|\ \exists I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}\cap{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega),[I]=\iota,\ \textit{and}\ {\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}\ \textit{s.t.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext='\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})\ \textit{is K\"{a}hler with respect to}\ {\widetilde{I}}\ \Big\}.' display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>π</mi><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">is Kähler with respect to</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})\ \textit{is K\"{a}hler with respect to}\ {\widetilde{I}}\ \Big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">As we will see in <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6 Connectedness of 𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜_𝒞_0 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>, the fibers of <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{pr}\,}:{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}})\to\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">pr</mtext><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{pr}\,}:{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}})\to\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> are connected and therefore <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝙺</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively, <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math>)
is connected if and only if so is <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively, <math id="S3.SS1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed manifold equipped with a Kähler-type symplectic form <math id="S3.SS1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">(I)
The set <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> admits a Kähler-type embedding into <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
for some <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and some <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the cohomology class <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S3.SS1.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely, assume <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex structure on <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold. If <math id="S3.SS1.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{n}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{n}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (with the product complex structure), we also allow
<math id="S3.SS1.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=({\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{n}\times\textrm{pt})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=({\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{n}\times\textrm{pt})</annotation></semantics></math> (which is not smooth).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following conditions are equivalent:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M\setminus\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M\setminus\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the cohomology class <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists an <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
which is holomorphic with respect to the complex structure <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}=Id</annotation></semantics></math>, is an isotopy such that <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(\Sigma)=\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(\Sigma)=\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p22" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p22.1" class="ltx_p">(II)
Let <math id="S3.SS1.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected component of <math id="S3.SS1.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume that
the set <math id="S3.SS1.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math> (or, equivalently, the set <math id="S3.SS1.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math>)
is connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Then any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> favoring <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
lie in the same <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit. In particular, there exists <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> such that
both embeddings are of <math id="S3.SS1.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p24.1" class="ltx_p">If, in addition, <math id="S3.SS1.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on the set of connected components of <math id="S3.SS1.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S3.SS1.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, then
any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS1.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
lie in the same <math id="S3.SS1.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p25.1" class="ltx_p">For the proof of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> see Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p26" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.3</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p27" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p27.1" class="ltx_p">1. For an analogue of part (I) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> for tame embeddings
see <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p28" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p28.1" class="ltx_p">2. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fl</a>, Remark 5.2]</cite> states that the methods of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fl</a>]</cite> can be used to prove a result that, in view of
<a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Kähler-type and tame embeddings ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>,
is equivalent to Part (I) <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> in the case where
<math id="S3.SS1.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with a complex structure <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the complex manifold <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is projective and
the cohomology class <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the first Chern class of an ample line bundle on <math id="S3.SS1.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p29.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that the symplectic blow-up construction (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDS</a>, Sec. 7]</cite>, cf. Section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> below) associates to a complex blow-up of a symplectic manifold at a point (with respect to, say, an almost complex structure compatible with the symplectic form and integrable near the point) a class of symplectic forms on the blow-up. We will call such a symplectic form on the blow-up a <span id="S3.SS1.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">blow-up symplectic form</span>. A <span id="S3.SS1.p29.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">rational (4-dimensional) symplectic manifold</span> is either <math id="S3.SS1.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with a standard Fubini-Study symplectic form, or <math id="S3.SS1.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with a product symplectic form, or their blow-up at several points equipped with a blow-up
symplectic form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p30.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S3.SS1.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ruled (4-dimensional) symplectic manifold</span> is a closed symplectic 4-manifold which is the total space of an <math id="S3.SS1.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-fiber bundle over a closed connected orientable 2-dimensional surface, so that all the fibers are symplectic submanifolds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx58" title="" class="ltx_ref">LalM</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p31.1" class="ltx_p">For rational or ruled closed symplectic manifolds of real dimension <math id="S3.SS1.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> we have a stronger connectedness result, which follows directly
from similar connectedness results for <span id="S3.SS1.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">symplectic</span> (not necessarily Kähler-type) embeddings of balls
that were proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx67" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD3</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLW</a>]</cite>
(for previous partial results see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD1</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx66" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD2</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lal</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir1</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p32" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.4 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p33.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a rational or ruled closed symplectic manifold, <math id="S3.SS1.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=4</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS1.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
be either of the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p34" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p34.1" class="ltx_p">(1) the empty set;</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p35" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p35.1" class="ltx_p">(2) a finite union of closed compact symplectic submanifolds (without boundary) of real dimension 2
whose pairwise intersections (if they exist) are transverse and <math id="S3.SS1.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>–orthogonal;</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p36" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p36.1" class="ltx_p">(3) a Lagrangian submanifold which is diffeomorphic to either <math id="S3.SS1.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS1.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p37.1" class="ltx_p">Then for any <math id="S3.SS1.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S3.SS1.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
the following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p38" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p38.1" class="ltx_p">(I)
Any two Kähler-type, respectively tame, embeddings
<math id="S3.SS1.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S3.SS1.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-action.
In particular, (for fixed <math id="S3.SS1.p38.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>) the space of all such Kähler-type, respectively tame, embeddings is connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p39" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p39.1" class="ltx_p">(II) If there exists an <math id="S3.SS1.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type, respectively tame, embedding <math id="S3.SS1.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>,
then any symplectic embedding <math id="S3.SS1.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is of <math id="S3.SS1.p39.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type, respectively tame.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p40" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p40.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p40.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p41.1" class="ltx_p">Any two symplectic embeddings <math id="S3.SS1.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S3.SS1.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-action:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p42" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p42.1" class="ltx_p">- In case (1) this was proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx67" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD3</a>, Cor. 1.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p43" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p43.1" class="ltx_p">- In case (2), where <math id="S3.SS1.p43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is just a symplectic submanifold, this was essentially proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx67" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD3</a>, Cor. 1.5]</cite> – see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLW</a>, Prop. 2.1]</cite> where more details of the proof are spelled out in the case where the symplectic submanifold <math id="S3.SS1.p43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a sphere; in fact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx69" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDO</a>, Cor. 1.2.13]</cite>, used in the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLW</a>, Prop. 2.1]</cite>, yields the same claim for any <math id="S3.SS1.p43.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> as in (2).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p44" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p44.1" class="ltx_p">- In case (3), this is proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLW</a>, Thm. 1.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p45.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.SS1.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type, respectively tame, embeddings into <math id="S3.SS1.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are mapped by
the <math id="S3.SS1.p45.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-action
into <math id="S3.SS1.p45.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type, respectively tame, embeddings, we get the
results of both parts of the theorem.
<span id="S3.SS1.p45.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p46.1" class="ltx_p">Now let us discuss applications of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>
and <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> to particular symplectic manifolds.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>The case of projective spaces and their products</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math>, or by <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Fubini-Study form on <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> normalized so that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}}\omega_{FS}=\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}}\omega_{FS}=\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> be the standard complex structure on <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also set <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{0}:=\textrm{pt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{0}:=\textrm{pt}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.5 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the manifold <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:={\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\ldots\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:={\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\ldots\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1},\ldots,n_{m}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1},\ldots,n_{m}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}+\ldots+n_{m}=:n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}+\ldots+n_{m}=:n</annotation></semantics></math>, endowed with the symplectic form <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\mathbbm{c}}:=c_{1}\omega_{FS,n_{1}}\oplus\ldots\oplus c_{m}\omega_{FS,n_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>𝕔</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\mathbbm{c}}:=c_{1}\omega_{FS,n_{1}}\oplus\ldots\oplus c_{m}\omega_{FS,n_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{c}}:=(c_{1},\ldots,c_{m})\in({\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0})^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕔</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{c}}:=(c_{1},\ldots,c_{m})\in({\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0})^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> be the complex
structure on <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> which is the product of the standard complex structures on the factors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1},\ldots,l_{m}\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1},\ldots,l_{m}\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[l_{1}:\ldots:l_{m}]=[c_{1}:\ldots:c_{m}]\in{\mathbb{R}}P^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[l_{1}:\ldots:l_{m}]=[c_{1}:\ldots:c_{m}]\in{\mathbb{R}}P^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k:=\frac{(n_{1}+\ldots+n_{m})!}{n_{1}!\cdot\ldots\cdot n_{m}!}l_{1}^{n_{1}}\cdot\ldots\cdot l_{m}^{n_{m}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">!</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k:=\frac{(n_{1}+\ldots+n_{m})!}{n_{1}!\cdot\ldots\cdot n_{m}!}l_{1}^{n_{1}}\cdot\ldots\cdot l_{m}^{n_{m}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold. If <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we also allow
<math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\textrm{pt})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\textrm{pt})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Then Kähler-type embeddings of disjoint unions of <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> equal balls into <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mathbbm{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>𝕔</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mathbbm{c}})</annotation></semantics></math>
are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely, if <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, then for each proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding of <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>
into <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> that is compatible with <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>
and isotopic to <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> by an isotopy preserving <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (as a set).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For the proof of
<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> see Section <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (<a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}=\ldots=n_{m}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}=\ldots=n_{m}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}=\ldots=l_{m}=l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}=\ldots=l_{m}=l</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}=\ldots=c_{m}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}=\ldots=c_{m}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>,
<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> yields the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.6 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:=({\mathbb{C}}P^{1})^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:=({\mathbb{C}}P^{1})^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider a symplectic form <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> which is the product of equal Fubini-Study forms on the <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-factors of <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> be the complex structure on <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> which is the product of the standard complex structures on the <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-factors of <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Then for any <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, Kähler-type embeddings of disjoint unions of <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m!l^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m!l^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> equal balls into <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely, if <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m!l^{m}{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m!l^{m}{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists a Kähler-type embedding of <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{m!l^{m}}B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{m!l^{m}}B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>
into <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> that is compatible with <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>
and isotopic to <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S3.SS2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Now let us consider the case of a single complex projective space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.7 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p16.1" class="ltx_p">A. For each <math id="S3.SS2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
Kähler-type embeddings of disjoint unions of <math id="S3.SS2.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> equal balls into <math id="S3.SS2.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p17.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely,
if <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{n}{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{n}{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>, then for any complex structure <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math> (and, in particular, for the standard complex structure <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math>) and for each proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},J)</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding of <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{l^{n}}B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{l^{n}}B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>
into <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> that is compatible with <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math>
and isotopic to <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> by an isotopy preserving <math id="S3.SS2.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (as a set).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p18.1" class="ltx_p">B. The group <math id="S3.SS2.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on the set of connected components of <math id="S3.SS2.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with
<math id="S3.SS2.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p19" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p19.1" class="ltx_p">C.
For any <math id="S3.SS2.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, any two Kähler-type embeddings
<math id="S3.SS2.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>
(if they exist!) lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S3.SS2.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>-action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p20.1" class="ltx_p">They lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S3.SS2.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>-action
if and only if they favor a common connected component of <math id="S3.SS2.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. In the latter case there exists <math id="S3.SS2.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> such that
both embeddings are of <math id="S3.SS2.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p21.1" class="ltx_p">Part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>
follows from
<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.
For the proof of parts B and C of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> see Section <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (<a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p22" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.8</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Let us say that a Kähler-type complex structure <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on a closed manifold <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
is <span id="S3.SS2.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">rigid</span>, if the connected component of <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>
(or, in other words, if any Kähler-type complex structure on <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> by a smooth deformation is, in fact, isotopic to <math id="S3.SS2.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p24.1" class="ltx_p">As we will see in
<a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, any
Kähler-type complex structure on <math id="S3.SS2.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is rigid. More
examples of rigid complex structures include
so-called <math id="S3.SS2.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>-manifolds
(i.e., Calabi-Yau manifolds with
<math id="S3.SS2.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{n-1,1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{n-1,1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">CDP</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p25.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of part C of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> allows to generalize the last claim of part C as follows:
Assume <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed manifold such that <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with a rigid complex structure <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected component
of <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. Then two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> favoring <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S3.SS2.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p26.1" class="ltx_p">Applying part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> with
<math id="S3.SS2.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS2.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}=J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}=J</annotation></semantics></math> being fixed, and with <math id="S3.SS2.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math> being varied by an isotopy, we get the following immediate corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p27" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.9 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p28.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p29.1" class="ltx_p">Then for any <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{n}{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{n}{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a Kähler form <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},I_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},I_{st})</annotation></semantics></math> isotopic to <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math> and such that the Kähler manifold <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},I_{st},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},I_{st},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a Kähler (i.e., both holomorphic and symplectic) embedding of <math id="S3.SS2.p29.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{l^{n}}B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{l^{n}}B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math> with the standard flat Kähler metric on it.
<span id="S3.SS2.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p30.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>
mean that by changing either <math id="S3.SS2.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math> by an isotopy one can get a Kähler metric on <math id="S3.SS2.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> which is the standard flat Kähler metric on <math id="S3.SS2.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint balls in <math id="S3.SS2.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> that fill out an arbitrarily large portion of the volume of the manifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p31" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.10 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p32.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS2.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> was proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ec</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p33.1" class="ltx_p">Applying <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> with <math id="S3.SS2.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> and with the hyperplane <math id="S3.SS2.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma={\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},I_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma={\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},I_{st})</annotation></semantics></math>, we get the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p34" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p34.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.11 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p35.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S3.SS2.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S3.SS2.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS2.p35.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{n}{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(1),\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{n}{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(1),\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a Kähler-type
embedding of <math id="S3.SS2.p35.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{l^{n}}B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{l^{n}}B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>
into <math id="S3.SS2.p35.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B^{2n}(1),\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B^{2n}(1),\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S3.SS2.p35.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> isotopic to <math id="S3.SS2.p35.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and compatible with <math id="S3.SS2.p35.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S3.SS2.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p36.1" class="ltx_p">Now let us focus on the case of <math id="S3.SS2.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p37" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p37.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p37.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.12 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p38.1" class="ltx_p">There is only one connected component of <math id="S3.SS2.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S3.SS2.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math> – it is the connected component
of <math id="S3.SS2.p38.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S3.SS2.p38.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math>. Any two complex structures in that connected component are isotopic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p39.1" class="ltx_p">For the proof of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> see Section <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (<a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).
We do not know whether <math id="S3.SS2.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> has more than one connected component – see <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p40.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> yields the following immediate corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p41" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p41.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p41.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.13 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p42" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p42.1" class="ltx_p">Any Kähler-type embedding (of any disjoint union of domains) into <math id="S3.SS2.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> is of <math id="S3.SS2.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I_{st}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I_{st}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type: it is symplectic with respect to <math id="S3.SS2.p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math> and holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S3.SS2.p42.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> isotopic to <math id="S3.SS2.p42.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> and compatible with <math id="S3.SS2.p42.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S3.SS2.p42.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p43.1" class="ltx_p">Recall the following theorem of McDuff-Polterovich <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p44" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p44.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p44.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.14 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p45.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p46" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p46.1" class="ltx_p">A. Assume that <math id="S3.SS2.p46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}\geq r_{2}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}\geq r_{2}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p47.1" class="ltx_p">Then
the following conditions are equivalent:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p48" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The radii <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the following inequalities (listed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>, Cor. 1.3G]</cite>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">(v) <math id="S3.I2.i2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{4}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{4}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> (volume inequality),</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(c1) <math id="S3.I2.i2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}^{2}+r_{2}^{2}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}^{2}+r_{2}^{2}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.I2.i2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">(c2) <math id="S3.I2.i2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}^{2}+\ldots+r_{5}^{2}&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}^{2}+\ldots+r_{5}^{2}&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.I2.i2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">(c3) <math id="S3.I2.i2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r_{1}^{2}+\sum_{i=2}^{7}r_{i}^{2}&lt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>7</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r_{1}^{2}+\sum_{i=2}^{7}r_{i}^{2}&lt;3</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.I2.i2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">(c4) <math id="S3.I2.i2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r_{1}^{2}+2r_{2}^{2}+2r_{3}^{2}+r_{4}^{2}+\ldots+r_{8}^{2}&lt;4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>8</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r_{1}^{2}+2r_{2}^{2}+2r_{3}^{2}+r_{4}^{2}+\ldots+r_{8}^{2}&lt;4</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.I2.i2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">(c5) <math id="S3.I2.i2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\sum_{i=1}^{6}r_{i}^{2}+r_{7}^{2}+r_{8}^{2}&lt;5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>6</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>8</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\sum_{i=1}^{6}r_{i}^{2}+r_{7}^{2}+r_{8}^{2}&lt;5</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.I2.i2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">(c6) <math id="S3.I2.i2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3r_{1}^{2}+2\sum_{i=2}^{8}r_{i}^{2}&lt;6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>8</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3r_{1}^{2}+2\sum_{i=2}^{8}r_{i}^{2}&lt;6</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S3.I2.i2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p49" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p49.1" class="ltx_p">B. Symplectic embeddings of a disjoint union of <math id="S3.SS2.p49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=l^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=l^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, equal balls into <math id="S3.SS2.p49.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed:
if <math id="S3.SS2.p49.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{4}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{4}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S3.SS2.p49.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p50" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p50.1" class="ltx_p">Here is an extension of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> to Kähler-type embeddings of balls into <math id="S3.SS2.p50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p51" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p51.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p51.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.15 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p52" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p52.1" class="ltx_p">A. Assume that <math id="S3.SS2.p52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p52.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}\geq r_{2}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}\geq r_{2}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p53" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p53.1" class="ltx_p">Then for any proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold <math id="S3.SS2.p53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})</annotation></semantics></math>,
any symplectic embedding <math id="S3.SS2.p53.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> is, in fact, of <math id="S3.SS2.p53.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I_{st}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I_{st}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type:
it is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S3.SS2.p53.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> that is compatible with <math id="S3.SS2.p53.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math> and isotopic
to <math id="S3.SS2.p53.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> by an isotopy fixing <math id="S3.SS2.p53.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (as a set).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p54" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p54.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, by part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, such a Kähler-type embedding <math id="S3.SS2.p54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>
exists if and only if the radii <math id="S3.SS2.p54.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the inequalities (v), (c1)-(c6) listed in part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p55" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p55.1" class="ltx_p">B. For any proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold <math id="S3.SS2.p55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S3.SS2.p55.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=l^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=l^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p55.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
any symplectic embedding <math id="S3.SS2.p55.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> is, in fact, of <math id="S3.SS2.p55.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I_{st}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I_{st}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type:
it is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S3.SS2.p55.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> that is compatible with <math id="S3.SS2.p55.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math>
and isotopic to <math id="S3.SS2.p55.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> by an isotopy preserving <math id="S3.SS2.p55.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (as a set).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p56" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p56.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, by part B of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, such Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed: they exist if and only if <math id="S3.SS2.p56.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{4}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{4}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p57" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p57.1" class="ltx_p">C. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>
be either of the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p58" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p58.1" class="ltx_p">(1) the empty set;</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p59" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p59.1" class="ltx_p">(2) a finite union of closed compact symplectic submanifolds (without boundary) of real dimension 2
whose pairwise intersections (if they exist) are transverse and <math id="S3.SS2.p59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>–orthogonal;</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p60" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p60.1" class="ltx_p">(3) a Lagrangian submanifold which is diffeomorphic to either <math id="S3.SS2.p60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.p60.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p61" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p61.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S3.SS2.p61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p61.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p61.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> (if they exist!) lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S3.SS2.p61.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>-action, meaning that
the space of Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p61.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p62" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p62.1" class="ltx_p">Part B of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> follows from
part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>
and from part (II) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
Part C of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> follows directly from part (I) of
<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
For the proof of part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> see Section <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (<a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p63" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p63.1" class="ltx_p">Applying <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> with the hyperplane <math id="S3.SS2.p63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\subset{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\subset{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and
recalling that <math id="S3.SS2.p63.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus{\mathbb{C}}P^{1},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus{\mathbb{C}}P^{1},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectomorphic to <math id="S3.SS2.p63.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>,
we get the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p64" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p64.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p64.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.16 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p65" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p65.1" class="ltx_p">A. Assume that <math id="S3.SS2.p65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p65.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}\geq r_{2}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}\geq r_{2}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p66" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p66.1" class="ltx_p">Then
any symplectic embedding <math id="S3.SS2.p66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>
is, in fact, of Kähler-type: it is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S3.SS2.p66.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> that is compatible with <math id="S3.SS2.p66.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and isotopic
to <math id="S3.SS2.p66.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p67" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p67.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, by part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, such a Kähler-type embedding <math id="S3.SS2.p67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>
exists if and only if the radii <math id="S3.SS2.p67.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the inequalities (v), (c1)-(c6) listed in part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p68" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p68.1" class="ltx_p">B. For any <math id="S3.SS2.p68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=l^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=l^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p68.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
any symplectic embedding <math id="S3.SS2.p68.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\to(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\to(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is, in fact, of Kähler type:
it is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S3.SS2.p68.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{4}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{4}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> that is compatible with <math id="S3.SS2.p68.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
and isotopic to <math id="S3.SS2.p68.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p69" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p69.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, by part B of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, such Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\to(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\to(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed: they exist if and only if <math id="S3.SS2.p69.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{4}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{4}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p70" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p70.1" class="ltx_p">C.
For any <math id="S3.SS2.p70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p70.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p70.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> (if they exist!) lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S3.SS2.p70.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>-action, meaning that
the space of Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS2.p70.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.
<span id="S3.SS2.p70.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p71" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p71.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p71.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.17 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p72" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p72.1" class="ltx_p">1.
The description of the Kähler cone of the blow-up of <math id="S3.SS2.p72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S3.SS2.p72.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math> (generic) points is a difficult open question related, in particular, to the Nagata conjecture
(which is equivalent to the claim that a certain cohomology class of the blow-up lies in the boundary of the Kähler cone,
see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir3</a>]</cite>).
In particular, if there is a counterexample to the Nagata conjecture for <math id="S3.SS2.p72.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math>, it
would yield that the Kähler-type embeddings of <math id="S3.SS2.p72.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> equal balls to <math id="S3.SS2.p72.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are not unobstructed – see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>, Thm. 1.4.B]</cite>. Since by a theorem of Biran <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir2</a>]</cite>, <span id="S3.SS2.p72.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">symplectic</span> embeddings of a disjoint union of <math id="S3.SS2.p72.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math> equal balls into <math id="S3.SS2.p72.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed, this would imply the existence of such
symplectic embeddings into <math id="S3.SS2.p72.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> that are <span id="S3.SS2.p72.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">not</span> of Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p73" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p73.1" class="ltx_p">2. The connectedness of the space of <span id="S3.SS2.p73.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">symplectic</span> embeddings of <math id="S3.SS2.p73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS2.p73.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p73.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B^{4}(1),\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>
was proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx67" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD3</a>]</cite> (for previous partial results see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD1</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx66" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD2</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lal</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir1</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p74" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p74.1" class="ltx_p">3. By a theorem of Taubes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tau1</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tau2</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tau3</a>]</cite>, any symplectic form on <math id="S3.SS2.p74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> can be mapped into a positive multiple of <math id="S3.SS2.p74.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math> by a diffeomorphism of <math id="S3.SS2.p74.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, any symplectic form <math id="S3.SS2.p74.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p74.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is of Kähler type and the results above stated for <math id="S3.SS2.p74.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>
hold also for <math id="S3.SS2.p74.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>The cases of <math id="S3.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">♯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with the standard complex structure on it. Define <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">♯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> – as a smooth manifold – as the complex blow-up of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> at one point. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subset M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subset M</annotation></semantics></math> be the exceptional divisor and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\subset M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\subset M</annotation></semantics></math> a projective line.
Given <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler form on <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle[\omega_{\lambda}],[{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}]\rangle=\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle[\omega_{\lambda}],[{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}]\rangle=\pi</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle[\omega_{\lambda}],[E]\rangle=\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle[\omega_{\lambda}],[E]\rangle=\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The manifold <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">♯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> admits Kähler-type complex structures <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{2l+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{2l+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,J_{2l+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,J_{2l+1})</annotation></semantics></math> is biholomorphic to the odd Hirzebruch surface <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{2l+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{2l+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, we assume <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{1}={\widetilde{I}}_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{1}={\widetilde{I}}_{st}</annotation></semantics></math>, as we identify the Hirzebruch surface <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P}^{2}_{I_{st},x},{\widetilde{I}}_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P}^{2}_{I_{st},x},{\widetilde{I}}_{st})</annotation></semantics></math>. Each complex structure on <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> can be mapped by a diffeomorphism into exactly one complex structure <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{2l+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{2l+1}</annotation></semantics></math> – this follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">FriQ</a>]</cite>. In fact, this diffeomorphism can be chosen to lie in <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> – see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mee</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.18 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">♯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler-type form on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">A. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}\geq R_{2}\geq\ldots\geq R_{k-1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}\geq R_{2}\geq\ldots\geq R_{k-1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that
<math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}:=R_{1}\geq\ldots\geq r_{i-1}:=R_{i-1}\geq r_{i}:=\sqrt{\lambda/\pi}\geq r_{i+1}:=R_{i}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}:=R_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}:=R_{1}\geq\ldots\geq r_{i-1}:=R_{i-1}\geq r_{i}:=\sqrt{\lambda/\pi}\geq r_{i+1}:=R_{i}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}:=R_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Then for any proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset(M,J_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset(M,J_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>,
the following conditions are equivalent:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding
<math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> that is
compatible with <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> and isotopic to <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by an isotopy preserving <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (as a set).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The numbers <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the inequalities (v), (c1)-(c6) in part A of
<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the inequalities (v), (c1)-(c6) in part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, any symplectic embedding
<math id="S3.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> is, in fact, of <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">B.
For any <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\ldots,R_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\ldots,R_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> (if they exist!) lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>-action, meaning that
the space of Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Part B of <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> follows directly from
part (I) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
For the proof of part A of
<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> see Section <a href="#S7" title="7 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> (<a href="#S7" title="7 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.19</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">1. We do not know under which conditions on <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the complex structure <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> can be replaced in part A of <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> by
<math id="S3.SS3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{2l+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{2l+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. In order to obtain these conditions one needs to get an explicit description of the Kähler cones
of the blow-ups of the Hirzebruch surfaces <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{2l+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{2l+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, at <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> points – see Section <a href="#S7" title="7 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">2. For any <math id="S3.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, the connectedness of the space of <span id="S3.SS3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">symplectic</span> embeddings of <math id="S3.SS3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> into
<math id="S3.SS3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> was proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx67" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD3</a>]</cite> (also see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lal</a>]</cite> for a previous partial result
for <math id="S3.SS3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p15.1" class="ltx_p">3. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> admits a generalization concerning Kähler-type embeddings
of a disjoint union of <math id="S3.SS3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> balls into a complex blow-up of <math id="S3.SS3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S3.SS3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> points (<math id="S3.SS3.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq 8-j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq 8-j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1,\ldots,7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1,\ldots,7</annotation></semantics></math>) equipped with an appropriate symplectic form.
The proof is a straightforward modification of the proof of <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Now let <math id="S3.SS3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> – as a smooth manifold. Let
<math id="S3.SS3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Fubini-Study form on <math id="S3.SS3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> normalized so that <math id="S3.SS3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}}\omega_{0}=\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}}\omega_{0}=\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given <math id="S3.SS3.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS3.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\mu}=\mu\omega_{0}\oplus\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\mu}=\mu\omega_{0}\oplus\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p17.1" class="ltx_p">The manifold <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> admits Kähler-type complex structures <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{2l}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,J_{2l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,J_{2l})</annotation></semantics></math> is biholomorphic to the even Hirzebruch surface <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{2l}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as the standard complex structure on <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each complex structure on <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be mapped by a diffeomorphism into exactly one complex structure <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{2l}</annotation></semantics></math> – this follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">FriQ</a>]</cite>. In fact, this diffeomorphism can be chosen to lie in <math id="S3.SS3.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> – see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mee</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.20 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S3.SS3.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> be as above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset(M,J_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset(M,J_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> be either a proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold or <math id="S3.SS3.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=(\textrm{pt}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times\textrm{pt})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>pt</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=(\textrm{pt}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times\textrm{pt})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p21.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p22" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p22.1" class="ltx_p">A. Assume <math id="S3.SS3.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS3.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},R_{2},\ldots,R_{k-2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},R_{2},\ldots,R_{k-2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;R_{k-1}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;R_{k-1}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the numbers</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{R_{1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\frac{R_{2}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\ldots,\frac{\mu-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\frac{1-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{R_{1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\frac{R_{2}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\ldots,\frac{\mu-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\frac{1-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p22.2" class="ltx_p">sort them in the non-increasing order and denote the resulting <math id="S3.SS3.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> numbers by <math id="S3.SS3.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following conditions are equivalent:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p24" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding
<math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
that is compatible with
<math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and isotopic to <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by an isotopy preserving <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (as a set).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The numbers <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> defined above satisfy the inequalities in part A of
<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p25.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS3.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the inequalities (v), (c1)-(c6) in part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, any symplectic embedding
<math id="S3.SS3.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> is, in fact, of <math id="S3.SS3.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p26" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p26.1" class="ltx_p">B. Assume that <math id="S3.SS3.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p27.1" class="ltx_p">Then for any <math id="S3.SS3.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
any symplectic embedding of a disjoint union of <math id="S3.SS3.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2l^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2l^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> equal balls into <math id="S3.SS3.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>
is, in fact, of <math id="S3.SS3.p27.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type, and such symplectic (or, equivalently, <math id="S3.SS3.p27.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type) embeddings are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p28.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely, if <math id="S3.SS3.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2l^{2}{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{4}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2l^{2}{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{4}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists a Kähler-type embedding of <math id="S3.SS3.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{2l^{2}}B^{4}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{2l^{2}}B^{4}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>
into <math id="S3.SS3.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S3.SS3.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> that is compatible with <math id="S3.SS3.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
and isotopic to <math id="S3.SS3.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
by an isotopy preserving <math id="S3.SS3.p28.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (as a set).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p29" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p29.1" class="ltx_p">C. For any <math id="S3.SS3.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\ldots,R_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\ldots,R_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS3.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> (if they exist!) lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S3.SS3.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math>-action, meaning that
the space of Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS3.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p30.1" class="ltx_p">Part B of <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> is a particular case of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> (for <math id="S3.SS3.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2</annotation></semantics></math>).
Part C of <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> follows directly from
part (I) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
For the proof of part A of <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> see Section <a href="#S8" title="8 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>
(<a href="#S8" title="8 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p31" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.21</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p32" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p32.1" class="ltx_p">1. For <math id="S3.SS3.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=(\textrm{pt}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times\textrm{pt})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>pt</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=(\textrm{pt}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times\textrm{pt})</annotation></semantics></math> the manifold <math id="S3.SS3.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math>
is symplectomorphic to <math id="S3.SS3.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B^{2}(1)\times B^{2}(\sqrt{\mu}),\omega_{0}\oplus\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>μ</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B^{2}(1)\times B^{2}(\sqrt{\mu}),\omega_{0}\oplus\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> yields a straightforward corollary concerning Kähler-type embeddings of balls into <math id="S3.SS3.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B^{2}(1)\times B^{2}(\sqrt{\mu}),\omega_{0}\oplus\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mi>μ</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B^{2}(1)\times B^{2}(\sqrt{\mu}),\omega_{0}\oplus\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p33.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="S3.SS3.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">symplectic</span> embeddings of balls a similar corollary, as well as the result of part B of <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>,
were proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p34" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p34.1" class="ltx_p">2. We do not know under which conditions on <math id="S3.SS3.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the complex structure <math id="S3.SS3.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS3.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> can be replaced in part A of <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> by
<math id="S3.SS3.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{2l}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
In order to obtain these conditions one needs to get an explicit description of the Kähler cones
of the blow-ups of the Hirzebruch surfaces <math id="S3.SS3.p34.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{2l}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p34.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, at <math id="S3.SS3.p34.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> points – see Section <a href="#S8" title="8 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p35" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p35.1" class="ltx_p">3. For any <math id="S3.SS3.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\ldots,R_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\ldots,R_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the connectedness of the space of <span id="S3.SS3.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">symplectic</span> embeddings <math id="S3.SS3.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> was proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx67" title="" class="ltx_ref">McD3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>The case of tori and K3 surfaces</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=2n</annotation></semantics></math>, be either <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> or a smooth manifold (of real dimension <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>) underlying a complex K3 surface.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As before, let <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler-type symplectic form on <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the case <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> one can assume, without loss of generality, that <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is linear (see <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Kähler-type complex and symplectic structures ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is called <span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">rational</span> if the real cohomology class <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a real multiple of a rational one,
and <span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">irrational</span> otherwise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Irrational Kähler-type symplectic forms on tori and K3 surfaces are compatible with so-called Campana-simple complex structures. Namely, given a Kähler-type complex structure <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on a closed manifold <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, the union of all positive-dimensional proper complex
subvarieties of <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is
either a countable union of proper analytic subvarieties of <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> (and hence has a dense connected complement) or the whole <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Remark 4.2]</cite>).
In the former case, the complex structure <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is called <span id="S3.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Campana-simple</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cam</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">CaDV</a>]</cite> (for more details see Section <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, a Kähler-type complex structure on a torus is Campana-simple if and only if it has no proper positive-dimensional complex subvarieties (see <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>). A symplectic form <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on the torus or a K3 surface is compatible with such a complex structure if and only if <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational (see <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a> and <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>).
See Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 The IHS-hyperkähler case ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> for other examples of manifolds admitting Campana-simple complex structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.22 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=2n</annotation></semantics></math>, be either <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> or a smooth manifold (of real dimension <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>) underlying a complex K3 surface
and let <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler-type symplectic form on <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S3.SS4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p12.1" class="ltx_p">A. Kähler-type embeddings of <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p13.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely, assume that <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is
compatible with a Campana-simple complex structure <math id="S3.SS4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Then
there exists an <math id="S3.SS4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding <math id="S3.SS4.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p15.1" class="ltx_p">B.
The group <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on the set of connected components of
<math id="S3.SS4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S3.SS4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p16.1" class="ltx_p">C. Any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS4.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> (if they exist!) lie in the same orbit of the
<math id="S3.SS4.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-action. They lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S3.SS4.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-action
if and only if they favor a common connected component of <math id="S3.SS4.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. In the latter case there exists <math id="S3.SS4.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt Teich}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt Teich}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
such that both embeddings are of <math id="S3.SS4.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p17" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.23 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{T}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{T}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a torus of real dimension <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case all symplectic forms and complex structures on <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are of Kähler-type and
any symplectic (i.e.,
area-preserving) embedding into <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is of Kähler-type. In particular, for any symplectic form <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (note that any such form is rational!) the result of part A of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> is true
and the space of Kähler-type embeddings of any disjoint union of domains into <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is path-connected
(since the space of symplectic forms on <math id="S3.SS4.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is path-connected <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mos</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p19.1" class="ltx_p">For the proof
of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>
see Section <a href="#S10.SS3" title="10.3 Kähler-type embeddings of balls into tori and K3 surfaces – the proofs ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.3</span></a> (<a href="#S10.SS3" title="10.3 Kähler-type embeddings of balls into tori and K3 surfaces – the proofs ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p20.1" class="ltx_p">For a generalization of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> to arbitrary
closed symplectic manifolds admitting Campana-simple complex structures compatible with the symplectic form see <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. In fact, we will use <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> in the proof of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p21.1" class="ltx_p">For tori the existence result in <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> admits the following equivalent formulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p22" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.24 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S3.SS4.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\subset{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\subset{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a lattice of rank <math id="S3.SS4.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS4.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:={\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:={\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding torus. Let <math id="S3.SS4.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega,I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega,I</annotation></semantics></math> be the symplectic and the complex structures on <math id="S3.SS4.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the standard flat symplectic and complex structures on <math id="S3.SS4.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p24.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S3.SS4.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> admits no proper positive-dimensional
complex subvarieties. Let <math id="S3.SS4.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS4.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S3.SS4.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p25.1" class="ltx_p">Then there exists a Kähler form <math id="S3.SS4.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS4.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> isotopic to <math id="S3.SS4.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and a Kähler-type embedding
<math id="S3.SS4.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> which is holomorphic with respect to <math id="S3.SS4.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> – i.e., an embedding which is symplectic with respect
to <math id="S3.SS4.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and holomorphic with respect to <math id="S3.SS4.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p26" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p27.1" class="ltx_p">It suffices to prove a similar claim for the case where <math id="S3.SS4.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma={\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma={\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> are compatible symplectic and complex structures on <math id="S3.SS4.p27.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS4.p27.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> being Campana-simple (the proof can be reduced to this case by using
a diffeomorphism <math id="S3.SS4.p27.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\Gamma\to{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\Gamma\to{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>
induced by an <math id="S3.SS4.p27.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear isomorphism of <math id="S3.SS4.p27.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}={\mathbb{C}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}={\mathbb{C}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> sending <math id="S3.SS4.p27.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS4.p27.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>). In order to prove the corollary in this case, apply part A of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> and
keep the complex structure fixed while changing the symplectic form by an isotopy
(cf. <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Kähler-type and tame embeddings ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>).
<span id="S3.SS4.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p28" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.25 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p29" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p29.1" class="ltx_p">1. For an arbitrary Kähler-type symplectic form <math id="S3.SS4.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS4.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> or on a manifold <math id="S3.SS4.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> underlying a K3 surface,
the unobstructedness of symplectic – but not necessarily Kähler-type or tame – embeddings of an arbitrary collection of disjoint balls
was proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Thm. 3.1]</cite>. In the case <math id="S3.SS4.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{T}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{T}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> a weaker version of this result had been previously proved by Latschev-McDuff-Schlenk <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx60" title="" class="ltx_ref">LatMS</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p30" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p30.1" class="ltx_p">2. It is a well-known open problem (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDS</a>, Ch.15, Prob. 15]</cite>) whether <math id="S3.SS4.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)=\mathrm{Symp}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)=\mathrm{Symp}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
for a Kähler-type symplectic form <math id="S3.SS4.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS4.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
If the answer to this question is positive, then
Part B of
<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>
would imply that the space of the Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS4.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
is path-connected for any <math id="S3.SS4.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p30.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Conversely, a counter-example to the latter claim would show that
<math id="S3.SS4.p30.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)\neq\mathrm{Symp}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)\neq\mathrm{Symp}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p31.1" class="ltx_p">In the case when <math id="S3.SS4.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a manifold underlying a K3 surface it is known by the result of Sheridan-Smith <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx78" title="" class="ltx_ref">ShS</a>]</cite>
that <math id="S3.SS4.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)\neq\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)\neq\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> for certain
Kähler-type symplectic structures <math id="S3.SS4.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS4.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>,
including some irrational ones.
It has been known since the work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sei</a>]</cite> of Seidel that for some (not necessarily irrational) Kähler-type symplectic forms <math id="S3.SS4.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS4.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> one has <math id="S3.SS4.p31.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\neq\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\neq\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>; by a result of Smirnov <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sm</a>]</cite>, this definitely holds for some irrational Kähler-type symplectic forms <math id="S3.SS4.p31.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS4.p31.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p32" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p32.1" class="ltx_p">3. <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> for one ellipsoid – and, in particular, for one ball – was previously proved by Luef and Wang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx64" title="" class="ltx_ref">LuWa</a>]</cite> using a method similar to the one in this paper.
Let us note that the paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx64" title="" class="ltx_ref">LuWa</a>]</cite> by Luef and Wang provides a very interesting connection between Kähler-type embeddings of ellipsoids into tori and Gabor frames – an important notion in signal processing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p33.1" class="ltx_p">We expect that the technique used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx64" title="" class="ltx_ref">LuWa</a>]</cite>, as well as the
techniques developed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV2</a>]</cite> in relation to symplectic embeddings of ellipsoids into tori and K3 surfaces,
can be used to generalize <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>
to Kähler-type embeddings of ellipsoids.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p34" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p34.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.26 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p35.1" class="ltx_p">The assumption that the symplectic form <math id="S3.SS4.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS4.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational is essential in <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>: otherwise there may be obstructions
for the existence of Kähler-type embeddings of (disjoint unions of) balls into <math id="S3.SS4.p35.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> that
are independent of the symplectic volume – for instance, the obstructions coming from Seshadri constants.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p36.1" class="ltx_p">For simplicity, we will explain this below in the case of one ball and <math id="S3.SS4.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
expanding on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx60" title="" class="ltx_ref">LatMS</a>, Sec. 2.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p37.1" class="ltx_p">First, recall the definition of Seshadri constants. Let <math id="S3.SS4.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,I)</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex projective manifold and let <math id="S3.SS4.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler form on <math id="S3.SS4.p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,I)</annotation></semantics></math>
such that the cohomology class <math id="S3.SS4.p37.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is integral and equals the first Chern class of an ample line bundle on <math id="S3.SS4.p37.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,I)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume that <math id="S3.SS4.p37.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p37.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:{\widetilde{X}}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:{\widetilde{X}}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is the complex blow-up of <math id="S3.SS4.p37.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S3.SS4.p37.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS4.p37.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:=\pi^{-1}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:=\pi^{-1}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the exceptional divisor.
Define the <span id="S3.SS4.p37.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Seshadri constant</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\epsilon(X,I,[\omega],x):=\sup\left\{s&gt;0\ \left|\right.\ \left\langle\pi^{*}[\omega],[{\widetilde{C}}]\right\rangle-sE\cdot[{\widetilde{C}}]\geq 0\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo fence="false" lspace="0.500em" rspace="0.667em">|</mo><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon(X,I,[\omega],x):=\sup\left\{s&gt;0\ \left|\right.\ \left\langle\pi^{*}[\omega],[{\widetilde{C}}]\right\rangle-sE\cdot[{\widetilde{C}}]\geq 0\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left.\textrm{for all (possibly singular) complex curves}\ {\widetilde{C}}\subset{\widetilde{X}}\ \right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>for all (possibly singular) complex curves</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left.\textrm{for all (possibly singular) complex curves}\ {\widetilde{C}}\subset{\widetilde{X}}\ \right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p37.2" class="ltx_p">Observe that <math id="S3.SS4.p37.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\cdot[{\widetilde{C}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\cdot[{\widetilde{C}}]</annotation></semantics></math> is the multiplicity of the singularity of <math id="S3.SS4.p37.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S3.SS4.p37.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p38.1" class="ltx_p">Then</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\epsilon(X,I,[\omega],x)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\epsilon(X,I,[\omega],x)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex16.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext='\displaystyle\sup\left\{\ \pi r^{2}\ |\ \textrm{There exists a K\"{a}hler form}\ \omega^{\prime},\ [\omega^{\prime}]=[\omega],\right.' display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">{</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext>There exists a Kähler form</mtext><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sup\left\{\ \pi r^{2}\ |\ \textrm{There exists a K\"{a}hler form}\ \omega^{\prime},\ [\omega^{\prime}]=[\omega],\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext='\displaystyle\textrm{and a K\"{a}hler embedding}\ f:B^{2n}(r)\to(X,\omega^{\prime},I),' display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>and a Kähler embedding</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textrm{and a K\"{a}hler embedding}\ f:B^{2n}(r)\to(X,\omega^{\prime},I),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left.f^{*}\omega^{\prime}=\omega_{0},\ f^{*}I=J_{0},\ f(0)=x\ \right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.500em">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left.f^{*}\omega^{\prime}=\omega_{0},\ f^{*}I=J_{0},\ f(0)=x\ \right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p38.2" class="ltx_p">For a proof see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ec</a>, Thm. 0.6]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fl</a>, Thm. 1.4]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx103" title="" class="ltx_ref">WN2</a>, Thm. 1.3]</cite>, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx64" title="" class="ltx_ref">LuWa</a>, Thm. A]</cite>. (It had been previously observed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Laz</a>]</cite> that the inequality <math id="S3.SS4.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon(X,I,[\omega],x)\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon(X,I,[\omega],x)\geq</annotation></semantics></math> [the right-hand side in (<a href="#S3.Ex16" title="In 3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>)] follows from the results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p39.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S3.SS4.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is a rational symplectic form on <math id="S3.SS4.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the study of the symplectic and Kähler-type embeddings in <math id="S3.SS4.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> we may assume then, without loss of generality, that <math id="S3.SS4.p39.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS4.p39.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex structure on <math id="S3.SS4.p39.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>
compatible with <math id="S3.SS4.p39.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider an ample holomorphic bundle <math id="S3.SS4.p39.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS4.p39.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS4.p39.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(L)=[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(L)=[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The complex manifold <math id="S3.SS4.p39.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I)</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as an abelian variety and <math id="S3.SS4.p39.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> as its polarization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p40.1" class="ltx_p">Since the
identity component of the group of biholomorphisms of a complex torus acts transitively on the torus,
<math id="S3.SS4.p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I,[\omega],x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I,[\omega],x)</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on <math id="S3.SS4.p40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p40.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> and not on <math id="S3.SS4.p40.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, so we will denote <math id="S3.SS4.p40.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I,[\omega]):=\epsilon({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I,[\omega],x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I,[\omega]):=\epsilon({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I,[\omega],x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p41.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.SS4.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})=\pi^{n}r^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})=\pi^{n}r^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, (<a href="#S3.Ex16" title="In 3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) implies that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext='\sup\left\{\ {\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})\ \left|\right.\ \textrm{There exists a K\"{a}hler-type embedding}\right.' display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">{</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em">|</mo><mtext>There exists a Kähler-type embedding</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup\left\{\ {\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})\ \left|\right.\ \textrm{There exists a K\"{a}hler-type embedding}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left.f:(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)\ \right\}=\sup_{I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)}\epsilon^{n}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I,[\omega])," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left.f:(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)\ \right\}=\sup_{I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)}\epsilon^{n}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I,[\omega]),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p41.2" class="ltx_p">where the supremum in the right-hand side is taken over all <math id="S3.SS4.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S3.SS4.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p42" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p42.1" class="ltx_p">There exist examples where</p>
<table id="S3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)}\epsilon^{n}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I,[\omega])&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)}\epsilon^{n}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I,[\omega])&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p42.2" class="ltx_p">For instance, consider the torus <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{4}={\mathbb{R}}^{4}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{4}={\mathbb{R}}^{4}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with the symplectic form <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=dp_{1}\wedge dq_{1}+dp_{2}\wedge dq_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=dp_{1}\wedge dq_{1}+dp_{2}\wedge dq_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},p_{2},q_{1},q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},p_{2},q_{1},q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are the coordinates on <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any complex structure <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> one can biholomorphically identify <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{4},I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{4},I)</annotation></semantics></math> with a principally polarized abelian variety. In this case, by a result of Steffens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ste</a>, Prop. 2]</cite>, we have <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon({\mathbb{T}}^{4},I,[\omega])\leq 4/3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon({\mathbb{T}}^{4},I,[\omega])\leq 4/3</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. At the same time <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{4},\omega)=\int_{{\mathbb{T}}^{4}}\omega^{2}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></msub><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{4},\omega)=\int_{{\mathbb{T}}^{4}}\omega^{2}=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)}\epsilon^{2}({\mathbb{T}}^{4},I,[\omega])\leq(4/3)^{2}&lt;2={\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{4},\omega)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)}\epsilon^{2}({\mathbb{T}}^{4},I,[\omega])\leq(4/3)^{2}&lt;2={\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{4},\omega).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p42.3" class="ltx_p">This yields an obstruction, independent of the symplectic volume, for the existence of Kähler-type embeddings of a ball into <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{4},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{4},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>:
a ball whose volume is between <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4/3)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4/3)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2={\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{4},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2={\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{4},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> does not admit Kähler-type embeddings into <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{4},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{4},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
At the same time, by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx60" title="" class="ltx_ref">LatMS</a>, Thm. 1.1]</cite>,
such a ball does admit a
symplectic embedding into <math id="S3.SS4.p42.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{4},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{4},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S3.SS4.p42.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thus, there exists
a symplectic embedding of a ball which is not of Kähler type</span>.
We do not know whether such an embedding is tame.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p43.1" class="ltx_p">Let us now consider tame embeddings of balls into tori and K3 surfaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p44" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p44.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p44.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.27 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p45.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=2n</annotation></semantics></math>, be either <math id="S3.SS4.p45.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> or a smooth manifold (of real dimension <math id="S3.SS4.p45.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>) underlying a complex K3 surface.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p46.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be
Kähler-type forms on <math id="S3.SS4.p46.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> that are irrational and satisfy
<math id="S3.SS4.p46.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{M}\omega_{1}^{n}=\int_{M}\omega_{2}^{n}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{M}\omega_{1}^{n}=\int_{M}\omega_{2}^{n}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p47.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS4.p47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, be compact domains with piecewise-smooth boundary whose interiors contain the origin.
Assume that <math id="S3.SS4.p47.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(W_{i};{\mathbb{R}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(W_{i};{\mathbb{R}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS4.p47.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S3.SS4.p47.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p48.1" class="ltx_p">Then, for any <math id="S3.SS4.p48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})=\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})=\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p49" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p49.1" class="ltx_p">For the proof of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> see Section <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> (<a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p50" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p50.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p50.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.28</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p51" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p51.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> generalizes a similar result in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>]</cite> about symplectic (not necessarily
tame) embeddings and its proof is similar to the proof in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p52" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p52.1" class="ltx_p">Let us present applications of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p53" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p53.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p53.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.29 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p54" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p54.1" class="ltx_p">Assume:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p55" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is either <math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> or a smooth manifold underlying a K3 surface.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint union of <math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> (possibly different) balls.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I5.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p56" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p56.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p57" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p57.1" class="ltx_p">A.
For any irrational Kähler-type symplectic form <math id="S3.SS4.p57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS4.p57.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p57.2" class="ltx_p">meaning that Kähler-type embeddings of <math id="S3.SS4.p57.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS4.p57.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are
unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p58" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p58.1" class="ltx_p">B. There exists a <math id="S3.SS4.p58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant open dense set of Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S3.SS4.p58.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on <math id="S3.SS4.p58.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p58.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and containing, in particular, all irrational Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S3.SS4.p58.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that for each <math id="S3.SS4.p58.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> in this set
<math id="S3.SS4.p58.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math> – meaning that <math id="S3.SS4.p58.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame embeddings of <math id="S3.SS4.p58.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS4.p58.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are
unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p59" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p59.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p59.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.30 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p60" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p60.1" class="ltx_p">Assume:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p61" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is either a disjoint union of <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> identical copies of a <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional polydisk</p>
<table id="S3.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n_{1}}(R_{1})\times\ldots\times B^{2n_{l}}(R_{l}),\ n_{1}+\ldots+n_{l}=n,\ R_{1},\ldots,R_{l}&gt;0,l&gt;1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n_{1}}(R_{1})\times\ldots\times B^{2n_{l}}(R_{l}),\ n_{1}+\ldots+n_{l}=n,\ R_{1},\ldots,R_{l}&gt;0,l&gt;1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I6.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">or a disjoint union of <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> identical copies of a parallelepiped</p>
<table id="S3.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}):=\left\{\sum_{j=1}^{2n}s_{j}e_{j},0\leq s_{j}\leq 1,j=1,\ldots,2n\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}):=\left\{\sum_{j=1}^{2n}s_{j}e_{j},0\leq s_{j}\leq 1,j=1,\ldots,2n\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I6.i2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a basis of the vector space <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I6.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p62" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p62.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p63" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p63.1" class="ltx_p">A.
For any positive volume there exists a dense <math id="S3.SS4.p63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit (of an irrational Kähler-type symplectic form depending on <math id="S3.SS4.p63.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>) in the space of Kähler-type symplectic forms of that volume on <math id="S3.SS4.p63.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that for any <math id="S3.SS4.p63.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in this orbit we have
<math id="S3.SS4.p63.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{K}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{K}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math> –
or, in other words, Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S3.SS4.p63.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p64" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p64.1" class="ltx_p">B. There exists a <math id="S3.SS4.p64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant open dense set of Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S3.SS4.p64.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on <math id="S3.SS4.p64.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p64.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and containing, in particular, all irrational Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S3.SS4.p64.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that for each <math id="S3.SS4.p64.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in this set
<math id="S3.SS4.p64.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math> –
or, in other words, <math id="S3.SS4.p64.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame embeddings <math id="S3.SS4.p64.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p65" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p65.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p65.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.31 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p66" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p66.1" class="ltx_p">Assume:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p67" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I7" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I7.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth manifold underlying a K3 surface.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I7.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint union of <math id="S3.I7.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> identical copies of a parallelepiped</p>
<table id="S3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}):=\left\{\sum_{j=1}^{2n}s_{j}e_{j},0\leq s_{j}\leq 1,j=1,\ldots,2n\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}):=\left\{\sum_{j=1}^{2n}s_{j}e_{j},0\leq s_{j}\leq 1,j=1,\ldots,2n\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I7.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.I7.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a basis of the vector space <math id="S3.I7.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I7.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I7.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I7.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I7.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p68" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p68.1" class="ltx_p">Then there exists a <math id="S3.SS4.p68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant open dense set of Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S3.SS4.p68.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on <math id="S3.SS4.p68.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p68.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and containing, in particular, all irrational Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S3.SS4.p68.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that for each <math id="S3.SS4.p68.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in this set
<math id="S3.SS4.p68.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that <math id="S3.SS4.p68.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame embeddings of <math id="S3.SS4.p68.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS4.p68.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p69" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p69.1" class="ltx_p">For the proof of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> and <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> see Section <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>. These corollaries generalize similar results for symplectic – but not necessarily tame – embeddings that were proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p70" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p70.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p70.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.32 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p71" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p71.1" class="ltx_p">We know only one way to show the possible existence of embeddings (of a disjoint union of domains) into <math id="S3.SS4.p71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> that are symplectic and non-tame, or tame and not Kähler-type – via the volume restrictions: e.g., if Kähler-type embeddings cannot fill more than a certain portion of the volume of <math id="S3.SS4.p71.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> while tame embeddings can. This is how we got in
<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> that there exist symplectic embeddings (of balls) that are not of Kähler-type.
It would be interesting to find out whether there are other ways of detecting symplectic and non-tame, or tame and not Kähler-type, embeddings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p72" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p72.1" class="ltx_p">Together with <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, this leads to the following questions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p73" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p73.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p73.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 3.33 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p74" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p74.1" class="ltx_p">Does there exist a closed manifold equipped with a Kähler-type symplectic form that admits a
symplectic embedding which is not tame, or a tame embedding which is not of Kähler-type? In particular, does there exist a rational Kähler-type symplectic form <math id="S3.SS4.p74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS4.p74.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p74.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, or on a smooth manifold <math id="S3.SS4.p74.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> underlying a complex K3 surface, and a disjoint union of balls <math id="S3.SS4.p74.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p74.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S3.SS4.p74.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> does not admit a tame embedding into <math id="S3.SS4.p74.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>?</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>The IHS-hyperkähler case</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">K3 surfaces are particular examples of so
called IHS-hyperkähler manifolds,
that is, compact hyperkähler manifolds
of maximal holonomy. Most of the results
of Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>
generalize to such manifolds. In this section we present
relevant definitions and statements.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that a <span id="S3.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hyperkähler manifold</span> is a manifold
equipped with three complex structures <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the
quaternionic relations and three symplectic forms
<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1},\omega_{2},\omega_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1},\omega_{2},\omega_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible, respectively, with
<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the three Riemannian metrics <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega_{i}(\cdot,I_{i}\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{i}(\cdot,I_{i}\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,3</annotation></semantics></math>, coincide. Such a collection <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{H}}=\{I_{1},I_{2},I_{3},\omega_{1},\omega_{2},\omega_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℌ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{H}}=\{I_{1},I_{2},I_{3},\omega_{1},\omega_{2},\omega_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of
complex structures and symplectic forms on a manifold is called a
<span id="S3.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hyperkähler structure</span>. All the complex and
symplectic structures appearing in a hyperkähler
structure induce the same orientation on the manifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed connected and simply connected manifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The Levi-Civita connection of a Kähler manifold
preserves its complex structure and the Kähler form.
Therefore, the Levi-Civita connection of a hyperkähler
manifold preserves the three complex structures <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{1},I_{2},I_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and the three
symplectic forms <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1},\omega_{2},\omega_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1},\omega_{2},\omega_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The subgroup of <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL({\mathbb{R}}^{4n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL({\mathbb{R}}^{4n})</annotation></semantics></math> preserving both the quaternionic
Hermitian metric and the quaternionic action is
called <span id="S3.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the compact symplectic group</span> and is denoted
by <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the group of all quaternionic
unitary matrices; it can also be obtained as
a maximal compact form of a complex symplectic
group <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Sp}({\mathbb{C}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Sp}({\mathbb{C}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the Levi-Civita
connection of <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,g,I_{1},I_{2},I_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,g,I_{1},I_{2},I_{3})</annotation></semantics></math>
preserves the metric and the quaternionic operators,
its holonomy belongs to <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, a priori the
holonomy group can be smaller; for example,
for a hyperkähler flat torus, the holonomy is trivial.
The de Rham theorem and Berger’s classification of
irreducible holonomies imply that any
compact hyperkähler manifold has a finite
covering which is decomposed into a product
of a torus and manifolds with maximal holonomy;
this result is called <span id="S3.SS5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the Bogomolov
decomposition theorem</span>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bo1</a>]</cite>.
This theorem is the reason why the
maximal holonomy hyperkähler manifolds are also
called <span id="S3.SS5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">irreducible
holomorphically symplectic (IHS)</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A maximal holonomy compact hyperkähler manifold
can be characterized cohomologically: <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
has maximal holonomy if and only <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{2,0}(M)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{2,0}(M)=1</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}(M)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}(M)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{2,0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{2,0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the
dimension of the group of closed, holomorphic 2-forms on <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>,
for any of its complex structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">K3 surfaces, as well as the Hilbert schemes of
points for K3 surfaces, are known to admit IHS-hyperkähler
structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We say that a symplectic/complex structure on <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is
<span id="S3.SS5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">of IHS-hyperkähler type</span>, if it appears
in <span id="S3.SS5.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">some</span> maximal holonomy hyperkähler structure. In
particular, all IHS-hyperkähler-type symplectic and
complex structures are of Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.34 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">1. Note that even if a symplectic and a complex structure are of IHS-hyperkähler type and compatible, it still does not mean that they can be included in <span id="S3.SS5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the same</span> IHS-hyperkähler structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">2. All complex structures and all Kähler-type symplectic structures on a K3 surface are in fact of IHS-hyperkähler type – see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV3</a>, Prop. 3.1]</cite>.
As we have already mentioned, it is an open question whether any symplectic form on a K3 surface is of Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p11.1" class="ltx_p">3. Manifolds admitting IHS-hyperkähler structures are also
called hyperkähler manifolds of maximal holonomy, because
the holonomy group of a hyperkähler manifold is <math id="S3.SS5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Sp}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Sp}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> (the
group of invertible quaternionic <math id="S3.SS5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices) – and not
its proper subgroup – if and only if the hyperkähler structure is IHS
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bes</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p12.1" class="ltx_p">4.
Note that the property of being IHS is a topological
invariant: a holomorphically symplectic Kähler manifold
<math id="S3.SS5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{C}}M=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{C}}M=2n</annotation></semantics></math>, is IHS if and only if <math id="S3.SS5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}(M)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}(M)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and
there exists a primitive integral quadratic form <math id="S3.SS5.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS5.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>
(called the Bogomolov-Beauville-Fujiki form <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bea1</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu</a>]</cite>)
that satisfies, for some rational <math id="S3.SS5.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
the
Fujiki relation <math id="S3.SS5.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{M}\eta^{2n}=Cq(\eta,\eta)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{M}\eta^{2n}=Cq(\eta,\eta)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the set of
IHS-hyperkähler type symplectic/complex structures on
a given IHS-hyperkähler manifold coincides with the set of
hyperkähler-type symplectic/complex structures – i.e., the symplectic/complex structures appearing in some
hyperkähler structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Now we can go over Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and define <span id="S3.SS5.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IHS-hyperkähler-type</span>
embeddings by replacing the words “Kähler-type” in the relevant definitions by the words “IHS-hyperkähler-type”. In particular, in this way we can define <span id="S3.SS5.p14.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IHS-hyperkähler-type symplectic embeddings</span> of domains in <math id="S3.SS5.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{4n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{4n}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS5.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with an IHS-hyperkähler-type symplectic form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Note that, in view of part 2 of <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 The IHS-hyperkähler case ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, for symplectic embeddings into a K3 surface being of IHS-hyperkähler type is the same as being of Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p16.1" class="ltx_p">The results of Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> on Kähler-type
embeddings generalize to the IHS-hyperkähler case as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p17" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p17.1" class="ltx_p">- Part A of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>
generalizes directly to the IHS-hyperkähler case as long as one assumes that the Campana-simple complex structure <math id="S3.SS5.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is of IHS-hyperkähler-type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p18.1" class="ltx_p">- The claim in part C of
<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>
concerning the action of <math id="S3.SS5.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
generalizes to the IHS-hyperkähler case, if <math id="S3.SS5.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced by the space of the IHS-hyperkähler-type complex structures on <math id="S3.SS5.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a general IHS-hyperkähler manifold <math id="S3.SS5.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> we cannot say anything about the transitivity of the
<math id="S3.SS5.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Let us discuss the results on
<math id="S3.SS5.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame
embeddings into <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> for an IHS-hyperkähler-type form <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.
One can define
tame and <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame
embeddings into <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> exactly as in <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Kähler-type and tame embeddings ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>
using <span id="S3.SS5.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">Kähler-type</span> complex structures. Another possibility is to modify the definition of
tame and <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame
embeddings into <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
by using <span id="S3.SS5.p19.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">IHS-hyperkähler-type</span> complex structures in <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Kähler-type and tame embeddings ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> instead of Kähler-type ones. The claim <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})=\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})=\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> holds for the original <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Kähler-type and tame embeddings ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, as well as for a version of <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> defined using the
<math id="S3.SS5.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame
embeddings in the second sense above,
if <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in the same connected component of the space of IHS-hyperkähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S3.SS5.p19.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p20.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S12" title="12 Hyperkähler case – proofs ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> we discuss how to modify
the proofs of the results of Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> in order to obtain the above-mentioned results in the IHS-hyperkähler case.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Plan of the paper and an outline of the proofs</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we describe the plan of the paper and outline the proofs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In order to simplify the exposition, let us fix a connected component <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and the radii <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
of the balls from the beginning. Let <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{r}}:=(r_{1},\ldots,r_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{r}}:=(r_{1},\ldots,r_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we prove <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>
and
<a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.
Namely, we define the following spaces:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">- The space <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑃𝑎𝑖𝑟𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> formed by pairs <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta,f)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is a Kähler-type symplectic form on <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
isotopic to <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> is a Kähler-type embedding favoring <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">- The Teichmüller space <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}:={\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑃𝑎𝑖𝑟𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}:={\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">- The space <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> formed by triples <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\cdot{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\cdot{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the pair <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the set <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math> defined in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, and
<math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}h_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}h_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>
is a holomorphic embedding such that <math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}(0)=x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}(0)=x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">- The Teichmüller space <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}:={\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}:={\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We then construct a continuous surjective map <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\to{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\to{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The construction uses
the symplectic blow-up and blow-down operations similar to the ones used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>]</cite>. Let us note that
for the local constructions on which these operations are based we use
the regularized maximum of plurisubharmonic functions while in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>]</cite> a different analytic technique is used.
We also need the regularized maximum tool to construct a Kähler form on <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}},{\widetilde{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}},{\widetilde{I}})</annotation></semantics></math> in the class
<math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> which is standard <span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">on a neighborhood of each
exceptional divisor</span> – this is needed to obtain a Kähler form on <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> by the symplectic blow-down construction (unlike in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>]</cite>, where the goal was to produce a symplectic, not necessarily Kähler, form on <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, and this was done much
easier, using Moser’s method for forms on <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The symplectic blow-down construction, enhanced as we have just described, and a relative version of Moser’s method are used to construct <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, and the symplectic blow-up is used to verify that
<math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective. The continuity of <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>
is verified using the observation that the set <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is discrete and therefore it suffices
to check that the preimage of a point is open, which is then proved using again a relative version of Moser’s method.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We then prove <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The mere existence of <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> implies that if <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math> (and hence <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>) is non-empty, there exists a Kähler-type embedding <math id="S4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> favoring <math id="S4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The connectivity of the set <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) yields the transitivity of the
<math id="S4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-action on the space of Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> favoring <math id="S4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. Using methods of complex geometry (the Kodaira-Spencer stability and the Demailly-Paun description of the Kähler cones of fibers of an analytic deformation family)
we show that the connectivity of <math id="S4.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the connectivity of <math id="S4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The latter
easily implies the connectivity of <math id="S4.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous and surjective, we get that <math id="S4.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
connected, and since it is discrete, it is just a point. This easily implies the transitivity of the <math id="S4.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> we consider the case of a closed symplectic manifold for which
the symplectic form is compatible with a so-called Campana-simple complex structure – i.e., a Kähler-type complex structure admitting “few” complex subvarieties. Examples of such manifolds include tori and smooth manifolds underlying complex K3 surfaces equipped with irrational Kähler-type symplectic forms. The general results on Kähler-type embeddings of balls into such manifolds, proved in Section <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, are used further in the paper to deduce the results on such embeddings into tori
and K3 surfaces stated in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.
The key existence result on such embeddings is based on the observation, made already in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx60" title="" class="ltx_ref">LatMS</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>]</cite>,
that for any Campana-simple complex structure <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> compatible
with <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and any Campana-generic <math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}})\in{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}})\in{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math>, as long as
<math id="S4.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}))&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}))&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. This follows from the Demailly-Paun description of the Kähler cone
of a closed Kähler manifold <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p14.1" class="ltx_p">In Sections <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, <a href="#S7" title="7 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, <a href="#S8" title="8 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>,
<a href="#S10" title="10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> we
discuss the cases
of the specific <math id="S4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>: complex projective spaces and their products, Hirzebruch surfaces, tori and K3 surfaces. In each of these cases we discuss the structure of the Teichmüller
space of Kähler-type complex structures on <math id="S4.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and of its subset formed by equivalence classes of complex structures
compatible with a given symplectic form on <math id="S4.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, as well as the Kähler cones of the complex blow-ups of <math id="S4.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
We use this information in order to apply our general results about Kähler-type embeddings of balls
and deduce the results in
Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>
about these specific <math id="S4.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p15.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> we discuss the proofs of the results in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.
concerning tame embeddings of arbitrary domains.
The strategy of the proof of
<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>
is the same as for a similar claim in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>]</cite>.
Namely, with <math id="S4.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> being either <math id="S4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> or a smooth manifold underlying a K3 surface,
the group <math id="S4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> of orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms of <math id="S4.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the space
<math id="S4.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
of Kähler-type forms on <math id="S4.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of
total volume <math id="S4.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. The function
<math id="S4.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\mapsto\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\mapsto\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> is clearly invariant under the action. We will show
that this function is lower semicontinuous (with respect to the
<math id="S4.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-topology on <math id="S4.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>) – the proof is a modification of the proof of a similar claim in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>]</cite>.
Then we use the result from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>]</cite> saying that
the orbit of a form <math id="S4.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> under the
action of <math id="S4.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is dense in <math id="S4.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, as long as <math id="S4.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational. Since the orbits
of both <math id="S4.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are dense in <math id="S4.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, we get, by the
lower semicontinuity, that
<math id="S4.p15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})\leq\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})\leq\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.p15.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})\geq\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})\geq\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
which means that
<math id="S4.p15.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})=\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})=\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the proofs of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> and <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> follow the same lines as the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Cor. 3.3]</cite>
where the existence of symplectic embeddings of polydisks into tori was proved.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p16.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S12" title="12 Hyperkähler case – proofs ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> we discuss how the proofs in the K3 case can be modified (or just carried over without any changes) in order to prove the results in the hyperkähler case stated in Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 The IHS-hyperkähler case ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, in Section <a href="#S13" title="13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> (the Appendix) we discuss various well-known facts concerning the dependence of the Hodge decomposition on the
complex structure, the deformations of complex structures, Moser’s method, and Alexander’s trick that we use in the paper and
for which we could not find a direct reference in the literature.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As before, let <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=2n</annotation></semantics></math>, be a closed connected manifold admitting Kähler structures. Let <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler-type symplectic form on <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{r}}=(r_{1},\ldots,r_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{r}}=(r_{1},\ldots,r_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected component of <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">From now on,
all the spaces of maps between manifolds and of differential forms and complex structures on manifolds will be endowed with the <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-topologies. All the product/quotient spaces will be endowed with the product/quotient topologies.
</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>The <math id="S5.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-action on the space of
Kähler-type embeddings</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Denote:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{Emb}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Emb</mtext><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{Emb}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math> – the space of the symplectic embeddings
<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{EK}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)\subset\textit{Emb}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">EK</mtext><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Emb</mtext><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{EK}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)\subset\textit{Emb}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math> –
the space of the Kähler-type embeddings
<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{EK}_{M,\omega,{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">EK</mtext><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{EK}_{M,\omega,{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math> –
the space of the Kähler-type
embeddings
<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
favoring <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Given a symplectic embedding <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, any symplectic embedding <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> can be mapped in <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> by an element of <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, and moreover, this element can be chosen arbitrarily close to the identity, provided <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. This fact is standard and follows from the well-known Alexander trick and the symplectic isotopy extension results – see <a href="#S13.SS4" title="13.4 Alexander’s trick ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.4</span></a> for details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Since symplectomorphisms of <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> map Kähler-type embeddings into Kähler-type embeddings, this yields that
<math id="S5.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{EK}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">EK</mtext><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{EK}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
is an open <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant subset of <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{Emb}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Emb</mtext><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{Emb}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
(in the <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-topology). This yields the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.1 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The space</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\textit{EK}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)}{\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">EK</mtext><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\textit{EK}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)}{\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">and consequently the space</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\textit{EK}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)}{\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">EK</mtext><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\textit{EK}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)}{\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.3" class="ltx_p">and its subspace</p>
<table id="S5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\textit{EK}_{M,\omega,{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)}{\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">EK</mtext><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\textit{EK}_{M,\omega,{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)}{\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.4" class="ltx_p">are discrete.
<span id="S5.SS1.p8.4.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.2 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑃𝑎𝑖𝑟𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the set of pairs <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta,f)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\cdot\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\cdot\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler-type
embedding into <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> favoring <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. (Note that the embedding here is into <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math>, not <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>!)</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Define</p>
<table id="S5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}:={\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑃𝑎𝑖𝑟𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}:={\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p11.2" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta,f)\in{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑃𝑎𝑖𝑟𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta,f)\in{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\eta,f\}\in{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\eta,f\}\in{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> the element of
<math id="S5.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> represented by <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta,f)</annotation></semantics></math> – i.e., the <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of <math id="S5.SS1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta,f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta,f)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The following proposition follows easily from the definitions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.3 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The natural map</p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\textit{EK}_{M,\omega,{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)}{\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)}\to{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">EK</mtext><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\textit{EK}_{M,\omega,{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)}{\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)}\to{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p14.2" class="ltx_p">is a homeomorphism. In particular, in both cases <math id="S5.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is discrete.
<span id="S5.SS1.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">A discrete topological space is connected if and only if it is a point. Together with <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 The Symp(𝑀,𝜔)∩Diff_0⁢(𝑀)-action on the space of Kähler-type embeddings ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, this immediately yields the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.4 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S5.SS1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S5.SS1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{EK}_{M,\omega,{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">EK</mtext><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{EK}_{M,\omega,{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
is transitive if and only if
<math id="S5.SS1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.
<span id="S5.SS1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.5 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.SS1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta_{t},f_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta_{t},f_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, is a smooth family in <math id="S5.SS1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑃𝑎𝑖𝑟𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Then all <math id="S5.SS1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta_{t},f_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta_{t},f_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S5.SS1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S5.SS1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑃𝑎𝑖𝑟𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p21" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 The Symp(𝑀,𝜔)∩Diff_0⁢(𝑀)-action on the space of Kähler-type embeddings ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p22.1" class="ltx_p">The forms <math id="S5.SS1.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, form a smooth family of cohomologous symplectic forms. Hence, by Moser’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mos</a>]</cite>, all these forms can be identified by the <math id="S5.SS1.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-action with <math id="S5.SS1.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under such an identification,
<math id="S5.SS1.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes
a smooth family of symplectic embeddings
<math id="S5.SS1.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{t}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\eta_{0})\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{t}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\eta_{0})\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the symplectic isotopy extension result (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDS</a>, Thm. 3.3.1]</cite>), all such <math id="S5.SS1.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in the same <math id="S5.SS1.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\eta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\eta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit. This proves the proposition.
<span id="S5.SS1.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As before, for each <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
and each complex structure <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
denote the complex blow-up of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> we will use the same notation for the following objects (suppressing the dependence on <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">- The lift of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> will be denoted by <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This complex structure is
of Kähler-type (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voi</a>, Vol I, Prop. 3.24]</cite>). If <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is another complex structure on <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> coinciding with
<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> near each <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, then it also lifts to <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and its lift will be denoted by <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">- <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi:{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi:{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> will denote the natural projection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">- <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}:=\Pi^{-1}(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}:=\Pi^{-1}(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, is an exceptional divisor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">- The cohomology classes Poincaré-dual to the fundamental homology classes of <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>,
will be denoted by <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\ldots,e_{k}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\ldots,e_{k}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> the groups <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> can be canonically identified (preserving the numbering of the classes <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\ldots,e_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\ldots,e_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>) and we will use these identifications without further mention.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.6 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the set of triples <math id="S5.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}})\in{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}})\in{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that</p>
<table id="S5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\cdot\left({\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)\cap{\mathcal{C}}_{0}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\cdot\left({\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)\cap{\mathcal{C}}_{0}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and
the cohomology class <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}h_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}h_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>
is a holomorphic embedding into <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}(0)=x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}(0)=x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Set <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}:={\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}:={\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},h\}\in{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},h\}\in{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> the element of
<math id="S5.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> represented by <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)</annotation></semantics></math> – i.e., the <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S5.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> define</p>
<table id="S5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\kappa}:{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n},\ {\mathcal{R}}_{\kappa}(x):=\kappa x." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\kappa}:{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n},\ {\mathcal{R}}_{\kappa}(x):=\kappa x.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> is based on the following key propositions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.7 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p16.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a continuous surjective map <math id="S5.SS2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\to{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\to{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>
with the following properties:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p17" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p17.1" class="ltx_p">1. For each <math id="S5.SS2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi\left(\left\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},h\right\}\right)=\left\{\eta,h\circ R_{\kappa}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi\left(\left\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},h\right\}\right)=\left\{\eta,h\circ R_{\kappa}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p17.2" class="ltx_p">for a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S5.SS2.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and cohomologous to <math id="S5.SS2.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and for a sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS2.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\kappa\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\kappa\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, so that
the embedding <math id="S5.SS2.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\circ R_{\kappa}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\circ R_{\kappa}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to M</annotation></semantics></math>
is symplectic with respect to <math id="S5.SS2.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> and holomorphic with respect to <math id="S5.SS2.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p18.1" class="ltx_p">2. If <math id="S5.SS2.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic form on <math id="S5.SS2.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with a complex structure <math id="S5.SS2.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and cohomologous to <math id="S5.SS2.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.SS2.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}f_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}f_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic
embedding into <math id="S5.SS2.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> holomorphic with respect to <math id="S5.SS2.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>,
then, for <math id="S5.SS2.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}:=f_{i}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}:=f_{i}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS2.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="S5.SS2.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},f)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},f)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi\left(\left\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},f\right\}\right)=\{\eta,f\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi\left(\left\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},f\right\}\right)=\{\eta,f\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p19" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.8 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p20.1" class="ltx_p">The space <math id="S5.SS2.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected
if only if the set <math id="S5.SS2.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) is connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p21.1" class="ltx_p">For the proof of <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> see Section <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>. The proof of <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> will be given in Section <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6 Connectedness of 𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜_𝒞_0 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Let us now prove
<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. For convenience, we restate it here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p23" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.9 (=<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p24.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS2.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed manifold equipped with a Kähler-type symplectic form <math id="S5.SS2.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p25" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p25.1" class="ltx_p">(I)
The set <math id="S5.SS2.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> admits a Kähler-type embedding into <math id="S5.SS2.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
for some <math id="S5.SS2.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and some <math id="S5.SS2.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the cohomology class <math id="S5.SS2.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>, is Kähler with respect to <math id="S5.SS2.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p26.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely, assume <math id="S5.SS2.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex structure on <math id="S5.SS2.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S5.SS2.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold. If <math id="S5.SS2.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (with the product complex structure), we also allow
<math id="S5.SS2.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\textrm{pt})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\textrm{pt})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p27.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following conditions are equivalent:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p28" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S5.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M\setminus\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M\setminus\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the cohomology class <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S5.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists an <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}=Id</annotation></semantics></math>, is an isotopy such that <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(\Sigma)=\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(\Sigma)=\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.I2.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p29" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p29.1" class="ltx_p">(II)
Let <math id="S5.SS2.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected component of <math id="S5.SS2.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume that
the set <math id="S5.SS2.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math> (or, equivalently, the set <math id="S5.SS2.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math>)
is connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p30.1" class="ltx_p">Then any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S5.SS2.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> favoring <math id="S5.SS2.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
lie in the same <math id="S5.SS2.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit. In particular, there exists <math id="S5.SS2.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> such that
both embeddings are of <math id="S5.SS2.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p31.1" class="ltx_p">If, in addition, <math id="S5.SS2.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on the set of connected components of <math id="S5.SS2.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S5.SS2.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, then
any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S5.SS2.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
lie in the same <math id="S5.SS2.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p32" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> (=<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>):</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p33.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove part (I) of <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p34.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS2.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected component of <math id="S5.SS2.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> containing the complex structure <math id="S5.SS2.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p35.1" class="ltx_p">Define the set <math id="S5.SS2.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\supset\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊃</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\supset\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> as follows: If <math id="S5.SS2.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex submanifold of <math id="S5.SS2.p35.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, set <math id="S5.SS2.p35.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S5.SS2.p35.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.SS2.p35.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\textrm{pt})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\textrm{pt})</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.SS2.p35.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be the union of
<math id="S5.SS2.p35.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and of the closure of an open neighborhood of <math id="S5.SS2.p35.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cap({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\textrm{pt})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cap({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\textrm{pt})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p36.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove <math id="S5.SS2.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\Longrightarrow 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\Longrightarrow 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that <math id="S5.SS2.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M\setminus\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M\setminus\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that the cohomology class <math id="S5.SS2.p36.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S5.SS2.p36.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose a holomorphic embedding <math id="S5.SS2.p36.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}h_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon)\to(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}h_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon)\to(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>,
for some <math id="S5.SS2.p36.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that <math id="S5.SS2.p36.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}(0)=x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}(0)=x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p36.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS2.p36.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Im}\,}h\cap Y=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Im}\,}h\cap Y=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S5.SS2.p36.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p37.1" class="ltx_p">By part 1 of <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>,
there exist a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and cohomologous to <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and a sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\kappa\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\kappa\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the embedding <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\circ R_{\kappa}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\circ R_{\kappa}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectic with respect to <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> and holomorphic with respect to <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Im}\,}(h\circ R_{\kappa})\cap Y=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Im}\,}(h\circ R_{\kappa})\cap Y=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. The linear family of 2-forms connecting <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is formed by cohomologous symplectic forms compatible with <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and, in particular, their restrictions to <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> are non-degenerate
(in the case <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.SS2.p37.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\textrm{pt})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\textrm{pt})</annotation></semantics></math>, the restrictions of the forms to both
<math id="S5.SS2.p37.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\textrm{pt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\textrm{pt}</annotation></semantics></math> are non-degenerate).
By a relative version of Moser’s theorem (see <a href="#S13.SS3" title="13.3 Relative version of Moser’s method ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.3</span></a>, parts II and III),
there exists an isotopy <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}=Id</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(\Sigma)=\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(\Sigma)=\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi_{1}^{-1})^{*}\omega=\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi_{1}^{-1})^{*}\omega=\eta</annotation></semantics></math> outside <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{-1}\circ h\circ R_{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{-1}\circ h\circ R_{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> is an embedding of <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\setminus\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\setminus\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>
which is symplectic with respect to <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and holomorphic with respect to the complex structure <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S5.SS2.p37.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p38.1" class="ltx_p">Now let us prove <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\Longrightarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\Longrightarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}f_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}f_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a Kähler-type embedding holomorphic with
respect to a complex structure <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J:=\phi_{1}^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J:=\phi_{1}^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math>, which is compatible with <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
is an isotopy such that <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}=Id</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(\Sigma)=\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(\Sigma)=\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}:=f_{i}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}:=f_{i}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, by property 2 of <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> listed in <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>,
<math id="S5.SS2.p38.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(J,{\mathbbm{x}},f)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(J,{\mathbbm{x}},f)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
meaning, in particular, that the cohomology class <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{J}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M\setminus\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M\setminus\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}(\Sigma)=\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}(\Sigma)=\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>,
we get that the cohomology class <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S5.SS2.p38.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p39.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof of part (I) of <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p40.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove part (II) of <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p41.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that the set <math id="S5.SS2.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is connected.
By <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, this means that
<math id="S5.SS2.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. Since, by <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>,
<math id="S5.SS2.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\to{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\to{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a surjective continuous map, we get that
<math id="S5.SS2.p41.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. Hence, <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 The Symp(𝑀,𝜔)∩Diff_0⁢(𝑀)-action on the space of Kähler-type embeddings ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>
implies that <math id="S5.SS2.p41.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on <math id="S5.SS2.p41.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{EK}_{M,\omega,{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">EK</mtext><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{EK}_{M,\omega,{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, by <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Kähler-type and tame embeddings ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, there exists <math id="S5.SS2.p41.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> such that
both embeddings are of <math id="S5.SS2.p41.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p42" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p42.1" class="ltx_p">Assume, in addition, that <math id="S5.SS2.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on the set of connected components of <math id="S5.SS2.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S5.SS2.p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then any element of <math id="S5.SS2.p42.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{EK}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">EK</mtext><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{EK}_{M,\omega}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
can be mapped by <math id="S5.SS2.p42.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> into an element of the space <math id="S5.SS2.p42.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textit{EK}_{M,\omega,{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">EK</mtext><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textit{EK}_{M,\omega,{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The transitivity of the <math id="S5.SS2.p42.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-action on the latter space yields that
any two Kähler-type embeddings
of <math id="S5.SS2.p42.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.SS2.p42.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
can be mapped into each other by an element of <math id="S5.SS2.p42.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p43.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof of part (II) of <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.
<span id="S5.SS2.p43.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Regularized maximum</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-form <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> on a complex manifold with a complex structure <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(v,Iv)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(v,Iv)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for any non-zero tangent vector <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of the manifold. If <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is another <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-form on the same complex manifold, we write <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta&gt;\theta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta&gt;\theta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta-\theta^{\prime}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta-\theta^{\prime}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dem</a>, Lemma 5.18]</cite> that
for any smooth functions <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1},Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1},Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on a complex manifold and any <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, one can define their regularized maximum <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\delta}\{Q_{1},Q_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\delta}\{Q_{1},Q_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is a smooth function on the same manifold, depending smoothly on <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and satisfying the following conditions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">1. <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\{Q_{1},Q_{2}\}\leq\max_{\delta}\{Q_{1},Q_{2}\}\leq\max\{Q_{1},Q_{2}\}+\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\{Q_{1},Q_{2}\}\leq\max_{\delta}\{Q_{1},Q_{2}\}\leq\max\{Q_{1},Q_{2}\}+\delta</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">2. <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\delta}\{Q_{1},Q_{2}\}(y)=Q_{j}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\delta}\{Q_{1},Q_{2}\}(y)=Q_{j}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}(y)+2\delta\leq Q_{j}(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}(y)+2\delta\leq Q_{j}(y)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">3. If <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϑ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta</annotation></semantics></math> is a
Hermitian form on the manifold and <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}Q_{i}&gt;-\vartheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϑ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}Q_{i}&gt;-\vartheta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S5.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}\max_{\delta}\{Q_{1},Q_{2}\}&gt;-\vartheta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>δ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ϑ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}\max_{\delta}\{Q_{1},Q_{2}\}&gt;-\vartheta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Blow-up – local constructions</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we recall some standard local constructions concerning the blow-up of <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> at the origin.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1},\ldots,z_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1},\ldots,z_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the complex coordinates on <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(z_{1},\ldots,z_{n})\in{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(z_{1},\ldots,z_{n})\in{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote</p>
<table id="S5.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|z|:=\sqrt{|z_{1}|^{2}+\ldots+|z_{n}|^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|z|:=\sqrt{|z_{1}|^{2}+\ldots+|z_{n}|^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">As before, let <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the standard symplectic form on <math id="S5.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\cong{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\cong{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>. It can be written as</p>
<table id="S5.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}=\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}|z|^{2}/2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}=\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}|z|^{2}/2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> be the Fubini-Study symplectic form on <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> normalized by <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}}\sigma=\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}}\sigma=\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\subset{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\subset{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the incidence relation, i.e. <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}:=\{(z,{\mathfrak{l}})\ |\ z\in{\mathfrak{l}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝔩</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔩</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}:=\{(z,{\mathfrak{l}})\ |\ z\in{\mathfrak{l}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is the complex blow-up of <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> at the origin. Let <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pr_{1}:{\mathcal{L}}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr_{1}:{\mathcal{L}}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pr_{2}:{\mathcal{L}}\to{\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr_{2}:{\mathcal{L}}\to{\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the natural projections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, denote <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}(r):=pr_{1}^{-1}(B^{2n}(r))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}(r):=pr_{1}^{-1}(B^{2n}(r))</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will also write <math id="S5.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}(0):=pr_{1}^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}(0):=pr_{1}^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, define a differential 2-form <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(c,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(c,r)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S5.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(c,r):=c^{2}pr_{1}^{*}\omega_{0}+r^{2}pr_{2}^{*}\sigma." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(c,r):=c^{2}pr_{1}^{*}\omega_{0}+r^{2}pr_{2}^{*}\sigma.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The restriction of <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pr_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\setminus pr_{1}^{-1}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\setminus pr_{1}^{-1}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> is a diffeomorphism <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\setminus pr_{1}^{-1}(0)\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\setminus pr_{1}^{-1}(0)\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0</annotation></semantics></math>. One can easily see that
it identifies the form <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(c,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(c,r)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\setminus{\mathcal{L}}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\setminus{\mathcal{L}}(0)</annotation></semantics></math> with the form <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0</annotation></semantics></math> that can be written as</p>
<table id="S5.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)=\frac{\sqrt{-1}}{2}\partial\bar{\partial}\left(c^{2}|z|^{2}+r^{2}\log|z|^{2}+d\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)=\frac{\sqrt{-1}}{2}\partial\bar{\partial}\left(c^{2}|z|^{2}+r^{2}\log|z|^{2}+d\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS4.p7.2" class="ltx_p">for any <math id="S5.SS4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The following two propositions and their proofs describe local constructions needed further on to define symplectic blow-up and blow-down operations. Here we use the regularized maximum for these constructions – cf. e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDS</a>]</cite>,
where different tools were used.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.10 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Then for any sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a Kähler form <math id="S5.SS4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0</annotation></semantics></math>, depending smoothly on <math id="S5.SS4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, so that</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho^{c}=\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho^{c}=\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on a neighborhood of <math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus Int\,B^{2n}(r+\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus Int\,B^{2n}(r+\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho^{c}=(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho^{c}=(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)</annotation></semantics></math> on a neighborhood of <math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(r)\setminus 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(r)\setminus 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Blow-up – local constructions ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The functions <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{2}-r^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}-r^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{2}\log\left((s/r)^{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{2}\log\left((s/r)^{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> (as functions of <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>) coincide with their first derivatives at <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=r</annotation></semantics></math>. The first function is convex while the second one is concave and <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{2}-r^{2}&gt;r^{2}\log\left((s/r)^{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{2}-r^{2}&gt;r^{2}\log\left((s/r)^{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\neq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\neq r</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore for any sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and a sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (depending on <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>) the functions <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(s):=s^{2}-r^{2}-\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(s):=s^{2}-r^{2}-\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(s):=cs^{2}+r^{2}\log s^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(s):=cs^{2}+r^{2}\log s^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> have exactly two points
<math id="S5.SS4.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1},s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1},s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;s_{1}&lt;r&lt;s_{2}&lt;r+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;s_{1}&lt;r&lt;s_{2}&lt;r+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, where they coincide, so that <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(s)&lt;G(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(s)&lt;G(s)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[s_{1},r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[s_{1},r)</annotation></semantics></math>, while
<math id="S5.SS4.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(s)&gt;G(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(s)&gt;G(s)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p14.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(s_{2},r+\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{2},r+\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Choose <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(r)&lt;G(r)-2\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(r)&lt;G(r)-2\delta</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.SS4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(r+\epsilon)&gt;G(r+\epsilon)+2\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(r+\epsilon)&gt;G(r+\epsilon)+2\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="S5.SS4.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.SS4.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(z):=\max_{\delta}\{F(|z^{2}|),G(|z^{2}|)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(z):=\max_{\delta}\{F(|z^{2}|),G(|z^{2}|)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS4.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> is the regularized maximum mentioned in Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Regularized maximum ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>.
It follows from (<a href="#S5.E1" title="In 5.4 Blow-up – local constructions ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>)
and (<a href="#S5.E2" title="In 5.4 Blow-up – local constructions ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>) and the properties of the regularized maximum (see Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Regularized maximum ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>) that
<math id="S5.SS4.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}H</annotation></semantics></math> is a Kähler form on <math id="S5.SS4.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0</annotation></semantics></math> equal to
<math id="S5.SS4.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}F=\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}F=\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on a neighborhood <math id="S5.SS4.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus Int\,B^{2n}(r+\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus Int\,B^{2n}(r+\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and to <math id="S5.SS4.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}G=(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}G=(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)</annotation></semantics></math> on a neighborhood of the sphere <math id="S5.SS4.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Define the form <math id="S5.SS4.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S5.SS4.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(r)\setminus 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(r)\setminus 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS4.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}G</annotation></semantics></math> on the neighborhood of <math id="S5.SS4.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}H</annotation></semantics></math> outside
<math id="S5.SS4.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>. It is a well-defined form. The parameters <math id="S5.SS4.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma,s_{1},s_{2},\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma,s_{1},s_{2},\delta</annotation></semantics></math> in the construction can be chosen to depend smoothly on <math id="S5.SS4.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>
and thus <math id="S5.SS4.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> can be assumed to depend smoothly on <math id="S5.SS4.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>.
One easily sees that <math id="S5.SS4.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the bulleted properties listed in the proposition.
This finishes the proof.
<span id="S5.SS4.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.11 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS4.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Then for any <math id="S5.SS4.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a Kähler form <math id="S5.SS4.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, depending smoothly on <math id="S5.SS4.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, so that</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p21" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho_{c}=\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho_{c}=\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on a neighborhood of <math id="S5.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho_{c}=(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho_{c}=(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)</annotation></semantics></math> on a neighborhood of <math id="S5.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus Int\,B^{2n}(r+\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus Int\,B^{2n}(r+\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p22" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Blow-up – local constructions ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p23.1" class="ltx_p">For an appropriate constant <math id="S5.SS4.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> the functions <math id="S5.SS4.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(s):=s^{2}+d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(s):=s^{2}+d</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(s):=c^{2}s^{2}+r^{2}\log\left((s/r)^{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(s):=c^{2}s^{2}+r^{2}\log\left((s/r)^{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> (as functions of <math id="S5.SS4.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>) are equal at some point <math id="S5.SS4.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}\in(r,r+\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}\in(r,r+\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S5.SS4.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(s)&gt;G(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(s)&gt;G(s)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[r,s_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[r,s_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS4.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(s)&lt;G(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(s)&lt;G(s)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{1},s_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{1},s_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S5.SS4.p23.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}\in(s_{1},r+\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}\in(s_{1},r+\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p24.1" class="ltx_p">Having chosen such <math id="S5.SS4.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1},s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1},s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
pick <math id="S5.SS4.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S5.SS4.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(r)&gt;G(r)+2\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(r)&gt;G(r)+2\delta</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.SS4.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(s_{2})&lt;G(s_{2})-2\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(s_{2})&lt;G(s_{2})-2\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p25.1" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="S5.SS4.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.SS4.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(z):=\max_{\delta}\{F(|z^{2}|),G(|z^{2}|)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(z):=\max_{\delta}\{F(|z^{2}|),G(|z^{2}|)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS4.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> is the regularized maximum mentioned in Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Regularized maximum ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>.
It follows from (<a href="#S5.E1" title="In 5.4 Blow-up – local constructions ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>)
and (<a href="#S5.E2" title="In 5.4 Blow-up – local constructions ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>) and the properties of the regularized maximum (see
Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Regularized maximum ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>) that <math id="S5.SS4.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}H</annotation></semantics></math> is a Kähler form on <math id="S5.SS4.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus 0</annotation></semantics></math> equal to <math id="S5.SS4.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}F=\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}F=\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S5.SS4.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(r+\sigma)\setminus Int\,B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(r+\sigma)\setminus Int\,B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math> for some sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS4.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and to
<math id="S5.SS4.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}G=(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}G=(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)</annotation></semantics></math> on a neighborhood of <math id="S5.SS4.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial B^{2n}(s_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial B^{2n}(s_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p26.1" class="ltx_p">Define a form <math id="S5.SS4.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S5.SS4.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>, as <math id="S5.SS4.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}H</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(s_{2})\setminus B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(s_{2})\setminus B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math> and as <math id="S5.SS4.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}G=(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sqrt{-1}/2)\cdot\partial\bar{\partial}G=(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(c,r)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p26.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus B^{2n}(s_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\setminus B^{2n}(s_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is a well-defined smooth form. The parameters <math id="S5.SS4.p26.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d,s_{1},s_{2},\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d,s_{1},s_{2},\delta</annotation></semantics></math> in the construction can be chosen to depend smoothly on <math id="S5.SS4.p26.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>
and thus <math id="S5.SS4.p26.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> can be assumed to depend smoothly on <math id="S5.SS4.p26.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>.
One easily sees that <math id="S5.SS4.p26.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϱ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the bulleted properties listed in the proposition.
This finishes the proof.
<span id="S5.SS4.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p27" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.12 (<math id="S5.SS4.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial\bar{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial\bar{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-lemma for a complex blow-up of a ball)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p28.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS4.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a ball centered at the origin <math id="S5.SS4.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.SS4.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the complex blow-up of <math id="S5.SS4.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.SS4.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p29.1" class="ltx_p">Then any exact <math id="S5.SS4.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-form <math id="S5.SS4.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\upsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>υ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upsilon</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as <math id="S5.SS4.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\upsilon=\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>υ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upsilon=\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}Q</annotation></semantics></math> for a smooth real-valued function <math id="S5.SS4.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p30" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Blow-up – local constructions ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p31.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS4.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:{\widetilde{B}}\to B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:{\widetilde{B}}\to B</annotation></semantics></math> be the natural projection. Let <math id="S5.SS4.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}_{\widetilde{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}_{\widetilde{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the structure sheaf (i.e., the sheaf of germs of holomorphic functions) on <math id="S5.SS4.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p32.1" class="ltx_p">Note that
<math id="S5.SS4.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{i}\pi_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{\widetilde{B}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}\pi_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{\widetilde{B}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS4.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>
(see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ue</a>, Prop. 2.14]</cite>,
cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hi</a>, (2), p.144]</cite>).
Consequently, <math id="S5.SS4.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}({\widetilde{B}},{\mathcal{O}}_{\widetilde{B}})=H^{1}(B,\pi_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{\widetilde{B}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}({\widetilde{B}},{\mathcal{O}}_{\widetilde{B}})=H^{1}(B,\pi_{*}{\mathcal{O}}_{\widetilde{B}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p33.1" class="ltx_p">By the Dolbeault theorem, this implies that any <math id="S5.SS4.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-closed <math id="S5.SS4.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-form on <math id="S5.SS4.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS4.p33.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-exact. The latter fact yields, by the complex conjugation symmetry, that any <math id="S5.SS4.p33.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-closed <math id="S5.SS4.p33.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-form on <math id="S5.SS4.p33.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS4.p33.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-exact.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p34.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\upsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>υ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is exact, it can be written as <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\upsilon=d\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>υ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upsilon=d\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> for some 1-form <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Write <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> as a sum of its <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-parts: <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=\sigma^{1,0}+\sigma^{0,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=\sigma^{1,0}+\sigma^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the 1-form <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{0,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{0,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-closed and hence <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math>-exact, meaning that <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{0,1}=\bar{\partial}S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{0,1}=\bar{\partial}S</annotation></semantics></math> for a smooth function <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>. One readily checks that the form <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma-dS" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma-dS</annotation></semantics></math> is of type <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-closed, hence <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math>-exact,
meaning that <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma-dS=\partial(-\sqrt{-1}Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma-dS=\partial(-\sqrt{-1}Q)</annotation></semantics></math> for a smooth function <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>B</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, a direct computation yields
<math id="S5.SS4.p34.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\upsilon=\bar{\partial}\partial(-\sqrt{-1}Q)=\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>υ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upsilon=\bar{\partial}\partial(-\sqrt{-1}Q)=\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}Q</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the form <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\upsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>υ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is real, the function <math id="S5.SS4.p34.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> can be also chosen to be real-valued. This finishes the proof of the lemma.
<span id="S5.SS4.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of <math id="S5.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex structure on <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fix an
<math id="S5.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic embedding <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}h_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}h_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to M</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}(0)=x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}(0)=x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> extends to a holomorphic embedding
<math id="S5.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}h_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon)\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}h_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon)\to M</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each map <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}:B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon)\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}:B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon)\to M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>,
lifts to the unique map
<math id="S5.SS5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{h}}_{i}:{\mathcal{L}}(r_{i}+\epsilon)\to{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{h}}_{i}:{\mathcal{L}}(r_{i}+\epsilon)\to{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S5.SS5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\circ pr_{1}=\Pi\circ{\widetilde{h}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\circ pr_{1}=\Pi\circ{\widetilde{h}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pr_{1}:{\mathcal{L}}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr_{1}:{\mathcal{L}}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection defined in Section <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Blow-up – local constructions ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>.
For any <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> the map <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{h}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{h}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, identifies the form <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(c,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(c,r)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}(r_{i}+\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}(r_{i}+\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> with a form on a neighborhood of the exceptional divisor <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}\subset{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}\subset{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> that will be denoted by <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(c,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(c,r)</annotation></semantics></math>. Each <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(c,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(c,r)</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic form compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a cohomology class <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>, define the cohomology class <math id="S5.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S5.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}:=\Pi^{*}\alpha-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}:=\Pi^{*}\alpha-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.13 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume that the cohomology class <math id="S5.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is Kähler with respect to <math id="S5.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Then there exists a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>, represents <math id="S5.SS5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and equals to
<math id="S5.SS5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> on a neighborhood of <math id="S5.SS5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S5.SS5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.14</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">If one drops the requirement that <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the existence of a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\widetilde{\theta}}]={\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\widetilde{\theta}}]={\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>, coinciding with <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> on a neighborhood of each <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be proved much easier using Moser’s method, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>, Pf. of Prop. 2.1.C]</cite>. The need to make <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> comes from the fact that otherwise the blow-down construction (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>, Sec. 5]</cite>) applied to <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> will produce only a symplectic, but not necessarily a holomorphic, embedding of balls. Unlike Moser’s method – which can be applied, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>, Pf. of Prop. 2.1.C]</cite>, also when <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is not necessarily integrable outside neighborhoods of the base points <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> – here we do rely on the integrability of <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on the whole <math id="S5.SS5.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, as we use methods of complex geometry.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The argument below uses a result
based on an idea of Demailly and
published in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx74" title="" class="ltx_ref">OV</a>, Thm. 4.1]</cite> and, moreover, mimics the idea of its proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Since the cohomology class <math id="S5.SS5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is Kähler, we can pick a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\eta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\eta}}</annotation></semantics></math> that represents it and that is compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p12.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S5.SS5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math> denote:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{x_{i}}:=h\left(B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{x_{i}}:=h\left(B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}_{x_{i}}:=h\left(B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon/2)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}_{x_{i}}:=h\left(B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon/2)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}:={\widetilde{h}}_{i}\left({\mathcal{L}}(r_{i}+\epsilon)\right)=\Pi^{-1}(U_{x_{i}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}:={\widetilde{h}}_{i}\left({\mathcal{L}}(r_{i}+\epsilon)\right)=\Pi^{-1}(U_{x_{i}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}:={\widetilde{h}}_{i}\left({\mathcal{L}}(r_{i})\right)=\Pi^{-1}(U^{\prime}_{x_{i}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}:={\widetilde{h}}_{i}\left({\mathcal{L}}(r_{i})\right)=\Pi^{-1}(U^{\prime}_{x_{i}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p12.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, <math id="S5.SS5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}_{x_{i}}\subset U_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}_{x_{i}}\subset U_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}\subset{\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}\subset{\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS5.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS5.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E:=\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E:=\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the union of the exceptional divisors of <math id="S5.SS5.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S5.SS5.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex submanifold of <math id="S5.SS5.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}},{\widetilde{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}},{\widetilde{I}})</annotation></semantics></math>, by a result of Demailly-Paun <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP</a>, Lem. 2.1]</cite>, there exists a smooth real-valued function <math id="S5.SS5.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\setminus E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\setminus E</annotation></semantics></math> so that</p>
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}G&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}G&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p14.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S5.SS5.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has logarithmic poles along <math id="S5.SS5.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p15.1" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="S5.SS5.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is biholomorphic to the blow-up of the ball at the origin and <math id="S5.SS5.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})-{\widetilde{\eta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})-{\widetilde{\eta}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is an exact <math id="S5.SS5.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-form on <math id="S5.SS5.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, by <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Blow-up – local constructions ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>,</p>
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})-{\widetilde{\eta}}|_{{\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}}=\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}F_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})-{\widetilde{\eta}}|_{{\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}}=\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p15.2" class="ltx_p">for
a smooth function <math id="S5.SS5.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}:{\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}:{\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Adding, if necessary, a constant to <math id="S5.SS5.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we may assume
without loss of generality that</p>
<table id="S5.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F_{i}&lt;G-2\delta\ \textrm{on an open neigborhood of}\ {\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}\setminus{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}},i=1,\ldots,k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>δ</mi><mtext>on an open neigborhood of</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>∖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}&lt;G-2\delta\ \textrm{on an open neigborhood of}\ {\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}\setminus{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}},i=1,\ldots,k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Since
the function <math id="S5.SS5.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> tends to <math id="S5.SS5.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> as its argument converges to <math id="S5.SS5.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="S5.SS5.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>
there exists a neighborhood <math id="S5.SS5.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{x_{i}}\subset U^{\prime}_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{x_{i}}\subset U^{\prime}_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS5.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose preimage <math id="S5.SS5.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{-1}(V_{x_{i}})\subset{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{-1}(V_{x_{i}})\subset{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> will be denoted
by <math id="S5.SS5.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}:=\Pi^{-1}(V_{x_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}:=\Pi^{-1}(V_{x_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math>, so that</p>
<table id="S5.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="G+2\delta&lt;F_{i}\ \textrm{on}\ {\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}},\ i=1,\ldots,k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>on</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G+2\delta&lt;F_{i}\ \textrm{on}\ {\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}},\ i=1,\ldots,k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Define the function <math id="S5.SS5.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows</p>
<table id="S5.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:=\max_{\delta}\{F_{i},G\},\ \textrm{on}\ {\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}},i=1,\ldots,k," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>δ</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>on</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:=\max_{\delta}\{F_{i},G\},\ \textrm{on}\ {\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}},i=1,\ldots,k,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K:=G,\ \textrm{on}\ {\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\setminus\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>:=</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>on</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K:=G,\ \textrm{on}\ {\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\setminus\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p17.2" class="ltx_p">By (<a href="#S5.E6" title="In 5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>), (<a href="#S5.E7" title="In 5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>)
and the properties of the regularized maximum (see Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Regularized maximum ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>),
<math id="S5.SS5.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is a well-defined smooth function on <math id="S5.SS5.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, equal to
<math id="S5.SS5.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, to <math id="S5.SS5.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\setminus\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\setminus\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and to
<math id="S5.SS5.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\delta}\{F_{i},G\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\delta}\{F_{i},G\}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}\setminus{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}\setminus{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Define the smooth 2-form <math id="S5.SS5.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}:={\widetilde{\eta}}+\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}K." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}:={\widetilde{\eta}}+\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}K.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p18.2" class="ltx_p">The form <math id="S5.SS5.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is of type <math id="S5.SS5.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S5.SS5.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let us verify that <math id="S5.SS5.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive – i.e. symplectic. We need to verify
that</p>
<table id="S5.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}K&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}}\ \textrm{everywhere on}\ {\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>everywhere on</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}K&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}}\ \textrm{everywhere on}\ {\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p18.3" class="ltx_p">Let us verify the latter inequality case by case
for the points of <math id="S5.SS5.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}\setminus{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}\setminus{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS5.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\setminus\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\setminus\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Consider first the case of <math id="S5.SS5.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>. By
(<a href="#S5.E5" title="In 5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>), and since <math id="S5.SS5.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S5.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}F_{i}=-{\widetilde{\eta}}+\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}},\ \textrm{on}\ {\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}},i=1,\ldots,k," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>on</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}F_{i}=-{\widetilde{\eta}}+\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}},\ \textrm{on}\ {\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}},i=1,\ldots,k,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p19.2" class="ltx_p">Hence, for the points of <math id="S5.SS5.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}\subset{\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}\subset{\widetilde{U}}_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}K=\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}F_{i}&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}K=\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}F_{i}&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p19.3" class="ltx_p">meaning that the inequality (<a href="#S5.E8" title="In 5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>) is satisfied in this case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p20.1" class="ltx_p">For the points of <math id="S5.SS5.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}\setminus{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}\setminus{\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S5.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}F_{i}&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}F_{i}&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p20.2" class="ltx_p">by (<a href="#S5.E9" title="In 5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.9</span></a>), and</p>
<table id="S5.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}G&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}G&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p20.3" class="ltx_p">by (<a href="#S5.E4" title="In 5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>).
Hence, for such points</p>
<table id="S5.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}K=\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}\max_{\delta}\{F_{i},G\}&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>δ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}K=\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}\max_{\delta}\{F_{i},G\}&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p20.4" class="ltx_p">by the third property of the regularized maximum (see Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Regularized maximum ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>).
Thus, the inequality (<a href="#S5.E8" title="In 5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>) is satisfied in this case too.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p21.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, for the points of <math id="S5.SS5.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\setminus\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\setminus\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}{\widetilde{U}}^{\prime}_{x_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}G&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}G&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p21.2" class="ltx_p">by (<a href="#S5.E4" title="In 5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>).
Hence,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}K=\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}G&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}K=\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}G&gt;-{\widetilde{\eta}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p21.3" class="ltx_p">meaning that the inequality (<a href="#S5.E8" title="In 5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>) is satisfied also in this case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p22.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the verification of (<a href="#S5.E8" title="In 5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p23.1" class="ltx_p">We have shown that <math id="S5.SS5.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive <math id="S5.SS5.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-form with respect to <math id="S5.SS5.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which means that it is compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is cohomologous to <math id="S5.SS5.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\eta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\eta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that it represents the cohomology class <math id="S5.SS5.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By
(<a href="#S5.E5" title="In 5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>),</p>
<table id="S5.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}={\widetilde{\eta}}+\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}K={\widetilde{\eta}}+\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}F_{i}=\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})\ \textrm{on}\ {\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}},i=1,\ldots,k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>η</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mover accent="true"><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mo>¯</mo></mover><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>on</mtext><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}={\widetilde{\eta}}+\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}K={\widetilde{\eta}}+\sqrt{-1}\partial\bar{\partial}F_{i}=\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})\ \textrm{on}\ {\widetilde{V}}_{x_{i}},i=1,\ldots,k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p23.2" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof
of the proposition.
<span id="S5.SS5.p23.2.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p24.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that for each <math id="S5.SS5.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we define</p>
<table id="S5.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\kappa}:{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n},\ R_{\kappa}(x):=\kappa x." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\kappa}:{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n},\ R_{\kappa}(x):=\kappa x.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p24.2" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S5.SS5.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a diffeomorphism of <math id="S5.SS5.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}\subset{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}\subset{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> that will be denoted by <math id="S5.SS5.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{R}}_{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>κ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{R}}_{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{R}}_{\kappa}:{\mathcal{L}}\to{\mathcal{L}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>κ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{R}}_{\kappa}:{\mathcal{L}}\to{\mathcal{L}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p24.3" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.SS5.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by <math id="S5.SS5.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\circ R_{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\circ R_{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> the holomorphic embedding</p>
<table id="S5.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle h\circ R_{\kappa}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}h_{i}\circ R_{\kappa}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,I)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mstyle><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle h\circ R_{\kappa}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}h_{i}\circ R_{\kappa}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,I).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p24.4" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S5.SS5.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="S5.SS5.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\kappa\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\kappa\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> denote</p>
<table id="S5.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{x_{i},\kappa}:=h_{i}\circ R_{\kappa}\left(B^{2n}\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)=h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{x_{i},\kappa}:=h_{i}\circ R_{\kappa}\left(B^{2n}\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)=h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p25.1" class="ltx_p">The following proposition describes the exact version of the symplectic blow-down operation
that we need for our purposes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p26" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.15 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p27.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS5.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.SS5.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\kappa\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\kappa\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1},\ldots,c_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1},\ldots,c_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p28.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that the cohomology class <math id="S5.SS5.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by
a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS5.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with
<math id="S5.SS5.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(c_{i},r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(c_{i},r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> on the neighborhood <math id="S5.SS5.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{-1}(U_{x_{i},\kappa})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{-1}(U_{x_{i},\kappa})</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S5.SS5.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p29.1" class="ltx_p">Then there exists a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS5.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> with the
following properties:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p30" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}]=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}]=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>; if <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex structure
on <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> coinciding with <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}U_{x_{i},\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}U_{x_{i},\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math>
and such that <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> tames <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> tames <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The embedding <math id="S5.I5.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\circ R_{\kappa}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\circ R_{\kappa}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectic with respect to <math id="S5.I5.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> (and holomorphic with respect to <math id="S5.I5.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I5.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> depends smoothly on <math id="S5.I5.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p31" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p32.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.SS5.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently small so that <math id="S5.SS5.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}=\rho_{x_{i}}(c_{i},r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}=\rho_{x_{i}}(c_{i},r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{-1}(U_{x_{i},\kappa})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{-1}(U_{x_{i},\kappa})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS5.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.SS5.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{R}}_{\kappa}^{*}\circ{\widetilde{h}}_{i}^{*}{\widetilde{\theta}}=\rho(\kappa c_{i},r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>κ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{R}}_{\kappa}^{*}\circ{\widetilde{h}}_{i}^{*}{\widetilde{\theta}}=\rho(\kappa c_{i},r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p32.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}(r_{i}+\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}(r_{i}+\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS5.p32.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p33.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S5.SS5.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math> we can use <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Blow-up – local constructions ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> in order to construct a form <math id="S5.SS5.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varrho_{\kappa c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϱ</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varrho_{\kappa c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, depending smoothly on <math id="S5.SS5.p33.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, that equals <math id="S5.SS5.p33.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on a neighborhood of <math id="S5.SS5.p33.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S5.SS5.p33.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(\kappa c_{i},r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(pr_{1})_{*}\rho(\kappa c_{i},r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> on a neighborhood of <math id="S5.SS5.p33.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the form
<math id="S5.SS5.p33.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h_{i})_{*}\varrho_{\kappa c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϱ</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h_{i})_{*}\varrho_{\kappa c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined on <math id="S5.SS5.p33.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{x_{i},\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{x_{i},\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math>
and coincides with <math id="S5.SS5.p33.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{*}{\widetilde{\theta}}=\Pi_{*}\left(\rho_{x_{i}}(\kappa c_{i},r_{i})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{*}{\widetilde{\theta}}=\Pi_{*}\left(\rho_{x_{i}}(\kappa c_{i},r_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math> near <math id="S5.SS5.p33.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial U_{x_{i},\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial U_{x_{i},\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p34.1" class="ltx_p">Consequently, we can define a 2-form <math id="S5.SS5.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> by setting it equal to
<math id="S5.SS5.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{*}{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{*}{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> outside <math id="S5.SS5.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}U_{x_{i},\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}U_{x_{i},\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> and to <math id="S5.SS5.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h_{i})_{*}\varrho_{\kappa c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϱ</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h_{i})_{*}\varrho_{\kappa c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> on each
<math id="S5.SS5.p34.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{x_{i},\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{x_{i},\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p34.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.
One easily checks that <math id="S5.SS5.p34.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectic and that the bulleted properties in the proposition are satisfied.
<span id="S5.SS5.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p35.1" class="ltx_p">The following proposition is a version of the symplectic blow-up construction – cf. e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDS</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p36" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p36.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p36.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.16 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p37.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S5.SS5.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic form on <math id="S5.SS5.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that the embedding <math id="S5.SS5.p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectic with respect to <math id="S5.SS5.p37.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.p37.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p37.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on the image of <math id="S5.SS5.p37.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p38.1" class="ltx_p">Then for any sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS5.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS5.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p38.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the following properties:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p39" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p39.1" class="ltx_p">1. <math id="S5.SS5.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}=\rho_{x_{i}}(c,r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}=\rho_{x_{i}}(c,r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{-1}\left(h_{i}\left(B(r_{i})\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{-1}\left(h_{i}\left(B(r_{i})\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p40" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p40.1" class="ltx_p">2. <math id="S5.SS5.p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{*}{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}=\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{*}{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}=\eta</annotation></semantics></math> outside <math id="S5.SS5.p40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}h\left(B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}h\left(B^{2n}(r_{i}+\epsilon)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p41" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p41.1" class="ltx_p">3. <math id="S5.SS5.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> depends smoothly on <math id="S5.SS5.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p42" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p42.1" class="ltx_p">4. If <math id="S5.SS5.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with, respectively tames, <math id="S5.SS5.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on the whole <math id="S5.SS5.p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.SS5.p42.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with,
respectively tames, <math id="S5.SS5.p42.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> on the whole <math id="S5.SS5.p42.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p43" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p43.1" class="ltx_p">5. <math id="S5.SS5.p43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}]=\Pi^{*}[\eta]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}]=\Pi^{*}[\eta]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p44" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p44.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p44.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p45.1" class="ltx_p">Using <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Blow-up – local constructions ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a> for each <math id="S5.SS5.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>,
for any sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS5.p45.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
we can construct a form <math id="S5.SS5.p45.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p45.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with properties 1-4.
Properties 1 and 2 imply that <math id="S5.SS5.p45.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic form satisfying also property 5.
This finishes the proof.
<span id="S5.SS5.p45.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p46" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p46.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p46.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p47.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS5.p47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:=[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:=[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p48.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a triple <math id="S5.SS5.p48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS5.p48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>. By <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>,
the cohomology class <math id="S5.SS5.p48.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is Kähler with respect to <math id="S5.SS5.p48.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, by <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>,
there exists a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS5.p48.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p48.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p48.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>, represents <math id="S5.SS5.p48.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and equals to
<math id="S5.SS5.p48.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> on a neighborhood of <math id="S5.SS5.p48.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S5.SS5.p48.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> implies that
for any sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS5.p48.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS5.p48.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p48.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> with the
following properties:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p49" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}]=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}]=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S5.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I6.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\circ R_{\kappa}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\circ R_{\kappa}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectic with respect to <math id="S5.I6.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> (and holomorphic with respect to <math id="S5.I6.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>),</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I6.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I6.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I6.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I6.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> depends smoothly on <math id="S5.I6.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p50" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p50.1" class="ltx_p">By <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, there exists <math id="S5.SS5.p50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.SS5.p50.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p50.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S5.SS5.p50.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.p50.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> are cohomologous symplectic forms compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p50.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math>. The linear family of 2-forms
connecting <math id="S5.SS5.p50.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.p50.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is then a family of cohomologous symplectic forms.
Hence, by Moser’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mos</a>]</cite>, <math id="S5.SS5.p50.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the <math id="S5.SS5.p50.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of <math id="S5.SS5.p50.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, and consequently so does <math id="S5.SS5.p50.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, for any sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS5.p50.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S5.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa})\in{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑃𝑎𝑖𝑟𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa})\in{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p51" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p51.1" class="ltx_p">Define the map</p>
<table id="S5.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\to{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\to{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p51.2" class="ltx_p">by</p>
<table id="S5.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi\left(\left\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},h\right\}\right):=\left\{\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi\left(\left\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},h\right\}\right):=\left\{\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p51.3" class="ltx_p">We need to show that the map <math id="S5.SS5.p51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined and has the required properties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p52" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p52.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.SS5.p52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS5.p52.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted"> is well-defined:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p53" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p53.1" class="ltx_p">Let us first show that <math id="S5.SS5.p53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>
does not depend on the choice of a sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS5.p53.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (for fixed <math id="S5.SS5.p53.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>). Given such <math id="S5.SS5.p53.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\kappa_{1}&lt;\kappa_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\kappa_{1}&lt;\kappa_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have a smooth family of pairs <math id="S5.SS5.p53.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p53.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\in[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\in[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>, lying in <math id="S5.SS5.p53.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑃𝑎𝑖𝑟𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 The Symp(𝑀,𝜔)∩Diff_0⁢(𝑀)-action on the space of Kähler-type embeddings ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, this family lies in the same
<math id="S5.SS5.p53.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit on <math id="S5.SS5.p53.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑃𝑎𝑖𝑟𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S5.SS5.p53.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on
the choice of <math id="S5.SS5.p53.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p54" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p54.1" class="ltx_p">Let us verify that <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>
does not depend on the choice of <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is another symplectic form
which is compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>, represents <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and equals
<math id="S5.SS5.p54.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> on a neighborhood of <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Pick a sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}={\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}=\rho_{x_{i}}(c_{i},r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}={\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}=\rho_{x_{i}}(c_{i},r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{-1}\left(h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{-1}\left(h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> are cohomologous symplectic forms compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and
coinciding on <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. By a relative version of Moser’s theorem (see <a href="#S13.SS3" title="13.3 Relative version of Moser’s method ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.3</span></a>, part I), there exists <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> that fixes <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math> pointwise (thus mapping <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\circ R_{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\circ R_{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> to itself) and maps <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S5.SS5.p54.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa}\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend
on the choice of <math id="S5.SS5.p54.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p55" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p55.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, a direct check shows that <math id="S5.SS5.p55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi\left(\left\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},h\right\}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi\left(\left\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},h\right\}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on a representative
<math id="S5.SS5.p55.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS5.p55.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},h\right\}\in{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},h\right\}\in{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>: acting by <math id="S5.SS5.p55.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S5.SS5.p55.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)</annotation></semantics></math> and applying the previous construction to the resulting element of <math id="S5.SS5.p55.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> we get a pair in <math id="S5.SS5.p55.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑃𝑎𝑖𝑟𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from <math id="S5.SS5.p55.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
by the action of the same <math id="S5.SS5.p55.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. (Here we identify the complex blow-ups of <math id="S5.SS5.p55.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.SS5.p55.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
and of <math id="S5.SS5.p55.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\phi_{*}I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\phi_{*}I)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.SS5.p55.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x_{1}),\ldots,\phi(x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x_{1}),\ldots,\phi(x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> by the diffeomorphism induced by <math id="S5.SS5.p55.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p56" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p56.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, we have shown that <math id="S5.SS5.p56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p57" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p57.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.SS5.p57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS5.p57.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted"> is continuous:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p58" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p58.1" class="ltx_p">By <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 The Symp(𝑀,𝜔)∩Diff_0⁢(𝑀)-action on the space of Kähler-type embeddings ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, the space <math id="S5.SS5.p58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is discrete. Thus, showing that
<math id="S5.SS5.p58.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi:{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\to{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\to{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous is equivalent to showing that the preimage of a point in <math id="S5.SS5.p58.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>
under <math id="S5.SS5.p58.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is open in <math id="S5.SS5.p58.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, or, in other words, that for any <math id="S5.SS5.p58.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime},h^{\prime})\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime},h^{\prime})\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently close to
<math id="S5.SS5.p58.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)</annotation></semantics></math> its <math id="S5.SS5.p58.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit <math id="S5.SS5.p58.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime},h^{\prime})\}\in{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime},h^{\prime})\}\in{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped by <math id="S5.SS5.p58.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> to the same point in <math id="S5.SS5.p58.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S5.SS5.p58.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p59" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p59.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S5.SS5.p59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime},h^{\prime})\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime},h^{\prime})\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, one easily constructs <math id="S5.SS5.p59.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> so that</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p60" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I7" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I7.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> sends <math id="S5.I7.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.I7.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I7.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\circ h^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\circ h^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with <math id="S5.I7.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> near each <math id="S5.I7.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.I7.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p61" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p61.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, are sufficiently <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close, then <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> can be chosen to be close to the identity and therefore it will send
<math id="S5.SS5.p61.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime},h^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime},h^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> into a triple close to <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, without loss of generality we may replace
<math id="S5.SS5.p61.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime},h^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime},h^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> by a triple <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}},h^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}},h^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> that is close to <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)</annotation></semantics></math> and satisfies <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\circ R_{\kappa}=h^{\prime}\circ R_{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\circ R_{\kappa}=h^{\prime}\circ R_{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math>
for any sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, for any such <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> the complex structures <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> coincide on each <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p61.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p62" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p62.1" class="ltx_p">Arguing as above, we can construct a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}={\widetilde{M}}_{I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}={\widetilde{M}}_{I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, represents <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for any sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> equals to
<math id="S5.SS5.p62.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(1,r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> on the neighborhood <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{-1}\left(h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{-1}\left(h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently,
for any sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we have a well-defined symplectic form <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
This form is compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, represents the class <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and coincides with <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
on each <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p62.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p63" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p63.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently close to <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> so that it is tamed by the form <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> (which is compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>).
Then <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> are cohomologous symplectic forms on <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> that both tame <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and coincide
on each <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>. A relative version of
Moser’s theorem (see <a href="#S13.SS3" title="13.3 Relative version of Moser’s method ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.3</span></a>, part I) implies then that there exists <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> that maps <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>
into <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
fixes pointwise
each <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa\left(r_{i}+\epsilon\right)\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>
maps <math id="S5.SS5.p63.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}},h^{\prime}\circ R_{\kappa}\right)\in{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑃𝑎𝑖𝑟𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}^{\prime}},h^{\prime}\circ R_{\kappa}\right)\in{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> into
<math id="S5.SS5.p63.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\eta_{\kappa,{\widetilde{\theta}}},h\circ R_{\kappa}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p64" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p64.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, for any <math id="S5.SS5.p64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime},h^{\prime})\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime},h^{\prime})\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently close to
<math id="S5.SS5.p64.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)</annotation></semantics></math>, its <math id="S5.SS5.p64.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit
<math id="S5.SS5.p64.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime},h^{\prime}\}\in{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{I^{\prime},{\mathbbm{x}}^{\prime},h^{\prime}\}\in{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped by <math id="S5.SS5.p64.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> to the same point in <math id="S5.SS5.p64.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S5.SS5.p64.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},h\}\in{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},h\}\in{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This finishes the proof of the claim that <math id="S5.SS5.p64.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p65" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p65.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.SS5.p65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS5.p65.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted"> is surjective:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p66" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p66.1" class="ltx_p">The proof uses the well-known fact (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>]</cite>) that the symplectic blow-down is an “inverse” of the symplectic blow-up, up to an isotopy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p67" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p67.1" class="ltx_p">Namely, let
<math id="S5.SS5.p67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta,f)\in{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑃𝑎𝑖𝑟𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta,f)\in{\it Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S5.SS5.p67.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}f_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}f_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic embedding holomorphic with respect to
a complex structure <math id="S5.SS5.p67.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p67.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S5.SS5.p67.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us show that <math id="S5.SS5.p67.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\eta,f\}\in{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\eta,f\}\in{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the image of <math id="S5.SS5.p67.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p68" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p68.1" class="ltx_p">Set</p>
<table id="S5.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{i}:=f_{i}(0),i=1,\ldots,k," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}:=f_{i}(0),i=1,\ldots,k,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S5.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p68.2" class="ltx_p">By <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>,
the cohomology class <math id="S5.SS5.p68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S5.SS5.p68.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, by <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, <math id="S5.SS5.p68.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},f)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},f)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover,
by <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>, the class <math id="S5.SS5.p68.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>
can be represented, for a sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS5.p68.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, by a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS5.p68.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p68.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the following properties:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p69" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I8" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I8.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I8.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I8.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I8.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}=\rho_{x_{i}}(c,r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}=\rho_{x_{i}}(c,r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.I8.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{-1}\left(f_{i}\left(B(r_{i})\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{-1}\left(f_{i}\left(B(r_{i})\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.I8.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I8.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I8.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I8.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I8.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}]=\Pi^{*}[\eta]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}]=\Pi^{*}[\eta]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I8.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I8.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I8.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I8.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S5.I8.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p70" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p70.1" class="ltx_p">Consequently, <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> implies that
for <math id="S5.SS5.p70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=1</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS5.p70.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{1}({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{1}({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p70.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> with the
following properties:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p71" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I9" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I9.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I9.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I9.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I9.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\eta_{1}({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c})]=\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\eta_{1}({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c})]=\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I9.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I9.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I9.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I9.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{1}({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{1}({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c})</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S5.I9.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I9.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I9.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I9.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S5.I9.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\circ R_{1}=f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\circ R_{1}=f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectic with respect to <math id="S5.I9.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{1}({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{1}({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c})</annotation></semantics></math> (and holomorphic with respect to <math id="S5.I9.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p72" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p72.1" class="ltx_p">This yields that</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p73" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p73.1" class="ltx_p">1. <math id="S5.SS5.p73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S5.SS5.p73.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, by <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, <math id="S5.SS5.p73.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},f)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},f)\in{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p74" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p74.1" class="ltx_p">2. <math id="S5.SS5.p74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{1}({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{1}({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c})</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with <math id="S5.SS5.p74.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> on each <math id="S5.SS5.p74.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa r_{i}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa r_{i}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p74.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p75" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p75.1" class="ltx_p">A relative version of
Moser’s theorem (see <a href="#S13.SS3" title="13.3 Relative version of Moser’s method ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.3</span></a>, part I) implies then that there exists <math id="S5.SS5.p75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> that maps <math id="S5.SS5.p75.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{1}({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{1}({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c})</annotation></semantics></math>
into <math id="S5.SS5.p75.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> and
fixes pointwise each
each <math id="S5.SS5.p75.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa r_{i}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}\left(B^{2n}\left(\kappa r_{i}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p75.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\left(\eta_{1}\left({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}\right),f\right)=(\eta,f)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\left(\eta_{1}\left({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}\right),f\right)=(\eta,f).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p75.2" class="ltx_p">Thus,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi\left(\left\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},f\right\}\right)=\left\{\eta_{1}\left({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}\right),f\right\}=\left\{\eta,f\right\}\in{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi\left(\left\{I,{\mathbbm{x}},f\right\}\right)=\left\{\eta_{1}\left({\widetilde{\theta}}_{c}\right),f\right\}=\left\{\eta,f\right\}\in{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS5.p75.3" class="ltx_p">This shows that <math id="S5.SS5.p75.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\eta,f\}\in{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝙿𝚊𝚒𝚛𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\eta,f\}\in{\tt Pairs}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the image of <math id="S5.SS5.p75.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S5.SS5.p75.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p76" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p76.1" class="ltx_p">We have shown that <math id="S5.SS5.p76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined, continuous and surjective. By construction, it clearly satisfies the
properties 1 and 2 listed in the statement of
<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.
This finishes the proof of <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.
<span id="S5.SS5.p76.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p77" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p77.1" class="ltx_p">The tools developed above allow us also to prove the following existence result for tame embeddings of balls, which is an
analogue of part (I) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p78" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p78.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p78.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.17 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p79" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p79.1" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="S5.SS5.p79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> admits a tame embedding into <math id="S5.SS5.p79.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
for some <math id="S5.SS5.p79.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathit{CK}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathit{CK}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, tamed by <math id="S5.SS5.p79.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, and some <math id="S5.SS5.p79.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the class <math id="S5.SS5.p79.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>, can be represented by a form taming <math id="S5.SS5.p79.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> and equal to <math id="S5.SS5.p79.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(c_{i},r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(c_{i},r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="S5.SS5.p79.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, near each <math id="S5.SS5.p79.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.SS5.p79.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p80" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p80.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely, given <math id="S5.SS5.p80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathit{CK}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathit{CK}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, tamed by <math id="S5.SS5.p80.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, the following conditions are equivalent:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p81" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I10" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I10.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I10.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I10.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists <math id="S5.I10.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> so that the cohomology class <math id="S5.I10.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented by a form taming <math id="S5.I10.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> and equal to <math id="S5.I10.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(c_{i},r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(c_{i},r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="S5.I10.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, near each <math id="S5.I10.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.I10.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I10.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I10.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I10.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exist a tame embedding <math id="S5.I10.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S5.I10.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S5.I10.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic with respect to the complex structure <math id="S5.I10.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.I10.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> tamed by <math id="S5.I10.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p82" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p82.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS5.p82.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p83" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p83.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that for some <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathit{CK}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathit{CK}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, tamed by <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, and some <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the cohomology class
<math id="S5.SS5.p83.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented by a form taming <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> and equal to <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(c_{i},r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(c_{i},r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, near each <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}=\Pi^{-1}(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}=\Pi^{-1}(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.SS5.p83.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>. Then one can deduce from <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>
that there exist a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, cohomologous to <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and taming <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, and an embedding <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to M</annotation></semantics></math> symplectic with respect to <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> and holomorphic with respect to <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
The linear family of closed 2-forms connecting <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is formed by cohomologous forms each of which tames <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>
and is therefore symplectic. Therefore, by Moser’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mos</a>]</cite>, <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=\phi^{*}\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=\phi^{*}\eta</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The embedding <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:=\phi^{-1}\circ g:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:=\phi^{-1}\circ g:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectic with respect to <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>
and holomorphic with respect to the complex structure <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> tamed by <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that <math id="S5.SS5.p83.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is tame.
This proves one of the implications in the required equivalence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p84" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p84.1" class="ltx_p">Let us now prove the opposite implication.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p85" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p85.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S5.SS5.p85.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}f_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}f_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic embedding
holomorphic with respect to the complex structure <math id="S5.SS5.p85.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J:=\phi^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J:=\phi^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p85.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, tamed by <math id="S5.SS5.p85.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.
Set <math id="S5.SS5.p85.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}:=f_{i}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}:=f_{i}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p85.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS5.p85.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{y}}:=(y_{1},\ldots,y_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕪</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{y}}:=(y_{1},\ldots,y_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p86" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p86.1" class="ltx_p">By <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>,
there exists a symplectic form <math id="S5.SS5.p86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p86.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{J,{\mathbbm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕪</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{J,{\mathbbm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the following properties:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p87" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p87.1" class="ltx_p">1. <math id="S5.SS5.p87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}=\rho_{y_{i}}(c,r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}=\rho_{y_{i}}(c,r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, for a sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS5.p87.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
on a neighborhood of the exceptional divisor <math id="S5.SS5.p87.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{-1}(y_{i})\subset{\widetilde{M}}_{J,{\mathbbm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕪</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{-1}(y_{i})\subset{\widetilde{M}}_{J,{\mathbbm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p87.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p88" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p88.1" class="ltx_p">2. <math id="S5.SS5.p88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> tames <math id="S5.SS5.p88.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{J}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p89" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p89.1" class="ltx_p">3. <math id="S5.SS5.p89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\widetilde{\theta}}]=\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J,{\mathbbm{y}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕪</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\widetilde{\theta}}]=\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J,{\mathbbm{y}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p90" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p90.1" class="ltx_p">Set <math id="S5.SS5.p90.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}:=\phi(y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}:=\phi(y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS5.p90.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS5.p90.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The diffeomorphism <math id="S5.SS5.p90.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:M\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:M\to M</annotation></semantics></math> induces a diffeomorphism <math id="S5.SS5.p90.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{J,{\mathbbm{y}}}\to{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕪</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{J,{\mathbbm{y}}}\to{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
identifying the form <math id="S5.SS5.p90.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS5.p90.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{J,{\mathbbm{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕪</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{J,{\mathbbm{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a form on <math id="S5.SS5.p90.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
taming <math id="S5.SS5.p90.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>, equal to <math id="S5.SS5.p90.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{x_{i}}(c,r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{x_{i}}(c,r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> near each <math id="S5.SS5.p90.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i}=\Pi^{-1}(x_{i})\subset{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i}=\Pi^{-1}(x_{i})\subset{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.SS5.p90.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, and lying in the cohomology class <math id="S5.SS5.p90.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p91" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p91.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof of the opposite implication and of the proposition.
<span id="S5.SS5.p91.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6 </span>Connectedness of <math id="S5.SS6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler-type complex structure on <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Equip <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k}\times M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k}\times M</annotation></semantics></math> with the product complex structures induced by <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S5.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi\subset{\hat{M}}^{k}\times M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi\subset{\hat{M}}^{k}\times M</annotation></semantics></math>
the following incidence variety:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Xi:=\left\{\ \left(\left(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\right),x\right)\in{\hat{M}}^{k}\times M\ \big|\ x=x_{i}\ \textrm{for some}\ i=1,\ldots,k\ \right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">{</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.500em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>for some</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.500em">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi:=\left\{\ \left(\left(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\right),x\right)\in{\hat{M}}^{k}\times M\ \big|\ x=x_{i}\ \textrm{for some}\ i=1,\ldots,k\ \right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">It is a smooth complex submanifold of <math id="S5.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k}\times M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k}\times M</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="S5.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> the complex blow-up of <math id="S5.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k}\times M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k}\times M</annotation></semantics></math> along <math id="S5.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math>. It is a complex manifold that projects holomorphically to <math id="S5.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k}\times M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k}\times M</annotation></semantics></math> and consequently to <math id="S5.SS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote the latter projection by <math id="S5.SS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:{\mathcal{M}}\to{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:{\mathcal{M}}\to{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. This projection defines a complex analytic family of
complex manifolds equipped with Kähler-type complex structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The fiber of <math id="S5.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> over each <math id="S5.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
can be canonically identified with the complex blow-up <math id="S5.SS6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.SS6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.SS6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. The holonomy of the Gauss-Manin connection on <math id="S5.SS6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial and the homology/cohomology groups of all the fibers of <math id="S5.SS6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> are then identified with the homology/cohomology groups of the complex blow-up of <math id="S5.SS6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.SS6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> distinct ordered points (the identification does not depend on the choice of the points, since the homology/cohomology groups of the complex blow-ups for different choices can be canonically identified). The identifications preserve the Hodge decompositions and, in particular, cohomology classes of type <math id="S5.SS6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> of one fiber are identified with the <math id="S5.SS6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-classes of another fiber.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.18 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S5.SS6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S5.SS6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-cohomology class of the complex blow-up of <math id="S5.SS6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S5.SS6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S5.SS6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}_{\beta}\subset{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}_{\beta}\subset{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the set of all <math id="S5.SS6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S5.SS6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in H_{\widetilde{I}}^{1,1}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in H_{\widetilde{I}}^{1,1}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S5.SS6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6 Connectedness of 𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜_𝒞_0 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p9.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S5.SS6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> is empty, the claim is trivial. Assume it is non-empty. Since <math id="S5.SS6.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in H_{\widetilde{I}}^{1,1}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in H_{\widetilde{I}}^{1,1}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS6.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Kodaira-Spencer stability theorem implies (see <a href="#S13.SS2" title="13.2 Deformation families of complex structures ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.2</span></a>) that
the set <math id="S5.SS6.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> is open.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p10.1" class="ltx_p">A theorem of Demailly-Paun <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP</a>, Thm. 0.9]</cite> about the Kähler cones of the fibers of a complex analytic deformation family implies in our situation that there exists a countable union <math id="S5.SS6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{\nu}S_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>ν</mi></msub><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{\nu}S_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> of proper
analytic subsets <math id="S5.SS6.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\nu}\subsetneqq{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo>⫋</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\nu}\subsetneqq{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the Kähler cones
of the fibers <math id="S5.SS6.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}({\mathbbm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}({\mathbbm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> coincide for all <math id="S5.SS6.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}\setminus\bigcup_{\nu}S_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>ν</mi></msub><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}\setminus\bigcup_{\nu}S_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> under our identifications of the cohomologies of the fibers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Since the set <math id="S5.SS6.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}_{\beta}\subset{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}_{\beta}\subset{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is open, it intersects <math id="S5.SS6.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k}\setminus\bigcup_{\nu}S_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>ν</mi></msub><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k}\setminus\bigcup_{\nu}S_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore <math id="S5.SS6.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in H_{\widetilde{I}}^{1,1}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in H_{\widetilde{I}}^{1,1}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler for all <math id="S5.SS6.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}\setminus\bigcup_{\nu}S_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>ν</mi></msub><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}\setminus\bigcup_{\nu}S_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math>,
meaning that <math id="S5.SS6.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k}\setminus\bigcup_{\nu}S_{\nu}\subset{\mathcal{B}}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>ν</mi></msub><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k}\setminus\bigcup_{\nu}S_{\nu}\subset{\mathcal{B}}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Being the complement of a countable union of proper analytic subsets of a connected complex manifold, the set <math id="S5.SS6.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k}\setminus\bigcup_{\nu}S_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>ν</mi></msub><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k}\setminus\bigcup_{\nu}S_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected and dense in <math id="S5.SS6.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence,
the set <math id="S5.SS6.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}_{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}_{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected too.
<span id="S5.SS6.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS6.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.19 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p13.1" class="ltx_p">The fibers of the projection <math id="S5.SS6.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{pr}\,}:{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}})\to\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">pr</mtext><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{pr}\,}:{\mathcal{K}}({\bar{r}})\to\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> are connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS6.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6 Connectedness of 𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜_𝒞_0 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p15.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S5.SS6.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M,\omega)\cdot{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M,\omega)\cdot{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="S5.SS6.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the cohomology class
<math id="S5.SS6.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}=\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}=\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is of type <math id="S5.SS6.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The image of <span id="S5.SS6.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">pr</span>  lies in <math id="S5.SS6.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M,\omega)\cdot{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝑚𝑝𝑡</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M,\omega)\cdot{\it Cmpt}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and
the fiber of <span id="S5.SS6.p15.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">pr</span>  over <math id="S5.SS6.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\rm{Im}\,}{\textit{pr}\,}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">pr</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\rm{Im}\,}{\textit{pr}\,}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all <math id="S5.SS6.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that the <math id="S5.SS6.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-class <math id="S5.SS6.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler.
By <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6 Connectedness of 𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜_𝒞_0 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>, applied to <math id="S5.SS6.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta={\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta={\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>, this fiber is connected.
<span id="S5.SS6.p15.1.3" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS6.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.20 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.SS6.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex structure on <math id="S5.SS6.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS6.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS6.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Then the space of holomorphic embeddings <math id="S5.SS6.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:B^{2n}(r)\to(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:B^{2n}(r)\to(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S5.SS6.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(0)=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(0)=x</annotation></semantics></math>, is path-connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p19" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS6.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6 Connectedness of 𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜_𝒞_0 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S5.SS6.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:B^{2n}(r)\to(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:B^{2n}(r)\to(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS6.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(0)=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(0)=x</annotation></semantics></math>, is a holomorphic embedding. Let <math id="S5.SS6.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:B^{2n}(r)\to(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:B^{2n}(r)\to(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS6.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(0)=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(0)=x</annotation></semantics></math>, be another holomorphic
embedding. We want to show that <math id="S5.SS6.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> can be connected to <math id="S5.SS6.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> by a smooth path of holomorphic embeddings <math id="S5.SS6.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(r)\to(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(r)\to(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> mapping <math id="S5.SS6.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS6.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p21.1" class="ltx_p">We can assume without loss of generality that <math id="S5.SS6.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Im}\,}g\subset{\rm{Im}\,}h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Im}\,}g\subset{\rm{Im}\,}h</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed,
for a sufficiently small <math id="S5.SS6.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S5.SS6.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Im}\,}(g\circ R_{\kappa})\subset{\rm{Im}\,}h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Im}\,}(g\circ R_{\kappa})\subset{\rm{Im}\,}h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS6.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=g\circ R_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=g\circ R_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be
connected to <math id="S5.SS6.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\circ R_{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>κ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\circ R_{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> by the family <math id="S5.SS6.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g\circ R_{t}\}_{t\in[\kappa,1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g\circ R_{t}\}_{t\in[\kappa,1]}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming <math id="S5.SS6.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Im}\,}g\subset{\rm{Im}\,}h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Im}\,}g\subset{\rm{Im}\,}h</annotation></semantics></math>, in order to connect <math id="S5.SS6.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS6.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> by a smooth path of holomorphic embeddings <math id="S5.SS6.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(r)\to(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(r)\to(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> mapping <math id="S5.SS6.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS6.p22.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> it suffices to show that the holomorphic embedding
<math id="S5.SS6.p22.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:=h^{-1}\circ g:B^{2n}(r)\to B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:=h^{-1}\circ g:B^{2n}(r)\to B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS6.p22.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{-1}\circ g(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{-1}\circ g(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, can be connected to the identity by a smooth path of holomorphic embeddings <math id="S5.SS6.p22.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n}(r)\to B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n}(r)\to B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math> mapping <math id="S5.SS6.p22.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS6.p22.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The latter result is standard and is proved by a version of the Alexander trick – see <a href="#S13.SS4" title="13.4 Alexander’s trick ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.4</span></a>, part II.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p23.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof of the proposition.
<span id="S5.SS6.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p24" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS6.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p25.1" class="ltx_p">The space <math id="S5.SS6.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}={\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}={\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is connected if only if the space
<math id="S5.SS6.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p26.1" class="ltx_p">The projection <math id="S5.SS6.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)\mapsto(I,{\mathbbm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,{\mathbbm{x}},h)\mapsto(I,{\mathbbm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> defines a surjective continuous map <math id="S5.SS6.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\to{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}\to{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
In view of <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6 Connectedness of 𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜_𝒞_0 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>, the fibers of this map are connected.
Hence, <math id="S5.SS6.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑇𝑟𝑖𝑝𝑙𝑒𝑠</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected if and only <math id="S5.SS6.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is connected.
By <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6 Connectedness of 𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜_𝒞_0 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>, the fibers of the projection <math id="S5.SS6.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{pr}\,}:{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})\to{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">pr</mtext><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{pr}\,}:{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})\to{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> are connected. Thus,
<math id="S5.SS6.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math> is connected if and only if <math id="S5.SS6.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{pr}\,}({\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">pr</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{pr}\,}({\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}}))</annotation></semantics></math>
is connected.
The set <math id="S5.SS6.p26.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{pr}\,}({\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">pr</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{pr}\,}({\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}}))</annotation></semantics></math> is connected if and only if the set <math id="S5.SS6.p26.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})={\textit{pr}\,}({\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">pr</mtext><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})={\textit{pr}\,}({\mathcal{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.
Summing up, we see that <math id="S5.SS6.p26.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚃𝚛𝚒𝚙𝚕𝚎𝚜</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Triples}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected if and only if <math id="S5.SS6.p26.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.
This finishes the proof of the proposition.
<span id="S5.SS6.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>The case of complex projective spaces and their products</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As above, we denote by <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> the standard complex structure on <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and by <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math>, or by <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the standard Fubini-Study symplectic form on <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> normalized so that <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}}\omega_{FS}=\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}}\omega_{FS}=\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in H^{2}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n};{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in H^{2}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n};{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> be the generator of <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n};{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n};{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega_{FS}]=\pi h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega_{FS}]=\pi h</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. For convenience, we restate it here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.1 (= <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the manifold <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:={\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\ldots\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:={\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\ldots\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1},\ldots,n_{m}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1},\ldots,n_{m}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{1}+\ldots+n_{m}=:n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{1}+\ldots+n_{m}=:n</annotation></semantics></math>, endowed with the symplectic form <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\mathbbm{c}}:=c_{1}\omega_{FS,n_{1}}\oplus\ldots\oplus c_{m}\omega_{FS,n_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>𝕔</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\mathbbm{c}}:=c_{1}\omega_{FS,n_{1}}\oplus\ldots\oplus c_{m}\omega_{FS,n_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{c}}:=(c_{1},\ldots,c_{m})\in({\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0})^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕔</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{c}}:=(c_{1},\ldots,c_{m})\in({\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0})^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> be the complex
structure on <math id="S6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> which is the product of the standard complex structures on the factors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1},\ldots,l_{m}\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1},\ldots,l_{m}\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[l_{1}:\ldots:l_{m}]=[c_{1}:\ldots:c_{m}]\in{\mathbb{R}}P^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[l_{1}:\ldots:l_{m}]=[c_{1}:\ldots:c_{m}]\in{\mathbb{R}}P^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let</p>
<table id="S6.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k:=\frac{(n_{1}+\ldots n_{m})!}{n_{1}!\cdot\ldots\cdot n_{m}!}l_{1}^{n_{1}}\cdot\ldots\cdot l_{m}^{n_{m}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">!</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k:=\frac{(n_{1}+\ldots n_{m})!}{n_{1}!\cdot\ldots\cdot n_{m}!}l_{1}^{n_{1}}\cdot\ldots\cdot l_{m}^{n_{m}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> be a proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold. If <math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we also allow
<math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\textrm{pt})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\times\textrm{pt})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Then Kähler-type embeddings of disjoint unions of <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> equal balls into <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mathbbm{c}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>𝕔</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mathbbm{c}})</annotation></semantics></math>
are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely, if <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, then for each proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding of <math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>
into <math id="S6.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S6.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> that is compatible with <math id="S6.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>
and isotopic to <math id="S6.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> by an isotopy preserving <math id="S6.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (as a set).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (= <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>):</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The proof mimics the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>, Thm. 1.5.A]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Namely, as it is shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>, Pf. of Thm. 1.5.A]</cite>, there exist <math id="S6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in{\hat{M}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a holomorphic map <math id="S6.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1},I_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1},I_{st})</annotation></semantics></math> so that</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p11" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> induces a biholomorphism on each exceptional divisor,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-F^{*}[\omega_{FS,n-1}]\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-F^{*}[\omega_{FS,n-1}]\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear combination of the cohomology classes <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\ldots,e_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\ldots,e_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> Poincaré-dual to the fundamental homology classes of the exceptional divisors of <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi:{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi:{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is the blow-up projection, as before.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S6.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. A direct check using the above-mentioned properties of <math id="S6.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>
shows that the form <math id="S6.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\omega}}_{c}:=(1+c)^{-1}(\Pi^{*}\omega+cF^{*}\omega_{FS,n-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\omega}}_{c}:=(1+c)^{-1}(\Pi^{*}\omega+cF^{*}\omega_{FS,n-1})</annotation></semantics></math>
is a symplectic form on <math id="S6.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S6.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> and representing the cohomology class
<math id="S6.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}(1+c)^{-1}ce_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}(1+c)^{-1}ce_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, by part (I) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> (=<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>), for <math id="S6.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{c}:=\sqrt{(1+c)^{-1}c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{c}:=\sqrt{(1+c)^{-1}c}</annotation></semantics></math>,
there exists a symplectic embedding <math id="S6.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}B(r_{c})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}B(r_{c})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
and an isotopy <math id="S6.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}=Id</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(\Sigma)=\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(\Sigma)=\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S6.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic with respect to the complex structure <math id="S6.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> which is compatible with <math id="S6.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.
As it is shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>, Pf. of Thm. 1.5.A]</cite>, as <math id="S6.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\to+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\to+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the symplectic volume
of <math id="S6.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}B(r_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}B(r_{c})</annotation></semantics></math> tends from below to the symplectic volume of <math id="S6.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This readily yields the claim of the theorem.
<span id="S6.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Let us know consider the case <math id="S6.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The following result is classical – see <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> for the references.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.2 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p16.1" class="ltx_p">(I) Any Kähler-type complex structure on <math id="S6.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be identified with <math id="S6.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> by a diffeomorphism lying in <math id="S6.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p17" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p17.1" class="ltx_p">(II) Any two Kähler-type complex structures on <math id="S6.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> lying in the same component of <math id="S6.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> can be
mapped into each other by an element of <math id="S6.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S6.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 6.3 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p19" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p19.1" class="ltx_p">1. Part (I) of <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> is due to Hirzebruch and Kodaira <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">HiK</a>]</cite> for odd <math id="S6.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and to Yau <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx105" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yau1</a>]</cite> for even <math id="S6.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> – see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tos</a>]</cite> for an exposition and a proof. (The result in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tos</a>]</cite> does not state explicitly that the diffeomorphism in part (I) of the theorem acts trivially on homology but this can be easily deduced from the proof there.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p20" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p20.1" class="ltx_p">2.
It is not known whether each complex structure on <math id="S6.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, is of Kähler type. For <math id="S6.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> this is obviously true.
For <math id="S6.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> this is true by a deep result of Yau <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx105" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yau1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p21" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p21.1" class="ltx_p">3. Part (II) of the theorem implies that <math id="S6.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})=\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})=\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S6.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>
is connected.
It is known that <math id="S6.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})\neq\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})\neq\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx52" title="" class="ltx_ref">KrS</a>, Thm. 1.2]</cite> and <math id="S6.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bru</a>, Rem. II.11]</cite>. Thus, <math id="S6.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3,4</annotation></semantics></math>, has more than one connected component. We do not know whether the same facts hold for <math id="S6.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p22" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p22.1" class="ltx_p">4.
Part (II) of <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> follows from a theorem of Fröhlicher and Nijenhuis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">FroN</a>]</cite>, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">KoS1</a>, Thm. 6.3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="S6.p23" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.4 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p24.1" class="ltx_p">Each connected component of the space <math id="S6.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is a point. The <math id="S6.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>-action
on <math id="S6.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is transitive. <span id="S6.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p25" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.5 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p26.1" class="ltx_p">The group <math id="S6.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on the set of connected components of
<math id="S6.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S6.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p27" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p28.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S6.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex structure on <math id="S6.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S6.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, by part (I) of
<a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, there exists <math id="S6.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}J=I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}J=I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S6.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic form compatible with <math id="S6.p28.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> and cohomologous to <math id="S6.p28.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore the straight path in the space of 2-forms connecting these forms is formed by cohomologous symplectic forms, and consequently, by Moser’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mos</a>]</cite>, there exists <math id="S6.p28.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p28.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{*}\phi^{*}\omega_{FS}=\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{*}\phi^{*}\omega_{FS}=\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.p28.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\psi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\psi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p28.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi\psi)^{*}J=\psi^{*}I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi\psi)^{*}J=\psi^{*}I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the <math id="S6.p28.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit
of <math id="S6.p28.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that the connected component of <math id="S6.p28.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S6.p28.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J]</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped under the action of <math id="S6.p28.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\psi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\psi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> into the connected component of <math id="S6.p28.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S6.p28.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I_{st}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I_{st}]</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields the proposition. <span id="S6.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p29.1" class="ltx_p">We are ready to prove <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> – let us first recall it here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p30" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.6 (=<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p31" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p31.1" class="ltx_p">A. For each <math id="S6.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
Kähler-type embeddings of disjoint unions of <math id="S6.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> equal balls into <math id="S6.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p32.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely,
if <math id="S6.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l^{n}{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l^{n}{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>, then for any complex structure <math id="S6.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S6.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math> (and, in particular, for the standard complex structure <math id="S6.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math>) and for each proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold <math id="S6.p32.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},J)</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a <math id="S6.p32.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding of <math id="S6.p32.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{l^{n}}B^{2n}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>l</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{l^{n}}B^{2n}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>
into <math id="S6.p32.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S6.p32.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> that is compatible with <math id="S6.p32.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math>
and isotopic to <math id="S6.p32.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> by an isotopy preserving <math id="S6.p32.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (as a set).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p33" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p33.1" class="ltx_p">B. The group <math id="S6.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on the set of connected components of <math id="S6.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with
<math id="S6.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p34" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p34.1" class="ltx_p">C.
For any <math id="S6.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S6.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> (if they exist!) lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S6.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>-action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p35.1" class="ltx_p">They lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S6.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>-action
if and only if they favor a common connected component of <math id="S6.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. In the latter case there exists <math id="S6.p35.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> such that both embeddings are of <math id="S6.p35.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p36" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p36.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p36.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (=<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>):</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p37.1" class="ltx_p">As we already explained in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we only need to prove parts B and C.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p38.1" class="ltx_p">Part B is exactly
<a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> that we have just proved.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p39.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove part C. By <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, for each connected component <math id="S6.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>
the corresponding connected component <math id="S6.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p39.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is just a point. Consequently, for any <math id="S6.p39.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> the set <math id="S6.p39.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})\subset{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})\subset{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\mathbb{C}}P^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. Now applying <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>
along with part B of this theorem we get part C.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p40.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof.
<span id="S6.p40.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p41.1" class="ltx_p">Now consider the case of <math id="S6.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly to our previous notation, we write <math id="S6.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for the set of all ordered <math id="S6.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples of pairwise distinct points in <math id="S6.p41.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As before, given <math id="S6.p41.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the manifold <math id="S6.p41.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},{\mathbbm{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},{\mathbbm{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the complex blow-up of <math id="S6.p41.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S6.p41.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given <math id="S6.p41.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{r}}=(r_{1},\ldots,r_{k})\in({\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{r}}=(r_{1},\ldots,r_{k})\in({\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p41.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\in\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\in\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, set <math id="S6.p41.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:=[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:=[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}:=\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{J,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}:=\Pi^{*}[\omega]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{J,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p42" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p42.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p42.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.7 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p43.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S6.p43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p44" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p44.1" class="ltx_p">We say that <math id="S6.p44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> points in <math id="S6.p44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are in <span id="S6.p44.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">general position</span> if</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p45" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p45.1" class="ltx_p">(i) no three points are on a line;</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p46" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p46.1" class="ltx_p">(ii) no six points are on a conic;</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p47" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p47.1" class="ltx_p">(iii) no cubic passes through the points with one of the points being a singular
point.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p48" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p48.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p48.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.8 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p49" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p49.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.p49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p50" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p50.1" class="ltx_p">Then the set of tuples <math id="S6.p50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p50.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are in general position is connected
and dense in <math id="S6.p50.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p51" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p51.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p51.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p52" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p52.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the product complex structure on <math id="S6.p52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In view of <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>,
the <math id="S6.p52.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples <math id="S6.p52.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p52.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
are <span id="S6.p52.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">not</span> in general position lie in a proper analytic subset of
<math id="S6.p52.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that the set of <math id="S6.p52.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples <math id="S6.p52.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S6.p52.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
<span id="S6.p52.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">are</span> in general position
contains the complement of a proper analytic subset of <math id="S6.p52.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, it is connected (because <math id="S6.p52.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> itself is connected) and
dense (and open) in <math id="S6.p52.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S6.p52.1.3" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p53" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p53.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p53.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.9 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p54" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p54.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S6.p54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S6.p54.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{r}}=(r_{1},\ldots,r_{k})\in({\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{r}}=(r_{1},\ldots,r_{k})\in({\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p54.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}\geq r_{2}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}\geq r_{2}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.p54.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the connected component of <math id="S6.p54.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S6.p54.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p55" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p55.1" class="ltx_p">Then for any <math id="S6.p55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})\in(\widehat{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.p55.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are in general position,
the class <math id="S6.p55.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S6.p55.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the numbers <math id="S6.p55.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the following inequalities (listed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>, Cor. 1.3G]</cite>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p56" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p56.1" class="ltx_p">(v) <math id="S6.p56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{4}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{4}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p57" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p57.1" class="ltx_p">(c1) <math id="S6.p57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}^{2}+r_{2}^{2}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}^{2}+r_{2}^{2}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S6.p57.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p58" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p58.1" class="ltx_p">(c2) <math id="S6.p58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}^{2}+\ldots+r_{5}^{2}&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}^{2}+\ldots+r_{5}^{2}&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S6.p58.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p59" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p59.1" class="ltx_p">(c3) <math id="S6.p59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r_{1}^{2}+\sum_{i=2}^{7}r_{i}^{2}&lt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>7</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r_{1}^{2}+\sum_{i=2}^{7}r_{i}^{2}&lt;3</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S6.p59.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p60" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p60.1" class="ltx_p">(c4) <math id="S6.p60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r_{1}^{2}+2r_{2}^{2}+2r_{3}^{2}+r_{4}^{2}+\ldots+r_{8}^{2}&lt;4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>8</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r_{1}^{2}+2r_{2}^{2}+2r_{3}^{2}+r_{4}^{2}+\ldots+r_{8}^{2}&lt;4</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S6.p60.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p61" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p61.1" class="ltx_p">(c5) <math id="S6.p61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\sum_{i=1}^{6}r_{i}^{2}+r_{7}^{2}+r_{8}^{2}&lt;5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>6</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>8</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\sum_{i=1}^{6}r_{i}^{2}+r_{7}^{2}+r_{8}^{2}&lt;5</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S6.p61.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p62" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p62.1" class="ltx_p">(c6) <math id="S6.p62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3r_{1}^{2}+2\sum_{i=2}^{8}r_{i}^{2}&lt;6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>8</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3r_{1}^{2}+2\sum_{i=2}^{8}r_{i}^{2}&lt;6</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S6.p62.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=8</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p63" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p63.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p63.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p64" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p64.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S6.p64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math> and the points <math id="S6.p64.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are in general position (such points exist by <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), then <math id="S6.p64.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},{\mathbbm{x}}},{\widetilde{I}}_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},{\mathbbm{x}}},{\widetilde{I}}_{st})</annotation></semantics></math>,
for <math id="S6.p64.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, is a del Pezzo surface (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dol</a>, Thm. 8.1.25]</cite>).
As such, it is a good generic surface in the sense of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">FriM</a>, Def. 2.1]</cite> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">FriM</a>, Rem. 2.9]</cite>). The Kähler cone of <math id="S6.p64.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},{\mathbbm{x}}},{\widetilde{I}}_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},{\mathbbm{x}}},{\widetilde{I}}_{st})</annotation></semantics></math>
is the cone of classes in <math id="S6.p64.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> that have a positive square and
evaluate positively on the homology classes of holomorphically
embedded 2-spheres with self-intersection index equal to <math id="S6.p64.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> and on the class
<math id="S6.p64.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3[{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}]-\sum_{i=1}^{k}[E_{i}]\in H_{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3[{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}]-\sum_{i=1}^{k}[E_{i}]\in H_{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">FriM</a>]</cite>.
Together with the information on the homology classes of holomorphically
embedded 2-spheres with self-intersection index equal to <math id="S6.p64.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dmz</a>]</cite>,
this yields that <math id="S6.p64.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler
with respect to <math id="S6.p64.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the numbers <math id="S6.p64.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the inequalities (v), (c1)-(c6) – see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>, Cor. 1.3G]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p65" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p65.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof of the proposition.
<span id="S6.p65.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p66" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p66.1" class="ltx_p">Let us recall the following result of Gromov.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p67" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p67.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p67.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.10 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gro</a>]</cite></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p68" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p68.1" class="ltx_p">The group <math id="S6.p68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopy equivalent to <math id="S6.p68.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PU(3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PU(3)</annotation></semantics></math> and, in particular, connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p69" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p69.1" class="ltx_p">Recall <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p70" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p70.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p70.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.11 (=<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p71" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p71.1" class="ltx_p">There is only one connected component of <math id="S6.p71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S6.p71.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math> – it is the connected component
of <math id="S6.p71.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S6.p71.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math>. Any two complex structures in that connected component are isotopic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p72" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p72.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p72.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (= <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>):</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p73" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p73.1" class="ltx_p">The proposition follows immediately from <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
and part (II) of <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
<span id="S6.p73.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p74" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p74.1" class="ltx_p">Now we are ready to prove <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> – let us recall it here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p75" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p75.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p75.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.12 (=<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p76" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p76.1" class="ltx_p">A. Assume that <math id="S6.p76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p76.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}\geq r_{2}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}\geq r_{2}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p77" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p77.1" class="ltx_p">Then for any proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold <math id="S6.p77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})</annotation></semantics></math>,
any symplectic embedding <math id="S6.p77.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> is, in fact, of <math id="S6.p77.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I_{st}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I_{st}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type:
it is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S6.p77.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> that is compatible with <math id="S6.p77.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math> and isotopic
to <math id="S6.p77.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> by an isotopy fixing <math id="S6.p77.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (as a set).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p78" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p78.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, by part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, such a Kähler-type embedding <math id="S6.p78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>
exists if and only if the radii <math id="S6.p78.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the inequalities (v), (c1)-(c6) listed in part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p79" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p79.1" class="ltx_p">B. For any proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold <math id="S6.p79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S6.p79.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=l^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=l^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p79.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
any symplectic embedding <math id="S6.p79.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> is, in fact, of <math id="S6.p79.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I_{st}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I_{st}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type:
it is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S6.p79.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> that is compatible with <math id="S6.p79.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{FS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{FS}</annotation></semantics></math>
and isotopic to <math id="S6.p79.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> by an isotopy preserving <math id="S6.p79.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (as a set).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p80" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p80.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, by part B of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, such Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S6.p80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed: they exist if and only if <math id="S6.p80.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{4}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{4}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p81" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p81.1" class="ltx_p">C. Let <math id="S6.p81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>
be either of the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p82" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p82.1" class="ltx_p">(1) the empty set;</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p83" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p83.1" class="ltx_p">(2) a finite union of closed compact symplectic submanifolds (without boundary) of real dimension 2
whose pairwise intersections (if they exist) are transverse and <math id="S6.p83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>–orthogonal;</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p84" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p84.1" class="ltx_p">(3) a Lagrangian submanifold which is diffeomorphic to either <math id="S6.p84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{S}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{S}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p84.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p85" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p85.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S6.p85.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p85.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S6.p85.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> (if they exist!) lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S6.p85.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>-action, meaning that
the space of Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S6.p85.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p86" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p86.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p86.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> (=<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>):</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p87" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p87.1" class="ltx_p">As we already explained in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>,
part B of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> follows from
<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>
and from part (II) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>,
while part C of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> follows directly from part (I) of
<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p88" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p88.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove part A.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p89" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p89.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S6.p89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic embedding. Then, by <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>,
the radii <math id="S6.p89.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the conditions (v), (c1)-(c6).
Consequently, <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> together with part (I) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> (=<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 From holomorphic to Kähler-type embeddings of balls ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>) yields that there exists an <math id="S6.p89.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I_{st}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I_{st}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding <math id="S6.p89.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\to({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\omega_{FS})</annotation></semantics></math>. By part (II) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, this implies that <math id="S6.p89.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is of <math id="S6.p89.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I_{st}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I_{st}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type too.
This finishes the proof of part A and of the theorem.
<span id="S6.p89.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>The case of <math id="S7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">♯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">♯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. As a smooth manifold, <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> will be identified with the complex blow-up <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P}^{2}_{I_{st},x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P}^{2}_{I_{st},x}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})</annotation></semantics></math> at a point <math id="S7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the complex structure on <math id="S7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> identified in this way with the blow-up complex structure
<math id="S7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P}^{2}_{I_{st},x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P}^{2}_{I_{st},x}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subset M=\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P}^{2}_{I_{st},x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subset M=\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P}^{2}_{I_{st},x}</annotation></semantics></math> the exceptional divisor.
Let <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}]\in H_{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}]\in H_{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> be the homology class of a projective line <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\subset M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\subset M</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h,e\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h,e\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> the cohomology classes Poincaré-dual to the homology classes of <math id="S7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[E]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[E]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, assume that <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Kähler form on the complex manifold <math id="S7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,J_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,J_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle[\omega_{\lambda}],[{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}]\rangle=\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle[\omega_{\lambda}],[{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}]\rangle=\pi</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle[\omega_{\lambda}],[E]\rangle=\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle[\omega_{\lambda}],[E]\rangle=\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, <math id="S7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega_{\lambda}]=\pi h-\lambda e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega_{\lambda}]=\pi h-\lambda e</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Further on in this section we fix</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq k\leq 8," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq k\leq 8,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S7.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}\geq R_{2}\geq\ldots\geq R_{k-1}&gt;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}\geq R_{2}\geq\ldots\geq R_{k-1}&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">and set</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}:=R_{1}\geq\ldots\geq r_{i-1}:=R_{i-1}\geq r_{i}:=\sqrt{\lambda/\pi}\geq" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>≥</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}:=R_{1}\geq\ldots\geq r_{i-1}:=R_{i-1}\geq r_{i}:=\sqrt{\lambda/\pi}\geq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S7.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq r_{i+1}:=R_{i}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}:=R_{k-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq r_{i+1}:=R_{i}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}:=R_{k-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1})\in{\hat{M}}^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1})\in{\hat{M}}^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
and each complex structure <math id="S7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S7.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>
will denote the complex blow-up of <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> will denote the lift of <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{M}:{\widetilde{M}}_{I,x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{M}:{\widetilde{M}}_{I,x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the natural projection.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S7.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1,M},\ldots,e_{k-1,M}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1,M},\ldots,e_{k-1,M}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> are the cohomology classes Poincaré-dual to the fundamental homology classes of the exceptional divisors.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We say that <math id="S7.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> points <math id="S7.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}\in M</annotation></semantics></math> are in <span id="S7.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">general position</span> if <math id="S7.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}\in M\setminus E\cong{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}\in M\setminus E\cong{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus x</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S7.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> points <math id="S7.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1},x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1},x</annotation></semantics></math> are in general position as points of <math id="S7.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.1 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The set of tuples <math id="S7.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1})\in{\hat{M}}^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1})\in{\hat{M}}^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S7.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are in general position is connected
and dense in <math id="S7.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S7" title="7 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Arguing as in <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we get that
the set of <math id="S7.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-tuples <math id="S7.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1})\in{\hat{M}}^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1})\in{\hat{M}}^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S7.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
are in general position
contains the complement of a proper analytic subset of <math id="S7.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, it is connected (since <math id="S7.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected) and
dense (and open) in <math id="S7.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S7.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.2 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}\in M</annotation></semantics></math> be in general position.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Then the cohomology class</p>
<table id="S7.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{M}^{*}[\omega_{\lambda}]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,M}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{1},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{M}^{*}[\omega_{\lambda}]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,M}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{1},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p14.2" class="ltx_p">is Kähler with respect to <math id="S7.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{J}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{J}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the numbers <math id="S7.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the inequalities (v), (c1)-(c6) listed in <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S7" title="7 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Since the points <math id="S7.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in <math id="S7.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\setminus E\cong{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\setminus E\cong{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus x</annotation></semantics></math>, we can view them as points of <math id="S7.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote by <math id="S7.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}:\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1},x}\to{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}:\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1},x}\to{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> the blow-up projection and by
<math id="S7.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}},\ldots,e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\in H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1},x};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}},\ldots,e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\in H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1},x};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> the cohomology classes Poincaré-dual to the fundamental homology classes of the exceptional divisors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p17.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a natural biholomorphism between <math id="S7.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{1},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}},{\widetilde{J}}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{1},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}},{\widetilde{J}}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S7.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1},x},{\widetilde{I}}_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1},x},{\widetilde{I}}_{st})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S7.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega_{\lambda}]=\pi h-\lambda e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega_{\lambda}]=\pi h-\lambda e</annotation></semantics></math>,
this biholomorphism identifies the cohomology class</p>
<table id="S7.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{M}^{*}[\omega_{\lambda}]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,M}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{1},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{M}^{*}[\omega_{\lambda}]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,M}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{1},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p17.2" class="ltx_p">with the cohomology class</p>
<table id="S7.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}(\pi h)-\lambda e-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\in H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1},x};{\mathbb{R}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}(\pi h)-\lambda e-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\in H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1},x};{\mathbb{R}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p17.3" class="ltx_p">(Here <math id="S7.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{1},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{1},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with the cohomology class in <math id="S7.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1},x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1},x}</annotation></semantics></math> associated to the exceptional fiber over <math id="S7.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>). Now the proposition follows from <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
<span id="S7.p17.3.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Now we are ready to prove <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> – let us recall it here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p19" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.3 (=<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">♯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\sharp\overline{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S7.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler-type form on <math id="S7.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p21.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p22" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p22.1" class="ltx_p">A. Let <math id="S7.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1}\geq R_{2}\geq\ldots\geq R_{k-1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1}\geq R_{2}\geq\ldots\geq R_{k-1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that
<math id="S7.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}:=R_{1}\geq\ldots\geq r_{i-1}:=R_{i-1}\geq r_{i}:=\sqrt{\lambda/\pi}\geq r_{i+1}:=R_{i}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}:=R_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}:=R_{1}\geq\ldots\geq r_{i-1}:=R_{i-1}\geq r_{i}:=\sqrt{\lambda/\pi}\geq r_{i+1}:=R_{i}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}:=R_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Then for any proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold <math id="S7.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset(M,J_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset(M,J_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>,
the following conditions are equivalent:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p24" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S7.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S7.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a <math id="S7.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding
<math id="S7.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S7.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> that is
compatible with <math id="S7.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> and isotopic to <math id="S7.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by an isotopy preserving <math id="S7.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (as a set).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The numbers <math id="S7.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the inequalities (v), (c1)-(c6) in part A of
<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S7.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p25.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S7.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the inequalities (v), (c1)-(c6) in part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, any symplectic embedding
<math id="S7.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> is, in fact, of <math id="S7.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p26" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p26.1" class="ltx_p">B.
For any <math id="S7.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\ldots,R_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\ldots,R_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S7.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> (if they exist!) lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S7.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>-action, meaning that
the space of Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S7.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p27" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S7" title="7 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> (=<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>):</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p28.1" class="ltx_p">As we already explained in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, part B follows from part (I) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p29.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove part A.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p30.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that the numbers <math id="S7.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the conditions (v), (c1)-(c6). Then, by <a href="#S7" title="7 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, combined with part (I) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, the union <math id="S7.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> admits a
Kähler-type embedding into <math id="S7.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S7.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> isotopic to <math id="S7.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and compatible with <math id="S7.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that the embedding is of <math id="S7.p30.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type.
By part (II) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, this implies that
any symplectic embedding <math id="S7.p30.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> is of <math id="S7.p30.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p31.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, assume
there exists a Kähler-type embedding <math id="S7.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}f_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega_{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}f_{i}:\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega_{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure <math id="S7.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S7.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> and
such that <math id="S7.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=\phi^{*}J_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=\phi^{*}J_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S7.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Set <math id="S7.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}:=f_{i}(0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}:=f_{i}(0)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p31.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.p31.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.p31.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi({\mathbbm{x}}):=\left(\phi(x_{1}),\ldots,\phi(x_{k-1})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi({\mathbbm{x}}):=\left(\phi(x_{1}),\ldots,\phi(x_{k-1})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p32.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that a Kähler-type embedding of a closed ball is defined as a Kähler-type embedding of a larger open ball. Therefore using
<a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and composing <math id="S7.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, if necessary, with a small parallel translation of the balls in <math id="S7.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we may assume without loss of generality that the points <math id="S7.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x_{1}),\ldots,\phi(x_{k-1})\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x_{1}),\ldots,\phi(x_{k-1})\in M</annotation></semantics></math> are in general position.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p33.1" class="ltx_p">By part (I) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>,
the cohomology class <math id="S7.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S7.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The diffeomorphism <math id="S7.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:M\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:M\to M</annotation></semantics></math> induces a diffeomorphism <math id="S7.p33.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\to{\widetilde{M}}_{J_{1},\phi({\mathbbm{x}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}}\to{\widetilde{M}}_{J_{1},\phi({\mathbbm{x}})}</annotation></semantics></math> that identifies <math id="S7.p33.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.p33.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{J}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{J}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as the classes <math id="S7.p33.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S7.p33.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>
and in <math id="S7.p33.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{1},\phi({\mathbbm{x}})};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{1},\phi({\mathbbm{x}})};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> (abusing the notation, we use the same letter for both classes).
Therefore <math id="S7.p33.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{1},\phi({\mathbbm{x}})};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{1},\phi({\mathbbm{x}})};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S7.p33.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{J}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{J}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S7.p33.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x_{1}),\ldots,\phi(x_{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x_{1}),\ldots,\phi(x_{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math> are in general position,
<a href="#S7" title="7 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> yields that
the conditions (v), (c1)-(c6) are satisfied.
This proves part A and finishes the proof of the theorem.
<span id="S7.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>The case of <math id="S8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> the standard product complex structure on <math id="S8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{2l}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, be a complex structure on <math id="S8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,J_{2l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,J_{2l})</annotation></semantics></math> is biholomorphic to the Hirzebruch surface
<math id="S8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{2l}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, we assume that <math id="S8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard product complex structure on <math id="S8.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S8.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b,f\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b,f\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> be the cohomology classes Poincaré-dual, respectively, to <math id="S8.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times pt]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times pt]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[F]=[pt\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[F]=[pt\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Fubini-Study form on <math id="S8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> normalized so that <math id="S8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}}\omega_{0}=\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}}\omega_{0}=\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S8.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> define the symplectic form
<math id="S8.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S8.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S8.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\mu}:=\mu\omega_{0}\oplus\omega_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\mu}:=\mu\omega_{0}\oplus\omega_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Thus,
<math id="S8.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega_{\mu}]=\pi(\mu b+f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega_{\mu}]=\pi(\mu b+f)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Further on in this section, we fix</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq k\leq 8," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq k\leq 8,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S8.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},R_{2},\ldots,R_{k-2}&gt;0,0&lt;R_{k-1}&lt;1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},R_{2},\ldots,R_{k-2}&gt;0,0&lt;R_{k-1}&lt;1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">and define <math id="S8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}\geq r_{2}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}\geq r_{2}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as the numbers</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{R_{1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{1}^{2}},\frac{R_{2}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{1}^{2}},\ldots,\frac{R_{k-2}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{1}^{2}},\frac{\mu-R_{1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{1}^{2}},\frac{1-R_{1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{1}^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{R_{1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{1}^{2}},\frac{R_{2}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{1}^{2}},\ldots,\frac{R_{k-2}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{1}^{2}},\frac{\mu-R_{1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{1}^{2}},\frac{1-R_{1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{1}^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">sorted in the non-increasing order.
</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Abusing the notation, for a blow-up of <math id="S8.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively, of <math id="S8.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) at any finite collection of points <math id="S8.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},x_{2},\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},x_{2},\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively, <math id="S8.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},y_{2},\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},y_{2},\ldots</annotation></semantics></math>)
we will use the same letter <math id="S8.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively, <math id="S8.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>) for the blow-up projection
and the same letters <math id="S8.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1,M},e_{2,M},\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1,M},e_{2,M},\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively, <math id="S8.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}},e_{2,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}},\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}},e_{2,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}},\ldots</annotation></semantics></math>) for the cohomology classes Poincaré-dual to the homology classes of the exceptional
divisors.
For a complex structure <math id="S8.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S8.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> we will denote by <math id="S8.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> the lift of <math id="S8.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> to any complex blow-up of <math id="S8.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly we will denote by
<math id="S8.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> the lift of the standard complex structure <math id="S8.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{st}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S8.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to any complex blow-up of <math id="S8.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S8.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}\in M</annotation></semantics></math> be distinct points in <math id="S8.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Pick <math id="S8.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k-1},y_{k}\in{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k-1},y_{k}\in{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the complex blow-ups <math id="S8.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{k-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{k-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S8.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,J_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,J_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S8.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S8.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},y_{k-1},y_{k}},{\widetilde{I}}_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},y_{k-1},y_{k}},{\widetilde{I}}_{st})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S8.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S8.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k-1},y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k-1},y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p8.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a biholomorphism <math id="S8.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi:({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{k-1}},{\widetilde{J}}_{0})\to(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},y_{k-1},y_{k}},{\widetilde{I}}_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi:({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{k-1}},{\widetilde{J}}_{0})\to(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},y_{k-1},y_{k}},{\widetilde{I}}_{st})</annotation></semantics></math> such that
the induced isomorphism</p>
<table id="S8.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{*}:H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},y_{k-1},y_{k}};{\mathbb{Z}})\to H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{Z}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{*}:H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},y_{k-1},y_{k}};{\mathbb{Z}})\to H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p8.2" class="ltx_p">identifies</p>
<table id="S8.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{M}^{*}b,\Pi_{M}^{*}f,e_{k-1,M}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{Z}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{M}^{*}b,\Pi_{M}^{*}f,e_{k-1,M}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p8.3" class="ltx_p">respectively with</p>
<table id="S8.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}},\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}},\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\in H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},y_{k-1},y_{k}};{\mathbb{Z}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}},\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}},\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\in H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},y_{k-1},y_{k}};{\mathbb{Z}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S8.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Identify the complement of the exceptional divisor in <math id="S8.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{k-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{k-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\setminus x_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\setminus x_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the complement of the union of the exceptional divisors in <math id="S8.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},y_{k-1},y_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},y_{k-1},y_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\{y_{k-1},y_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\{y_{k-1},y_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The restriction of
<math id="S8.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> to the complements defines then a map <math id="S8.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:M\setminus x_{k-1}\to{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\{y_{k-1},y_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:M\setminus x_{k-1}\to{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\setminus\{y_{k-1},y_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We say that the points <math id="S8.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}\in M\setminus x_{k-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}\in M\setminus x_{k-2}</annotation></semantics></math> are <span id="S8.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">in general position</span> if the points
<math id="S8.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(x_{1}),\ldots,\psi(x_{k-2}),y_{k-1},y_{k}\in{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(x_{1}),\ldots,\psi(x_{k-2}),y_{k-1},y_{k}\in{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
are distinct and in general position as <math id="S8.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> points of <math id="S8.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to see that this definition does not depend on the choices of <math id="S8.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Arguing as in the proofs of <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and
<a href="#S7" title="7 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, and using the fact that any two points of <math id="S8.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
can be mapped into each other by a biholomorphism of <math id="S8.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,J_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,J_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>,
we get the following claim.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 8.1 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p13.1" class="ltx_p">The set of tuples <math id="S8.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-2},x_{k-1})\in{\hat{M}}^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-2},x_{k-1})\in{\hat{M}}^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> that are in general position is connected
and dense in <math id="S8.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{M}}^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{M}}^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S8.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 8.2 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S8.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}\in M</annotation></semantics></math> are in general position.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Then the cohomology class</p>
<table id="S8.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{M}^{*}[\omega_{\mu}]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,M}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{M}^{*}[\omega_{\mu}]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,M}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p16.2" class="ltx_p">is Kähler with respect to <math id="S8.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{J}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{J}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the numbers <math id="S8.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the inequalities (v), (c1)-(c6) listed in <a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p17" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S8" title="8 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p18.1" class="ltx_p">The biholomorphism <math id="S8.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi:({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{k-1}},{\widetilde{J}}_{0})\to(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},y_{k-1},y_{k}},{\widetilde{I}}_{st})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi:({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{k-1}},{\widetilde{J}}_{0})\to(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},y_{k-1},y_{k}},{\widetilde{I}}_{st})</annotation></semantics></math>
induces a biholomorphism</p>
<table id="S8.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon:\left({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}},{\widetilde{J}}_{0}\right)\to\left(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},\psi(x_{1}),\ldots,\psi(x_{k-2}),y_{k-1},y_{k}},{\widetilde{I}}_{st}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon:\left({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}},{\widetilde{J}}_{0}\right)\to\left(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},\psi(x_{1}),\ldots,\psi(x_{k-2}),y_{k-1},y_{k}},{\widetilde{I}}_{st}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p18.2" class="ltx_p">that identifies the cohomology class</p>
<table id="S8.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{M}^{*}[\omega_{\mu}]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,M}=\pi\left(\mu\Pi_{M}^{*}b+\Pi_{M}^{*}f-\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,M}\right)\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{M}^{*}[\omega_{\mu}]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,M}=\pi\left(\mu\Pi_{M}^{*}b+\Pi_{M}^{*}f-\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,M}\right)\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p18.3" class="ltx_p">with</p>
<table id="S8.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\cdot\Big(\mu\left(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\right)+\left(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\right)-" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\cdot\Big(\mu\left(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\right)+\left(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\right)-</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S8.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-R_{k-1}^{2}\left(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\right)-\sum_{i=1}^{k-2}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\Big)\in H^{2}\left(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},\psi(x_{1}),\ldots,\psi(x_{k-2}),y_{k-1},y_{k}};{\mathbb{R}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-R_{k-1}^{2}\left(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\right)-\sum_{i=1}^{k-2}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\Big)\in H^{2}\left(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},\psi(x_{1}),\ldots,\psi(x_{k-2}),y_{k-1},y_{k}};{\mathbb{R}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p18.4" class="ltx_p">Recalling the description of <math id="S8.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi^{*}:H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},y_{k-1},y_{k}};{\mathbb{Z}})\to H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi^{*}:H^{2}(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},y_{k-1},y_{k}};{\mathbb{Z}})\to H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>
above and the fact that <math id="S8.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega_{FS}]=\pi h\in H^{2}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega_{FS}]=\pi h\in H^{2}({\mathbb{C}}P^{2};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>, we get that</p>
<table id="S8.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\cdot\Big(\mu\left(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\right)+\left(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\right)-" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\cdot\Big(\mu\left(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\right)+\left(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\right)-</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S8.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-R_{k-1}^{2}\left(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\right)-\sum_{i=1}^{k-2}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\Big)=" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-R_{k-1}^{2}\left(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\right)-\sum_{i=1}^{k-2}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\Big)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S8.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\cdot\Big(\left(\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}\right)\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-\sum_{i=1}^{k-2}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\cdot\Big(\left(\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}\right)\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}h-\sum_{i=1}^{k-2}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S8.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\left(\mu-R_{k-1}^{2}\right)e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-(1-R_{k-1}^{2})e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\Big)=" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\left(\mu-R_{k-1}^{2}\right)e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-(1-R_{k-1}^{2})e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\Big)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S8.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\left(\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}\right)\Bigg(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}(\pi h)-\pi\cdot\sum_{i=1}^{k-2}\frac{R_{i}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{i,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\left(\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}\right)\Bigg(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}(\pi h)-\pi\cdot\sum_{i=1}^{k-2}\frac{R_{i}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{i,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S8.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\frac{\mu-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-\frac{1-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\Bigg)=" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\frac{\mu-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-\frac{1-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\Bigg)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S8.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\left(\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}\right)\Bigg(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}[\omega_{FS}]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-2}\frac{R_{i}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{i,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\left(\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}\right)\Bigg(\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}[\omega_{FS}]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-2}\frac{R_{i}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{i,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S8.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\frac{\mu-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-\frac{1-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\Bigg)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\frac{\mu-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-\frac{1-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\Bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S8.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, the cohomology class</p>
<table id="S8.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{M}^{*}[\omega_{\mu}]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,M}=\pi\cdot\left(\mu\Pi_{M}^{*}b+\Pi_{M}^{*}f-\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,M}\right)\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{M}^{*}[\omega_{\mu}]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,M}=\pi\cdot\left(\mu\Pi_{M}^{*}b+\Pi_{M}^{*}f-\sum_{i=1}^{k-1}R_{i}^{2}e_{i,M}\right)\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{J_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{k-1}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p19.2" class="ltx_p">is Kähler with respect to <math id="S8.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{J}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{J}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if the cohomology class</p>
<table id="S8.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}[\omega_{FS}]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-2}\frac{R_{i}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{i,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-\frac{\mu-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}^{*}[\omega_{FS}]-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k-2}\frac{R_{i}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{i,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-\frac{\mu-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{k-1,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}-</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S8.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\frac{1-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\in H^{2}\left(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},\psi(x_{1}),\ldots,\psi(x_{k-2}),y_{k-1},y_{k}};{\mathbb{R}}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\frac{1-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}e_{k,{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}\in H^{2}\left(\widetilde{{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}}_{I_{st},\psi(x_{1}),\ldots,\psi(x_{k-2}),y_{k-1},y_{k}};{\mathbb{R}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p19.3" class="ltx_p">is Kähler with respect to <math id="S8.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}_{st}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}_{st}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the numbers</p>
<table id="S8.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{R_{1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\ldots,\frac{R_{k-2}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\frac{\mu-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\frac{1-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{R_{1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\ldots,\frac{R_{k-2}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\frac{\mu-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\frac{1-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p19.4" class="ltx_p">sorted in non-increasing order,
are exactly <math id="S8.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the points <math id="S8.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(x_{1}),\ldots,\psi(x_{k-2}),y_{k-1},y_{k}\in{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(x_{1}),\ldots,\psi(x_{k-2}),y_{k-1},y_{k}\in{\mathbb{C}}P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
are in general position, the proposition follows from
<a href="#S6" title="6 The case of complex projective spaces and their products ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
<span id="S8.p19.4.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Now we are ready to prove <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> – let us recall it here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p21" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8.3 (=<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S8.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S8.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> be as above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S8.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset(M,J_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset(M,J_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> be either a proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold or <math id="S8.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=(\textrm{pt}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times\textrm{pt})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>pt</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=(\textrm{pt}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\cup({\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\times\textrm{pt})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p24.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p25" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p25.1" class="ltx_p">A. Assume <math id="S8.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq k\leq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq k\leq 8</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S8.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},R_{2},\ldots,R_{k-2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},R_{2},\ldots,R_{k-2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;R_{k-1}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;R_{k-1}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the numbers</p>
<table id="S8.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{R_{1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\frac{R_{2}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\ldots,\frac{\mu-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\frac{1-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{R_{1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\frac{R_{2}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\ldots,\frac{\mu-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},\frac{1-R_{k-1}^{2}}{\mu+1-R_{k-1}^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.p25.2" class="ltx_p">sort them in the non-increasing order and denote the resulting <math id="S8.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> numbers by <math id="S8.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}\geq\ldots\geq r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p26.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following conditions are equivalent:</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p27" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S8.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There exists a <math id="S8.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding
<math id="S8.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S8.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
that is compatible with
<math id="S8.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and isotopic to <math id="S8.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by an isotopy preserving <math id="S8.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (as a set).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The numbers <math id="S8.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> defined above satisfy the inequalities in part A of
<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S8.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p28.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S8.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the inequalities (v), (c1)-(c6) in part A of <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, any symplectic embedding
<math id="S8.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k-1}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> is, in fact, of <math id="S8.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p29" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p29.1" class="ltx_p">B. Assume that <math id="S8.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p30.1" class="ltx_p">Then for any <math id="S8.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
any symplectic embedding of a disjoint union of <math id="S8.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2l^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2l^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> equal balls into <math id="S8.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>
is, in fact, of <math id="S8.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler type, and such symplectic (or, equivalently, <math id="S8.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type) embeddings are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p31.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely, if <math id="S8.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2l^{2}{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{4}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2l^{2}{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{4}(r),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists a Kähler-type embedding of <math id="S8.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{2l^{2}}B^{4}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{2l^{2}}B^{4}(r)</annotation></semantics></math>
into <math id="S8.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on <math id="S8.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> that is compatible with <math id="S8.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
and isotopic to <math id="S8.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
by an isotopy preserving <math id="S8.p31.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (as a set).</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p32" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p32.1" class="ltx_p">C. For any <math id="S8.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\ldots,R_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\ldots,R_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S8.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> (if they exist!) lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S8.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math>-action, meaning that
the space of Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S8.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(R_{i})\to(M\setminus\Sigma,\omega_{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math> is connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p33" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S8" title="8 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> (=<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>):</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p34.1" class="ltx_p">As we already explained in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 The cases of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ and ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, part B of the theorem follows from <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and part C follows from part (I) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p35.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of part A
follows the proof of part A of <a href="#S7" title="7 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, with
<a href="#S8" title="8 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^1×ℂ⁢𝑃^1 ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>
used instead of
<a href="#S7" title="7 The case of ℂ⁢𝑃^2⁢♯⁢(ℂ⁢𝑃^2)̄ ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
<span id="S8.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we discuss Kähler-type embeddings of balls and other domains into symplectic manifolds admitting Kähler-type complex structures with “few” complex subvarieties. The relevant claims about such complex structures on tori, K3 surfaces and IHS-hyperkähler manifolds will be discussed in Sections <a href="#S10" title="10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and <a href="#S12" title="12 Hyperkähler case – proofs ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>.
The results of Sections <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 The IHS-hyperkähler case ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> on the existence of
Kähler-type embeddings of disjoint unions of balls into tori, K3 surfaces and IHS-hyperkähler manifolds will then follow as particular cases of the general
result (<a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) stated further on in this section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As before, let <math id="S9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">dim</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}=2n</annotation></semantics></math>, be a closed connected manifold equipped with a Kähler-type symplectic form <math id="S9.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let us recall the definition of a Campana-simple complex structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S9.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is a Kähler-type complex structure on <math id="S9.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S9.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the union of all positive-dimensional proper complex
subvarieties of <math id="S9.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>. The set <math id="S9.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
either a countable union of proper analytic subvarieties of <math id="S9.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> (and hence has a dense connected complement) or the whole <math id="S9.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Remark 4.2]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p5.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S9.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a countable union of proper analytic subvarieties of <math id="S9.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, the complex structure <math id="S9.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is called <span id="S9.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Campana-simple</span> and
the points of <math id="S9.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\setminus{\mathfrak{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>𝔘</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\setminus{\mathfrak{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> are called <span id="S9.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Campana-generic with respect to <math id="S9.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Examples of manifolds with
a Campana-simple complex structure
include the generic complex tori of complex dimension <math id="S9.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>
(see <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>), as well as generic K3 surfaces (see <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>),
and generic deformations of hyperkähler manifolds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ver4</a>]</cite>.
One can show that a torus equipped with a Campana-simple complex structure does not admit <span id="S9.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">any</span> positive-dimensional
proper complex subvarieties (see <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>), and the same holds for a generic complex K3 surface <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ver5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 9.1 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p8.1" class="ltx_p">1. If <math id="S9.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is Campana-simple, then the complex manifold <math id="S9.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is not projective, since otherwise it would have admitted a globally defined meromorphic function
<math id="S9.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and could have been represented as the union of zero divisors of the functions <math id="S9.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f-a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f-a</annotation></semantics></math>, for all
<math id="S9.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the zero divisor of
<math id="S9.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the symplectic form <math id="S9.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with a Campana-simple complex structure cannot be rational – otherwise,
for a Campana-simple complex structure <math id="S9.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S9.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> the complex manifold <math id="S9.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> would have been projective,
by the Kodaira embedding theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ko</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p9.1" class="ltx_p">2. Conjecturally (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cam</a>, Question 1.4]</cite>,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">CaDV</a>, Conj. 1.1]</cite>), any closed connected manifold equipped with a Campana-simple complex structure is bimeromorphic to a
hyperkähler orbifold or a finite quotient of a torus.
For complex threefolds this was proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">HöP</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Campana-simple
complex structures on <math id="S9.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> form a <math id="S9.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Diff</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant subset of the set of all Kähler-type complex structures on <math id="S9.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 9.2 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S9.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)\subset{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)\subset{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
the set of points of
<math id="S9.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> that can be represented by Campana-simple complex structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p13.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S9.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected component of <math id="S9.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by <math id="S9.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}^{s}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)\subset{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}^{s}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)\subset{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
the set of points of
<math id="S9.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> that can be represented by Campana-simple
complex structures.
In other words,</p>
<table id="S9.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}^{s}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)={\tt Teich}^{s}(M)\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}^{s}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)={\tt Teich}^{s}(M)\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S9.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 9.3 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S9.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be
a connected component of <math id="S9.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S9.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S9.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{r}}=(r_{1},\ldots,r_{k})\in({\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0})^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{r}}=(r_{1},\ldots,r_{k})\in({\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0})^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S9.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p18.1" class="ltx_p">(I)
Let <math id="S9.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S9.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=2n</annotation></semantics></math>, be a closed manifold.
Assume that <math id="S9.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic form on <math id="S9.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with a Campana-simple complex structure <math id="S9.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S9.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Then Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S9.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p20.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely, if <math id="S9.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>,
then <math id="S9.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> admits an <math id="S9.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding into <math id="S9.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p21" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p21.1" class="ltx_p">(II) Assume that
<math id="S9.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense connected subset of <math id="S9.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Then any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S9.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> favoring <math id="S9.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>
lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S9.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-action.
In particular, there exists <math id="S9.p22.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
such that both embeddings are of <math id="S9.p22.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p23.1" class="ltx_p">If, in addition, <math id="S9.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on the set of connected components of <math id="S9.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S9.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, then
any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S9.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S9.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p24.1" class="ltx_p">For the proof of <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>
we need the following claim.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p25" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 9.4 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p26.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S9.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is a Campana-simple complex structure on <math id="S9.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, a cohomology class <math id="S9.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S9.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the points <math id="S9.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M</annotation></semantics></math> are Campana-generic with respect to <math id="S9.p26.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S9.p26.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p27.1" class="ltx_p">Then the cohomology class</p>
<table id="S9.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}:=\Pi^{*}\alpha-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}:=\Pi^{*}\alpha-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.p27.2" class="ltx_p">is Kähler with respect to <math id="S9.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S9.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}^{n}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}^{n}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
or, equivalently,
if and only if</p>
<table id="S9.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;\langle\alpha^{n},[M]\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;\langle\alpha^{n},[M]\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S9.p28" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p29.1" class="ltx_p">This is proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Thm. 8.6]</cite> – the proof is a straightforward application of the Demailly-Paun theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP</a>, Thm. 0.1]</cite> describing the Kähler cone of an arbitrary closed manifold equipped with a Kähler-type complex structure.
<span id="S9.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p30.1" class="ltx_p">Now we can prove <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p31" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p32.1" class="ltx_p">The complex structure <math id="S9.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in the statement of part (I) of the theorem is compatible with <math id="S9.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence,
the cohomology class <math id="S9.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha:=[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha:=[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S9.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p33.1" class="ltx_p">Pick points <math id="S9.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M\setminus\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{k}\in M\setminus\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> that are Campana-generic with respect to <math id="S9.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is possible to pick these points in the complement of <math id="S9.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, because the set of Campana-generic points of <math id="S9.p33.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is dense in <math id="S9.p33.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
(see above)
and <math id="S9.p33.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper complex submanifold of <math id="S9.p33.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Set <math id="S9.p33.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕩</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbbm{x}}:=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p34.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that</p>
<table id="S9.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)=\langle\alpha^{n},[M]\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)=\langle\alpha^{n},[M]\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.p34.2" class="ltx_p">Then <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> yields that <math id="S9.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}:=\Pi^{*}\alpha-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕩</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\alpha}}_{\bar{r}}:=\Pi^{*}\alpha-\pi\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\in H^{2}({\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\mathbbm{x}}};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S9.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now
the claim
of part (I)
follows from part (I) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p35.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove part (II).</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p36.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S9.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense connected subset of <math id="S9.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The argument we have just used in the proof of part (I) shows that <math id="S9.p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore</p>
<table id="S9.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S9.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)\subset{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)\subset{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S9.p36.2" class="ltx_p">Hence, <math id="S9.p36.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝙺</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{K}}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\bar{r}})</annotation></semantics></math> is also a dense connected
subset of <math id="S9.p36.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now part (II) of <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Kähler-type embeddings of balls in general symplectic manifolds and Kähler classes on complex blow-ups ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> yields the needed claim.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p37.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof of <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.
<span id="S9.p37.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10 </span>The case of tori and K3 surfaces</h2>

<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we discuss Campana-simple complex structures on tori and K3 surfaces and show how to deduce <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>
from
<a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S10.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.1 </span>Campana-simple complex structures on tori</h3>

<div id="S10.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}\subset\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}\subset\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> the set of linear complex structures on <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> that are compatible with the standard orientation.
It is naturally identified with the space of linear complex structures on <math id="S10.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is well-known (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDS</a>, Prop. 2.5.2]</cite>) that the space <math id="S10.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> can be identified with
<math id="S10.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL^{+}(2n,{\mathbb{R}})/GL(n,{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL^{+}(2n,{\mathbb{R}})/GL(n,{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> which is a connected smooth manifold.
Here the group <math id="S10.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(n,{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(n,{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> is embedded in <math id="S10.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL(2n,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL(2n,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> by the map</p>
<table id="S10.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\mapsto\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\textrm{Re}\ C&amp;-\textrm{Im}\ C\\
\textrm{Im}\ C&amp;\textrm{Re}\ C\\
\end{array}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mtext>Re</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>Im</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mtext>Im</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mtext>Re</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\mapsto\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}\textrm{Re}\ C&amp;-\textrm{Im}\ C\\
\textrm{Im}\ C&amp;\textrm{Re}\ C\\
\end{array}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The tangent space <math id="S10.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{J}{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{J}{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S10.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> at a point
<math id="S10.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\in{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\in{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of all real <math id="S10.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n\times 2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n\times 2n</annotation></semantics></math> matrices <math id="S10.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S10.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RJ+JR=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RJ+JR=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The complex structure on <math id="S10.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{J}{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{J}{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S10.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\mapsto JR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\mapsto JR</annotation></semantics></math> (since <math id="S10.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL^{+}(2n,{\mathbb{R}})/GL(n,{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL^{+}(2n,{\mathbb{R}})/GL(n,{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric space, this
almost complex structure on <math id="S10.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> is indeed integrable
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bes</a>]</cite>).
Thus, further on <math id="S10.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> will be viewed as a connected complex manifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following claim is an easy exercise (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Sec. 6]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.1 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The space <math id="S10.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{2n}\times{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{2n}\times{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math>
can be equipped with a complex structure so that
<math id="S10.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{2n}\times{\it Lin}\to{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{2n}\times{\it Lin}\to{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a complex-analytic deformation family such that for each <math id="S10.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\in{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\in{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> the complex structure on the fiber over <math id="S10.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S10.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> itself.
<span id="S10.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S10.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math> denote by <math id="S10.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{2l}</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of exterior <math id="S10.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2l</annotation></semantics></math>-forms on <math id="S10.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> and by <math id="S10.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{2l}_{\mathbb{Q}}\subset\Lambda^{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{2l}_{\mathbb{Q}}\subset\Lambda^{2l}</annotation></semantics></math>
the space of exterior <math id="S10.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2l</annotation></semantics></math>-forms on <math id="S10.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> with rational coefficients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a linear symplectic form on <math id="S10.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="S10.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\omega}}\in\Lambda^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\omega}}\in\Lambda^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> its lift to <math id="S10.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> – it is a linear symplectic form on <math id="S10.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For a linear complex structure <math id="S10.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S10.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote by <math id="S10.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{J}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{J}}</annotation></semantics></math> the linear complex structure on <math id="S10.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> which is the lift of <math id="S10.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.2 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The cohomology class <math id="S10.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> of the linear symplectic form <math id="S10.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to a linear complex structure <math id="S10.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S10.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S10.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S10.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> then, obviously, <math id="S10.SS1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S10.SS1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> and let us prove that <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> – i.e., <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler on <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},J)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a Kähler form <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},J)</annotation></semantics></math> cohomologous to <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Viewing <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> as a compact Lie group and averaging <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get a <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant – hence, linear – form <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> which is Kähler on <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},J)</annotation></semantics></math> and cohomologous to <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Since there is only one linear
form representing a given cohomology class, we get that <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta=\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta=\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler on <math id="S10.SS1.p13.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},J)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof of the proposition.
<span id="S10.SS1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S10.SS1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
the subset of <math id="S10.SS1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> formed by the linear complex structures on <math id="S10.SS1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with the linear symplectic form <math id="S10.SS1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is naturally identified with the space of the linear complex structures on <math id="S10.SS1.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\omega}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S10.SS1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="S10.SS1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{2l}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{2l}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by <math id="S10.SS1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{a}\subset{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{a}\subset{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> the set of <math id="S10.SS1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\in{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\in{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S10.SS1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H_{J}^{l,l}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H_{J}^{l,l}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>. It can also be viewed as the Hodge locus of <math id="S10.SS1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> for the complex-analytic deformation
family <math id="S10.SS1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{2n}\times{\it Lin}\to{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{2n}\times{\it Lin}\to{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> given by <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>.
Consequently, <math id="S10.SS1.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex subvariety of <math id="S10.SS1.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> – see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voi</a>, Vol. 2, Lem. 5.13]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p17" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 10.3 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a linear symplectic form on <math id="S10.SS1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=[\omega]\in H^{2}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=[\omega]\in H^{2}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the set <math id="S10.SS1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of linear complex structures on <math id="S10.SS1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> preserving <math id="S10.SS1.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> (because a real cohomology class of degree 2 is of type <math id="S10.SS1.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to a complex structure if and only if it is preserved by it). In this case <math id="S10.SS1.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth complex submanifold of <math id="S10.SS1.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> and this can be verified by elementary means.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, first of all, <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDS</a>, Lem. 2.5.5]</cite>). If <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\in{\mathcal{T}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\in{\mathcal{T}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{J}{\mathcal{T}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{J}{\mathcal{T}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>
is formed by <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n\times 2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n\times 2n</annotation></semantics></math> matrices <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the two conditions: <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RJ+JR=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RJ+JR=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{t}AR=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{t}AR=0</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is the matrix of the linear symplectic form <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\omega}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{t}AJ=A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{t}AJ=A</annotation></semantics></math> (because <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\in{\mathcal{T}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\in{\mathcal{T}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="JR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">JR</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the same two conditions meaning that <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{J}{\mathcal{T}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{J}{\mathcal{T}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex vector subspace of <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{J}{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{J}{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math>. This means that <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex submanifold of <math id="S10.SS1.p20.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p21.1" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="S10.SS1.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is then an open contractible submanifold of <math id="S10.SS1.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDS</a>, Lem. 2.5.5]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p22" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.4 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Any Campana-simple complex structure on <math id="S10.SS1.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> admits no proper positive-dimensional complex subvarieties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p24" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p25.1" class="ltx_p">A Campana-simple complex structure on <math id="S10.SS1.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> is of Kähler type and therefore can be mapped by a diffeomorphism
of <math id="S10.SS1.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> into a linear complex structure (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Prop. 6.1]</cite>),
which is also Campana-simple. Thus, it suffices to prove the proposition for a Campana-simple linear complex structure
<math id="S10.SS1.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p26.1" class="ltx_p">Assume, by contradiction, that such a <math id="S10.SS1.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>
admits a positive-dimensional proper complex subvariety.
The parallel translations of <math id="S10.SS1.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> preserve <math id="S10.SS1.p26.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore any point of <math id="S10.SS1.p26.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the image of that subvariety under a parallel translation, which contradicts the assumption that <math id="S10.SS1.p26.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is Campana-simple.
This finishes the proof of the proposition.
<span id="S10.SS1.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p27.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S10.SS1.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}^{s}\subset{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}^{s}\subset{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> the space of linear Campana-simple complex structures on <math id="S10.SS1.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p28" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.5 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p29.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS1.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a linear symplectic form on <math id="S10.SS1.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p30.1" class="ltx_p">Then
<math id="S10.SS1.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with a Campana-simple linear complex structure (i.e., <math id="S10.SS1.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}^{s}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}^{s}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>) if and only if <math id="S10.SS1.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational. Moreover, in this case <math id="S10.SS1.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}^{s}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}^{s}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense connected subset of <math id="S10.SS1.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p31" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p32.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S10.SS1.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is rational.
Then, by part 1 of
<a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, there are no Campana-simple complex structures compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p33.1" class="ltx_p">Now assume <math id="S10.SS1.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational, or, equivalently, that <math id="S10.SS1.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\omega}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a real multiple of a form in <math id="S10.SS1.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{2}_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{2}_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let us prove that <math id="S10.SS1.p33.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}^{s}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}^{s}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense connected subset of <math id="S10.SS1.p33.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p34.1" class="ltx_p">We start with the following lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p35" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p35.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10.6 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p36.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that a linear symplectic form <math id="S10.SS1.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is not a real multiple of a form in <math id="S10.SS1.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{2}_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{2}_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p37.1" class="ltx_p">Then for all <math id="S10.SS1.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,\ldots,n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,\ldots,n-1</annotation></semantics></math> the form <math id="S10.SS1.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a real multiple of a form in <math id="S10.SS1.p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{2l}_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{2l}_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p38" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p38.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p38.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p39.1" class="ltx_p">Assume by contradiction that <math id="S10.SS1.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is a real multiple of a form in <math id="S10.SS1.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{2}_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{2}_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S10.SS1.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectic, <math id="S10.SS1.p39.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{l}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{l}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and we can assume without loss of generality that <math id="S10.SS1.p39.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{l}\in\Lambda^{2l}_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{l}\in\Lambda^{2l}_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p40.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the Galois group <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}({\mathbb{C}}/{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}({\mathbb{C}}/{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the group of field automorphisms of <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and its action on the coefficients of <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the standard basis of <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee</a>]</cite> that if <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{l}=\xi^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{l}=\xi^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\in\Lambda^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\in\Lambda^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,\ldots,n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,\ldots,n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi=\pm\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi=\pm\eta</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that the action of each element of <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}({\mathbb{C}}/{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}({\mathbb{C}}/{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> either fixes all the coefficients of <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> or multiplies each of them by <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The complex numbers preserved by the action of the whole group <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}({\mathbb{C}}/{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}({\mathbb{C}}/{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> are exactly the rationals (a proof of this well-known fact can be easily extracted e.g. from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx104" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yale</a>]</cite>). Therefore the square of each coefficient of <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> lies in <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This easily implies that there exists a positive <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the field <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}[\sqrt{r}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msqrt><mi>r</mi></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}[\sqrt{r}]</annotation></semantics></math> contains all the coefficients of <math id="S10.SS1.p40.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p41.1" class="ltx_p">If all elements <math id="S10.SS1.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}({\mathbb{C}}/{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}({\mathbb{C}}/{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> fix all the coefficients of <math id="S10.SS1.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, we get that <math id="S10.SS1.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in\Lambda^{2}_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in\Lambda^{2}_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>, in contradiction to the hypothesis of the lemma. Therefore there exists an element of <math id="S10.SS1.p41.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Gal}({\mathbb{C}}/{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Gal</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Gal}({\mathbb{C}}/{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> whose action multiplies all the coefficients of <math id="S10.SS1.p41.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S10.SS1.p41.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that all these coefficients are rational multiples of <math id="S10.SS1.p41.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>r</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S10.SS1.p41.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive real multiple of a form in <math id="S10.SS1.p41.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{2}_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{2}_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which contradicts again the hypothesis of the lemma. Thus, the assumption that <math id="S10.SS1.p41.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is a real multiple of a form in <math id="S10.SS1.p41.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{2}_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{2}_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> has led us to a contradiction, which means that it is false. This proves the lemma.
<span id="S10.SS1.p41.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p42" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p42.1" class="ltx_p">Since
<math id="S10.SS1.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\omega}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a real multiple of a form in <math id="S10.SS1.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{2}_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{2}_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a> yields that for each <math id="S10.SS1.p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,\ldots,n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,\ldots,n-1</annotation></semantics></math> the form <math id="S10.SS1.p42.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\omega}}^{l}\in\Lambda^{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\omega}}^{l}\in\Lambda^{2l}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a real multiple of a form in <math id="S10.SS1.p42.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda^{2l}_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda^{2l}_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p43.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS1.p43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{2l}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{2l}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS1.p43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS1.p43.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,\ldots,n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,\ldots,n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and let us view <math id="S10.SS1.p43.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{2l}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{2l}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> as an exterior <math id="S10.SS1.p43.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2l</annotation></semantics></math>-form on <math id="S10.SS1.p43.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> with rational coefficients: <math id="S10.SS1.p43.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Lambda^{2l}_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Lambda^{2l}_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also view <math id="S10.SS1.p43.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> as the set of linear complex structures on <math id="S10.SS1.p43.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p43.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\omega}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p44" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p44.1" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="S10.SS1.p44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{a}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{a}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex subvariety of
the complex manifold <math id="S10.SS1.p44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p45.1" class="ltx_p">We claim that this complex subvariety is proper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p46.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, assume, by contradiction, that it is not. Then, since <math id="S10.SS1.p46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is connected, <math id="S10.SS1.p46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{a}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)={\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{a}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)={\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>,
meaning that any linear complex structure on <math id="S10.SS1.p46.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p46.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\omega}}</annotation></semantics></math> also preserves the exterior <math id="S10.SS1.p46.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2l</annotation></semantics></math>-form <math id="S10.SS1.p46.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p47.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\subset SL(2n,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\subset SL(2n,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> the group of linear automorphisms of <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> preserving <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\omega}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\omega}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is isomorphic, as a real Lie group, to <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Sp(2n,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Sp(2n,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The set <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant under conjugations in <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and thus generates a normal subgroup <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. But <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\cong Sp(2n,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\cong Sp(2n,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a simple Lie group and therefore each normal
subgroup of <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has to either lie in its center (equal to <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{Id,-Id\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{Id,-Id\}</annotation></semantics></math>) or coincide with the whole <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rag</a>]</cite>). The former option is clearly not satisfied for <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore we get that <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=G</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is preserved by the whole group <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. By a theorem of Weyl <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weyl</a>, Ch. VI, Sec. 1]</cite> (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">GV</a>, Sect. 5.3.2]</cite> for a modern presentation), the only exterior <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2l</annotation></semantics></math>-forms preserved by the whole <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> are the real multiples of <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\omega}}^{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\omega}}^{2l}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Lambda^{2l}_{\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>ℚ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Lambda^{2l}_{\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a real multiple of <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\omega}}^{2l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\omega}}^{2l}</annotation></semantics></math> which, as we have seen above, is a not a real multiple of any form with rational coefficients. We have obtained a contradiction, and this proves the claim that
<math id="S10.SS1.p47.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{a}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{a}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper complex subvariety of
of the complex manifold <math id="S10.SS1.p47.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p48.1" class="ltx_p">The claim we have proved implies that the
complement in the connected complex manifold <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> of the countable union of proper complex subvarieties <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}_{a}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}_{a}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{2l}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{2l}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,\ldots,n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,\ldots,n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, is connected and dense in <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Any complex structure <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> in this complement is Campana-simple. Indeed, the <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2l</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional complex subvarieties of <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},J)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,\ldots,n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,\ldots,n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, all have non-zero rational fundamental classes; the corresponding Poincaré-dual non-zero rational cohomology classes are of type <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(l,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(l,l)</annotation></semantics></math> (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voi</a>, Vol. 1, Sec. 11]</cite>), which is impossible since <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\notin{\mathcal{T}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\notin{\mathcal{T}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{2l}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{2l}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},J)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},J)</annotation></semantics></math> has no complex subvarieties of dimension <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2l</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,\ldots,n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,\ldots,n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that <math id="S10.SS1.p48.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is Campana-simple.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p49" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p49.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, the set <math id="S10.SS1.p49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}^{s}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}^{s}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> of Campana-simple complex structures lying in <math id="S10.SS1.p49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> contains a subset which is connected and dense in <math id="S10.SS1.p49.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S10.SS1.p49.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}^{s}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}^{s}\cap{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is also connected and dense in <math id="S10.SS1.p49.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p50" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p50.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof.
<span id="S10.SS1.p50.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p51" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p51.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S10.SS1.p51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected, there is a connected component of <math id="S10.SS1.p51.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> containing it – denote this connected component by <math id="S10.SS1.p51.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p52" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p52.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS1.p52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=0\in{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=0\in{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S10.SS1.p52.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Diff</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)</annotation></semantics></math> be a subgroup of <math id="S10.SS1.p52.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Diff</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> formed by the diffeomorphisms of <math id="S10.SS1.p52.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> fixing <math id="S10.SS1.p52.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S10.SS1.p52.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)</annotation></semantics></math> be the identity component of <math id="S10.SS1.p52.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Diff</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)</annotation></semantics></math>. The group <math id="S10.SS1.p52.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)</annotation></semantics></math> lies in <math id="S10.SS1.p52.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> and is, in fact, the identity component of the subgroup of
<math id="S10.SS1.p52.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> formed by the elements of <math id="S10.SS1.p52.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> fixing <math id="S10.SS1.p52.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p53" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p53.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS1.p53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x):=\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})\cap\mathrm{Diff}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>Diff</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x):=\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})\cap\mathrm{Diff}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly, <math id="S10.SS1.p53.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p54" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p54.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p54.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.7 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p55" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p55.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS1.p55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p56" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p56.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p57" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p57.1" class="ltx_p">A. There exist unique <math id="S10.SS1.p57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{I}\in{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{I}\in{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p57.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{I}\in\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{I}\in\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S10.SS1.p57.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{I}=\phi_{I}^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{I}=\phi_{I}^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p58" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p58.1" class="ltx_p">B. The complex structure <math id="S10.SS1.p58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and the diffeomorphism <math id="S10.SS1.p58.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> depend
continuously on <math id="S10.SS1.p58.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p59" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p59.1" class="ltx_p">C. If <math id="S10.SS1.p59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S10.SS1.p59.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{I}\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{I}\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p60" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p60.1" class="ltx_p">D. If <math id="S10.SS1.p60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p60.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{*}I\in{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{*}I\in{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S10.SS1.p60.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S10.SS1.p60.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{*}I=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{*}I=I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p61" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p61.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p61.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p62" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p62.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove part A of the proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p63" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p63.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of the existence of <math id="S10.SS1.p63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p63.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> goes as in the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Prop. 6.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p64" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p64.1" class="ltx_p">Namely, recall the construction of the Albanese map: Let <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{I}^{1}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{I}^{1}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of holomorphic complex-valued holomorphic 1-forms on the complex
manifold <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I)</annotation></semantics></math>. It is a complex vector space of dimension <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> that can be identified with <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{I}^{1,0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{I}^{1,0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n};{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Pick a basis
<math id="S10.SS1.p64.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varsigma_{1},\ldots,\varsigma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ς</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ς</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varsigma_{1},\ldots,\varsigma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{I}^{1}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{I}^{1}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>. The Albanese map <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\rm Alb}\,}_{I}:{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\Gamma_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Alb</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\rm Alb}\,}_{I}:{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\Gamma_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>
is defined by the map <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\mapsto\left(\int_{\gamma}\varsigma_{1},\ldots,\int_{\gamma}\varsigma_{n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mi>γ</mi></msub><msub><mi>ς</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>γ</mi></msub><msub><mi>ς</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\mapsto\left(\int_{\gamma}\varsigma_{1},\ldots,\int_{\gamma}\varsigma_{n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is an arbitrary smooth path from <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is a lattice in <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> formed by the vectors <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\int_{\gamma}\varsigma_{1},\ldots,\int_{\gamma}\varsigma_{n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∫</mo><mi>γ</mi></msub><msub><mi>ς</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>γ</mi></msub><msub><mi>ς</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\int_{\gamma}\varsigma_{1},\ldots,\int_{\gamma}\varsigma_{n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>
for all the loops <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. The Albanese map <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\rm Alb}\,}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Alb</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\rm Alb}\,}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is a biholomorphism between <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I)</annotation></semantics></math> and the complex torus <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\Gamma_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\Gamma_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> with
the complex structure induced by the standard complex structure on <math id="S10.SS1.p64.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> – see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voi</a>, Vol. 1, Def. 12.10 and Thm.
12.15]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p65" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p65.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S10.SS1.p65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{I}:{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{I}:{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> the natural projection
and let <math id="S10.SS1.p65.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{I}:{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{I}:{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S10.SS1.p65.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear isomorphism of
vector spaces mapping <math id="S10.SS1.p65.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S10.SS1.p65.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S10.SS1.p65.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> covers a
diffeomorphism <math id="S10.SS1.p65.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{f}}_{I}:T^{2n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\Gamma_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{f}}_{I}:T^{2n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\Gamma_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>,
i.e., <math id="S10.SS1.p65.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{f}}_{I}\circ\pi_{I}=\pi_{I}\circ F_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{f}}_{I}\circ\pi_{I}=\pi_{I}\circ F_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.
One easily sees that <math id="S10.SS1.p65.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> can be chosen in such a way that <math id="S10.SS1.p65.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{f}}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{f}}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> induces the same map <math id="S10.SS1.p65.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}({\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n})\to H^{*}({\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}({\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n})\to H^{*}({\mathbb{C}}^{n}/\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>
as <math id="S10.SS1.p65.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\rm Alb}\,}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Alb</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\rm Alb}\,}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p66" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p66.1" class="ltx_p">Clearly, <math id="S10.SS1.p66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{f}}_{I}^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{f}}_{I}^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear complex structure on
<math id="S10.SS1.p66.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{2n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{2n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="S10.SS1.p66.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{I}:={\bar{f}}_{I}^{-1}\circ{{\rm Alb}\,}_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>Alb</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{I}:={\bar{f}}_{I}^{-1}\circ{{\rm Alb}\,}_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is a
diffeomorphism mapping <math id="S10.SS1.p66.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> to a linear complex structure <math id="S10.SS1.p66.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S10.SS1.p66.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, acting as identity on <math id="S10.SS1.p66.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(T^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(T^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> and sending <math id="S10.SS1.p66.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=0</annotation></semantics></math> to itself.
Thus, <math id="S10.SS1.p66.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{I}\in\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{I}\in\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves the existence of <math id="S10.SS1.p66.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p66.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p67" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p67.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove the uniqueness of <math id="S10.SS1.p67.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p67.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. It suffices to show that if <math id="S10.SS1.p67.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0},J_{1}\in{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0},J_{1}\in{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p67.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}J_{0}=J_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}J_{0}=J_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S10.SS1.p67.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S10.SS1.p67.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}=J_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}=J_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p67.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi=Id</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p68" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p68.1" class="ltx_p">Denote the lifts of <math id="S10.SS1.p68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0},J_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0},J_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S10.SS1.p68.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively by <math id="S10.SS1.p68.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{J}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{J}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS1.p68.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{J}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{J}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The diffeomorphism <math id="S10.SS1.p68.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to a diffeomorphism <math id="S10.SS1.p68.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\phi}}:{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\phi}}:{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS1.p68.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\phi}}^{*}{\overline{J}}_{0}={\overline{J}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\phi}}^{*}{\overline{J}}_{0}={\overline{J}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and for each <math id="S10.SS1.p68.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists <math id="S10.SS1.p68.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime}\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime}\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S10.SS1.p68.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\phi}}(x+v)={\bar{\phi}}(x)+v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\phi}}(x+v)={\bar{\phi}}(x)+v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S10.SS1.p68.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p69" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p69.1" class="ltx_p">The group <math id="S10.SS1.p69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL^{+}(2n,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL^{+}(2n,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on <math id="S10.SS1.p69.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDS</a>]</cite>, Prop. 2.5.2). Therefore there exists
<math id="S10.SS1.p69.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in GL^{+}(2n,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in GL^{+}(2n,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>
so that <math id="S10.SS1.p69.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{*}{\overline{J}}_{1}={\overline{J}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{*}{\overline{J}}_{1}={\overline{J}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S10.SS1.p69.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma:=F({\mathbb{Z}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma:=F({\mathbb{Z}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> is a lattice in <math id="S10.SS1.p69.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p69.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F{\bar{\phi}}:{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F{\bar{\phi}}:{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a diffeomorphism preserving <math id="S10.SS1.p69.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{J}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{J}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> so that for each <math id="S10.SS1.p69.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists <math id="S10.SS1.p69.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{\prime\prime}\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{\prime\prime}\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S10.SS1.p69.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F{\bar{\phi}}(x+v)=F{\bar{\phi}}(x)+v^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F{\bar{\phi}}(x+v)=F{\bar{\phi}}(x)+v^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S10.SS1.p69.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p70" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p70.1" class="ltx_p">Using an appropriate linear change of coordinates on <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can assume without loss of generality that <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{J}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{J}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard linear complex structure on <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> coming from the identification <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}\cong{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}\cong{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
and thus <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F{\bar{\phi}}:{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F{\bar{\phi}}:{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a holomorphic map. The partial derivatives of this map with respect to the complex coordinates on <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant and holomorphic, hence descend to holomorphic maps on the standard complex torus <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence are constant. Therefore <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F{\bar{\phi}}:{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F{\bar{\phi}}:{\mathbb{C}}^{n}\to{\mathbb{C}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex-linear affine map and consequently <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\phi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\phi}}</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\phi}}(x)=Ax+c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\phi}}(x)=Ax+c</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in SL(2n,{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in SL(2n,{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> acts trivially on <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>, the matrix <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is just the identity and <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\phi}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\phi}}</annotation></semantics></math> is just a parallel translation by <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is a parallel translation too, and since <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> preserves <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi=Id</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently <math id="S10.SS1.p70.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}=J_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}=J_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p71" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p71.1" class="ltx_p">This proves the uniqueness of <math id="S10.SS1.p71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p71.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and finishes the proof of part A.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p72" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p72.1" class="ltx_p">Part B follows from <a href="#S13.SS1" title="13.1 Dependence of the Hodge decomposition on the complex structure ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.1</span></a>
and from the construction of <math id="S10.SS1.p72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p72.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> in part A.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p73" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p73.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove part C. Assume <math id="S10.SS1.p73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then it can be connected to some <math id="S10.SS1.p73.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\in{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\in{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> by a continuous path <math id="S10.SS1.p73.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{I_{t}\}\subset{\mathcal{C}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{I_{t}\}\subset{\mathcal{C}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>. Considering the path <math id="S10.SS1.p73.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{I_{t}}\}\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{I_{t}}\}\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is
continuous
by part B, and noting that <math id="S10.SS1.p73.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{J}=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{J}=Id</annotation></semantics></math>, we immediately obtain that <math id="S10.SS1.p73.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{I}\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{I}\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves part C.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p74" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p74.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove part D. If <math id="S10.SS1.p74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p74.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{*}I\in{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{*}I\in{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S10.SS1.p74.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, then precomposing <math id="S10.SS1.p74.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> with a parallel translation, which is a biholomorphism of <math id="S10.SS1.p74.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},I)</annotation></semantics></math>, we can assume without loss
of generality that <math id="S10.SS1.p74.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(x)=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(x)=x</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S10.SS1.p74.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},x)</annotation></semantics></math>. By part A, this means that <math id="S10.SS1.p74.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{*}I=J_{I}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{*}I=J_{I}=I</annotation></semantics></math>.
This proves part D and finishes the proof of the proposition.
<span id="S10.SS1.p74.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p75" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p75.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p75.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.8 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p76" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p76.1" class="ltx_p">The restriction of the projection <math id="S10.SS1.p76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{l}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{l}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})=:{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{l}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{l}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{l}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})=:{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{l}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S10.SS1.p76.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> is a homeomorphism <math id="S10.SS1.p76.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{l}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})={\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{l}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{l}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})={\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{l}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>
that identifies <math id="S10.SS1.p76.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)\subset{\mathcal{C}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)\subset{\mathcal{C}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S10.SS1.p76.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{l}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)\subset{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{l}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{l}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)\subset{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{l}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p77" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p77.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p77.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p78" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p78.1" class="ltx_p">Parts A and C of <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>
imply that every orbit of the <math id="S10.SS1.p78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>-action on <math id="S10.SS1.p78.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> intersects <math id="S10.SS1.p78.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math>. By part D of <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>, it intersects <math id="S10.SS1.p78.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> at exactly one point.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p79" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p79.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, the map <math id="S10.SS1.p79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{l}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{l}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>, associating to each <math id="S10.SS1.p79.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math> its <math id="S10.SS1.p79.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit, is well-defined and bijective. Clearly, it is also continuous.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p80" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p80.1" class="ltx_p">By part B of <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>,
the map <math id="S10.SS1.p80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{l}\to{\it Lin}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{l}\to{\it Lin}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS1.p80.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\mapsto J_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\mapsto J_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, is continuous. This means that the inverse of the map <math id="S10.SS1.p80.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{l}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{l}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous too.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p81" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p81.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, the projection <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{l}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{l}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> is a homeomorphism.
Clearly, the image of <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> under this homeomorphism lies in <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{l}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{l}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us show that it coincides with <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{l}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{l}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, or, equivalently, that for any complex structure <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{I}\in{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{I}\in{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed,
since <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, the cohomology class <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, hence also with respect to <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, because <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> by the action of <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now
<a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>
implies
that <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{I}\in{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{I}\in{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the homeomorphism <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{l}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}\to{\mathcal{C}}_{l}/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>
identifies <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Lin}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐿𝑖𝑛</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Lin}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S10.SS1.p81.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{l}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{l}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S10.SS1.p81.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p82" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p82.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p82.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.9 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p83" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p83.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS1.p83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler-type form on <math id="S10.SS1.p83.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p84" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p84.1" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S10.SS1.p84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on the set of connected components of <math id="S10.SS1.p84.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p84.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p85" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p85.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p85.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p86" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p86.1" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Prop. 6.1]</cite>, any Kähler-type form on <math id="S10.SS1.p86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be mapped by a diffeomorphism of <math id="S10.SS1.p86.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> acting trivially on <math id="S10.SS1.p86.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> to a linear symplectic form. Thus, we can assume without loss of generality that our Kähler-type form <math id="S10.SS1.p86.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is linear.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p87" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p87.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS1.p87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary complex structure compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p87.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Prop. 6.1]</cite>, there exists a diffeomorphism <math id="S10.SS1.p87.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S10.SS1.p87.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> acting trivially on <math id="S10.SS1.p87.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> so that the complex structure <math id="S10.SS1.p87.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> is linear. The symplectic form <math id="S10.SS1.p87.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is then compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p87.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> and has the same cohomology class as <math id="S10.SS1.p87.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the cohomology class
<math id="S10.SS1.p87.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to the linear complex structure <math id="S10.SS1.p87.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, by <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>, the linear form
<math id="S10.SS1.p87.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> itself is Kähler with respect to <math id="S10.SS1.p87.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p88" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p88.1" class="ltx_p">We have obtained that <math id="S10.SS1.p88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.p88.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> are cohomologous and compatible with the same complex structure <math id="S10.SS1.p88.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore the straight path in the space of 2-forms connecting these forms is formed by cohomologous symplectic forms. By Moser’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mos</a>]</cite>, this means that there exists <math id="S10.SS1.p88.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S10.SS1.p88.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{*}\phi^{*}\omega=\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{*}\phi^{*}\omega=\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S10.SS1.p88.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\psi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\psi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
and moreover <math id="S10.SS1.p88.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi\psi)^{*}I=\psi^{*}\phi^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi\psi)^{*}I=\psi^{*}\phi^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the same connected component of <math id="S10.SS1.p88.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S10.SS1.p88.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> – i.e., in <math id="S10.SS1.p88.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p89" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p89.1" class="ltx_p">We have proved that any complex structure <math id="S10.SS1.p89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p89.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> can be mapped by an element of <math id="S10.SS1.p89.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> into a complex structure lying in <math id="S10.SS1.p89.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>. This readily implies the proposition.
<span id="S10.SS1.p89.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p90" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p90.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p90.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.10 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p91" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p91.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS1.p91.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler-type symplectic form on <math id="S10.SS1.p91.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p92" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p92.1" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S10.SS1.p92.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with a Campana-simple complex structure if and only if
<math id="S10.SS1.p92.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p93" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p93.1" class="ltx_p">In this case for any connected component <math id="S10.SS1.p93.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S10.SS1.p93.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p93.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> the set
<math id="S10.SS1.p93.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>
is a dense connected subset of <math id="S10.SS1.p93.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p94" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p94.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS1.p94.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p95" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p95.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S10.SS1.p95.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is rational.
Then, by part 1 of
<a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, there are no Campana-simple complex structures compatible with <math id="S10.SS1.p95.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS1.p96" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.p96.1" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Prop. 6.1]</cite>, any Kähler-type form on <math id="S10.SS1.p96.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be mapped by a diffeomorphism of <math id="S10.SS1.p96.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> to a linear symplectic form.
Thus, we can assume without loss of generality that our Kähler-type form <math id="S10.SS1.p96.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is linear.
In view of <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>, we can also assume without loss of generality
that <math id="S10.SS1.p96.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}={\mathcal{C}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}={\mathcal{C}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now the proposition follows from
<a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>,
<a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>.
<span id="S10.SS1.p96.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.2 </span>Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces</h3>

<div id="S10.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth manifold underlying a K3 surface.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The space <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> has the structure of a complex manifold (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cat</a>]</cite>).
More
precisely, a combination of the fundamental theorems of
Kuranishi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ku1</a>]</cite> and Bogomolov-Tian-Todorov
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bo2</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tia</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tod</a>]</cite> shows that for any <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}\in\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}\in\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the Kuranishi
space of <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,J_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,J_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> (the base of a certain universal complex-analytic local
deformation family of a complex structure defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ku1</a>]</cite>) is
a smooth complex manifold as long as the canonical bundle of
<math id="S10.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,J_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,J_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial, which, of course, is true in the hyperkähler
case, and in particular, for K3 surfaces. Moreover (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cat</a>]</cite>), there exists a
homeomorphism between a neighborhood
<math id="S10.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{U}}_{[J_{0}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚄</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{U}}_{[J_{0}]}</annotation></semantics></math>
of <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J_{0}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J_{0}]</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
and an open subset of the Kuranishi space of <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,J_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,J_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. This homeomorphism maps
each <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J]\in{\tt{U}}_{[J_{0}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝚄</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J]\in{\tt{U}}_{[J_{0}]}</annotation></semantics></math> to the point
<math id="S10.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> of the Kuranishi space
corresponding to a complex structure on <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> representing <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The pullback of
the universal complex-analytic local deformation family over the Kuranishi space
under the homeomorphism, together with a smooth trivialization of the universal fibration over a neighborhood of <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in the Kuranishi space, induces a complex-analytic deformation family <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times{\tt{U}}_{[J_{0}]}\to{\tt{U}}_{[J_{0}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>𝚄</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝚄</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times{\tt{U}}_{[J_{0}]}\to{\tt{U}}_{[J_{0}]}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The
smooth and the complex structures on <math id="S10.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt{U}}_{[J_{0}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚄</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt{U}}_{[J_{0}]}</annotation></semantics></math> are the pullbacks of the
corresponding structures on the Kuranishi space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 10.11 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the complex manifold <math id="S10.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is not Hausdorff (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">At1</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">BurR</a>, p.238]</cite>, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sal</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The description above also implies that for any <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\in\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\in\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
any smooth path in <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> starting at <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[J]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[J]</annotation></semantics></math> can be lifted to a smooth path in <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> starting at <math id="S10.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\times H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\to{\mathbb{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\times H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\to{\mathbb{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the intersection form. Its restriction to <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a real-valued symmetric form of signature <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,19)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>19</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,19)</annotation></semantics></math>.
A subspace of <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is called positive if the restriction of <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to it is positive. We use <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bot</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the orthogonal complement with respect to <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S10.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>
<table id="S10.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{C}}H_{I}^{2,0}(M;{\mathbb{C}})=\dim_{\mathbb{C}}H_{I}^{0,2}(M;{\mathbb{C}})=1,\ \dim_{\mathbb{C}}H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{C}})=20" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.389em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{C}}H_{I}^{2,0}(M;{\mathbb{C}})=\dim_{\mathbb{C}}H_{I}^{0,2}(M;{\mathbb{C}})=1,\ \dim_{\mathbb{C}}H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{C}})=20</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">(see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huyb</a>]</cite>). The spaces <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{I}^{2,0}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\oplus H_{I}^{0,2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{I}^{2,0}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\oplus H_{I}^{0,2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1,1}_{I}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1,1}_{I}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> are orthogonal with respect to <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> is a holomorphic <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-form spanning <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2,0}_{I}(M;{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2,0}_{I}(M;{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(H_{I}^{2,0}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\oplus H_{I}^{0,2}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\right)\cap H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(H_{I}^{2,0}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\oplus H_{I}^{0,2}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\right)\cap H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is the <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional plane spanned by <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Im}\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Im}\Omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote this plane by <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. The basis <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\operatorname{Re}\Omega,\operatorname{Im}\Omega\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\operatorname{Re}\Omega,\operatorname{Im}\Omega\}</annotation></semantics></math> defines an orientation of the plane <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{I}^{\bot}=H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\cap H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{I}^{\bot}=H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\cap H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The restriction of <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive and the restriction of <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to its orthogonal complement <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> has signature <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,19)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>19</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,19)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the set <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a\in H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\ |\ q(a,a)&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a\in H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\ |\ q(a,a)&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> has two connected components: the one that contains the Kähler cone of <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <span id="S10.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">positive cone of <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and will be denoted <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Pos}\,}(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pos</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Pos}\,}(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the other one
is <math id="S10.SS2.p7.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-{\rm{Pos}\,}(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Pos</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-{\rm{Pos}\,}(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))</annotation></semantics></math> be the Grassmanian of positive oriented <math id="S10.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional planes in
<math id="S10.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
There is a canonical diffeomorphism identifying <math id="S10.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))</annotation></semantics></math>
with an open subset <math id="S10.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{l\in{\mathbb{P}}H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\ |\ q(l,l)=0,\ q(l,{\bar{l}})&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>l</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{l\in{\mathbb{P}}H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\ |\ q(l,l)=0,\ q(l,{\bar{l}})&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math>
of the quadric <math id="S10.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{l\in{\mathbb{P}}H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\ |\ q(l,l)=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo rspace="0.222em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{l\in{\mathbb{P}}H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\ |\ q(l,l)=0\}</annotation></semantics></math>
(see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huyb</a>, Ch.6, Prop. 1.5]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ver6</a>, Claim 2.9]</cite>).
Thus <math id="S10.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))</annotation></semantics></math> has the structure of a connected complex manifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Define the <span id="S10.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">period map</span> <math id="S10.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\sf Per}:{\tt Teich}(M)\to Gr_{++}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\sf Per}:{\tt Teich}(M)\to Gr_{++}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S10.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\sf Per}([I]):=W_{I}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\sf Per}([I]):=W_{I},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.p9.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S10.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is the element of <math id="S10.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> represented by <math id="S10.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The global Torelli theorem for K3 surfaces (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huyb</a>]</cite> and the references therein to the original papers)
implies the following claim.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 10.12 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">A. There exists a subset <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}\subset{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}\subset{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> so that for each connected component <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
the intersection <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is dense and connected in <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
and the map</p>
<table id="S10.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\sf Per}:{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)\to Gr_{++}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\sf Per}:{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)\to Gr_{++}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.p12.2" class="ltx_p">is a surjective local biholomorphism which is injective on <math id="S10.SS2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">B. Assume that <math id="S10.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{0},I_{1}\in\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{0},I_{1}\in\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I_{0}],[I_{1}]\in{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I_{0}],[I_{1}]\in{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\sf Per}([I_{0}])=\operatorname{\sf Per}([I_{1}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\sf Per}([I_{0}])=\operatorname{\sf Per}([I_{1}])</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Then there exists a unique diffeomorphism <math id="S10.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{H}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{H}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}I_{1}=I_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}I_{1}=I_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S10.SS2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">By a <math id="S10.SS2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S10.SS2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-class</span> we will mean an integral cohomology class <math id="S10.SS2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(b,b)=-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(b,b)=-2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p16.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S10.SS2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> denote by <math id="S10.SS2.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> the set of all <math id="S10.SS2.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-classes <math id="S10.SS2.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> that are of type <math id="S10.SS2.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S10.SS2.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p17.1" class="ltx_p">The Kähler cone of <math id="S10.SS2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> admits the following description.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 10.13 (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huyb</a>, Sec. 8, Thm. 5.2]</cite>, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AmV2</a>, Thm. 6.2]</cite>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p19.1" class="ltx_p">The Kähler cone of <math id="S10.SS2.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected component of the set</p>
<table id="S10.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\rm{Pos}\,}(M,I)\setminus\cup_{b\in S(M,I)}\left(b^{\bot}\cap{\rm{Pos}\,}(M,I)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pos</mi><mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">∖</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∪</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mi>Pos</mi><mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Pos}\,}(M,I)\setminus\cup_{b\in S(M,I)}\left(b^{\bot}\cap{\rm{Pos}\,}(M,I)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.p19.2" class="ltx_p">In particular, if <math id="S10.SS2.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\bot}\cap{\rm{Pos}\,}(M,I)=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>Pos</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\bot}\cap{\rm{Pos}\,}(M,I)=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S10.SS2.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in S(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in S(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the Kähler cone of <math id="S10.SS2.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is just <math id="S10.SS2.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Pos}\,}(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pos</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Pos}\,}(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S10.SS2.p19.2.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p20.1" class="ltx_p">The next result follows from the Calabi-Yau theorem (formerly the Calabi conjecture) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx106" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yau2</a>]</cite> combined with Bochner’s technique – see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bea1</a>]</cite> (cf. e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">GHJ</a>, Thm. 5.11]</cite>) for the proof of the existence and uniqueness; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">FuS</a>, Thm. 6.3]</cite> for the proof of the smooth dependence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p21" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 10.14 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p22.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS2.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S10.SS2.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler class with respect to <math id="S10.SS2.p22.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Then there exists a unique <math id="S10.SS2.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in\mathit{SK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in\mathit{SK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S10.SS2.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS2.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\eta]=a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\eta]=a</annotation></semantics></math> and the Riemannian metric <math id="S10.SS2.p23.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\eta(\cdot,I\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(\cdot,I\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>
is Ricci-flat. The form <math id="S10.SS2.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> depends smoothly on the pair <math id="S10.SS2.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I,a)</annotation></semantics></math> (as <math id="S10.SS2.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> varies in a complex-analytic deformation family).
<span id="S10.SS2.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p24" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 10.15</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p25.1" class="ltx_p">For any Kähler structure <math id="S10.SS2.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta,I)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S10.SS2.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that the Riemannian metric <math id="S10.SS2.p25.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\eta(\cdot,I\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(\cdot,I\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>
is Ricci-flat,
the form <math id="S10.SS2.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> and the complex structure <math id="S10.SS2.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> can be included together in a hyperkähler structure <math id="S10.SS2.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\omega_{1}:=\eta,\omega_{2},\omega_{3},I_{1}:=I,I_{2},I_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\omega_{1}:=\eta,\omega_{2},\omega_{3},I_{1}:=I,I_{2},I_{3})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p26" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.16 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p27.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS2.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> be compatible with <math id="S10.SS2.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p28.1" class="ltx_p">Then there exists <math id="S10.SS2.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\in\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\in\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> isotopic to <math id="S10.SS2.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that the Riemannian metric <math id="S10.SS2.p28.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega(\cdot,J\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(\cdot,J\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is Ricci-flat.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p29" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p30.1" class="ltx_p">By <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>, the complex structure <math id="S10.SS2.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is a part of a (unique) Kähler structure <math id="S10.SS2.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta,I)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S10.SS2.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> for which the corresponding Riemannian metric <math id="S10.SS2.p30.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\eta(\cdot,I\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(\cdot,I\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is Ricci-flat, and moreover <math id="S10.SS2.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\eta]=[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\eta]=[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math>. The symplectic forms <math id="S10.SS2.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p30.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> are cohomologous and compatible with the same <math id="S10.SS2.p30.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore, by Moser’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mos</a>]</cite>, there exists <math id="S10.SS2.p30.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS2.p30.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}\eta=\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}\eta=\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S10.SS2.p30.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J:=\phi^{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J:=\phi^{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S10.SS2.p30.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and the Riemannian metric <math id="S10.SS2.p30.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega(\cdot,J\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(\cdot,J\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is Ricci-flat.
<span id="S10.SS2.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p31.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS2.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler-type symplectic form on <math id="S10.SS2.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p32.1" class="ltx_p">Define <math id="S10.SS2.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it D}_{\omega}\subset Gr_{++}\left(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it D}_{\omega}\subset Gr_{++}\left(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math> as the set of positive oriented <math id="S10.SS2.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional planes <math id="S10.SS2.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS2.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in W^{\bot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in W^{\bot}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S10.SS2.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{R}}[\omega]\oplus W)^{\bot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{R}}[\omega]\oplus W)^{\bot}</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain any <math id="S10.SS2.p32.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p33" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.17 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p34.1" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="S10.SS2.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it D}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it D}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense connected subset of the submanifold <math id="S10.SS2.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S10.SS2.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p35" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p35.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p36.1" class="ltx_p">We have <math id="S10.SS2.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q([\omega],[\omega])&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q([\omega],[\omega])&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The restriction of <math id="S10.SS2.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S10.SS2.p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]^{\bot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]^{\bot}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quadratic form of signature <math id="S10.SS2.p36.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,19)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>19</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,19)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The space <math id="S10.SS2.p36.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}([\omega]^{\bot})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}([\omega]^{\bot})</annotation></semantics></math> of positive oriented <math id="S10.SS2.p36.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional planes in <math id="S10.SS2.p36.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]^{\bot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]^{\bot}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth connected manifold diffeomorphic to
<math id="S10.SS2.p36.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO(2,19)/SO(2)\times SO(19)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>19</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>19</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO(2,19)/SO(2)\times SO(19)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p37.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S10.SS2.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S10.SS2.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-class, then <math id="S10.SS2.p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p37.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent and the restriction of <math id="S10.SS2.p37.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S10.SS2.p37.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}</annotation></semantics></math> has signature <math id="S10.SS2.p37.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,18)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>18</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,18)</annotation></semantics></math>. The space <math id="S10.SS2.p37.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left(a^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left(a^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> of positive oriented <math id="S10.SS2.p37.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional planes in
<math id="S10.SS2.p37.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a closed submanifold of <math id="S10.SS2.p37.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> diffeomorphic to
<math id="S10.SS2.p37.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO(2,18)/SO(2)\times SO(18)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>18</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>18</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO(2,18)/SO(2)\times SO(18)</annotation></semantics></math> and thus has real codimension <math id="S10.SS2.p37.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S10.SS2.p37.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p38.1" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="S10.SS2.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it D}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it D}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is the complement in <math id="S10.SS2.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> of the (at most) countable union of the closed codimension-<math id="S10.SS2.p38.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> submanifolds <math id="S10.SS2.p38.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left(a^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left(a^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S10.SS2.p38.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-classes <math id="S10.SS2.p38.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS2.p38.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(a,[\omega])=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(a,[\omega])=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="S10.SS2.p38.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it D}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it D}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense connected subset of the submanifold <math id="S10.SS2.p38.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S10.SS2.p38.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
(The density follows from Baire’s theorem; for a proof of the connectivity see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ver6</a>, Lem. 4.10]</cite>.)
<span id="S10.SS2.p38.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p39" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p39.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p39.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.18 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p40.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S10.SS2.p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected component of <math id="S10.SS2.p40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with the symplectic form <math id="S10.SS2.p40.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S10.SS2.p40.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a complex structure compatible with <math id="S10.SS2.p40.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p41.1" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S10.SS2.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)=\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}({\it D}_{\omega})\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)=\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}({\it D}_{\omega})\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and it is
a dense connected subset
of the closed submanifold <math id="S10.SS2.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}\left(Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)\right)\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}\left(Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)\right)\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S10.SS2.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p42" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p42.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p42.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p43.1" class="ltx_p">Let us show that <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\subset\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}({\it D}_{\omega})\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\subset\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}({\it D}_{\omega})\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider a complex structure <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is orthogonal to <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}[\omega]\oplus W_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}[\omega]\oplus W_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot contain any <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-class in its orthogonal complement: indeed, if <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> is
orthogonal to <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{I}^{2,0}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\oplus H_{I}^{0,2}(M;{\mathbb{C}}))\cap H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{I}^{2,0}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\oplus H_{I}^{0,2}(M;{\mathbb{C}}))\cap H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{1,1}_{I}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{1,1}_{I}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>. However, by <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>, a <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-class in <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be orthogonal to a Kähler class with respect to <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we have proved that <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\sf Per}([I])=W_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\sf Per}([I])=W_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it D}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it D}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields that <math id="S10.SS2.p43.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\subset\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}({\it D}_{\omega})\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\subset\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}({\it D}_{\omega})\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p44" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p44.1" class="ltx_p">Now let us show that <math id="S10.SS2.p44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\supset\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}({\it D}_{\omega})\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊃</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\supset\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}({\it D}_{\omega})\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p45.1" class="ltx_p">By the hypothesis of the proposition, there exists a complex structure <math id="S10.SS2.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S10.SS2.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.
In view of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>, we may assume, without loss of generality, that the metric <math id="S10.SS2.p45.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega(\cdot,I\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(\cdot,I\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is Ricci-flat.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p46.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary plane belonging to <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it D}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it D}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. By <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>, it can be connected with <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{I}</annotation></semantics></math>
by a smooth path of planes in <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it D}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it D}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\sf Per}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\sf Per}</annotation></semantics></math> is a local biholomorphism (by part A of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>) and
by the above-mentioned path-lifting property of the <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)\to{\tt Teich}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)\to{\tt Teich}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, this path can be lifted to a smooth path in <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> – i.e.,
there exists a smooth path
of complex structures <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{I_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{I_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, in <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{0}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{0}=I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{I_{1}}=W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{I_{1}}=W</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{I_{t}}\in{\it D}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{I_{t}}\in{\it D}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, for each <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> the plane <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{I_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{I_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> is orthogonal to <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{R}}[\omega]\oplus W)^{\bot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{R}}[\omega]\oplus W)^{\bot}</annotation></semantics></math> does not contain any <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-classes. Therefore, by <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>, for each <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> either <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the Kähler cone of <math id="S10.SS2.p46.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p47.1" class="ltx_p">We claim that it is always <math id="S10.SS2.p47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math>, and not <math id="S10.SS2.p47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p48.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, consider the set <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> for which it is <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The set <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is non-empty since <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\in A</annotation></semantics></math>: the cohomology class <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=I_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=I_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The set <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is also open:
if for some <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> the class <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect
to <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then for any <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently close to <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the class <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]_{I_{t}}^{1,1}=[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]_{I_{t}}^{1,1}=[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with
respect to <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (by the corollary of the Kodaira-Spencer stability theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Thm. 5.6]</cite>, see Section <a href="#S13.SS2" title="13.2 Deformation families of complex structures ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.2</span></a>).
For the same reason the complement of <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the set of <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) is also open. Thus, <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is open, closed and non-empty, meaning that <math id="S10.SS2.p48.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, which proves the claim.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p49" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p49.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, for each <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> the class <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect to a Kähler-type complex structure <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> depending smoothly on <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>
and for <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> the metric <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega(\cdot,I_{0}\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(\cdot,I_{0}\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is Ricci-flat. By <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>, there exists a smooth family of cohomologous symplectic forms <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}=\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}=\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (and, moreover, the Riemannian metric <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega_{t}(\cdot,I_{t}\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{t}(\cdot,I_{t}\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is Ricci-flat) for each <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Now Moser’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mos</a>]</cite> implies that there exists <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}\omega_{1}=\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}\omega_{1}=\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, the complex structure <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}I_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}I_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I_{1}]\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I_{1}]\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=W_{I_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=W_{I_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\sf Per}\left({\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\sf Per}\left({\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. This yields <math id="S10.SS2.p49.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\supset\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}({\it D}_{\omega})\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊃</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\supset\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}({\it D}_{\omega})\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p50" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p50.1" class="ltx_p">We have shown that <math id="S10.SS2.p50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)=\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}({\it D}_{\omega})\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)=\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}({\it D}_{\omega})\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since, by part A of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>, <math id="S10.SS2.p50.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\sf Per}:{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)\to Gr_{++}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\sf Per}:{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)\to Gr_{++}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))</annotation></semantics></math>
is a surjective local biholomorphism which is injective on a dense connected subset of <math id="S10.SS2.p50.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and since, by <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>, <math id="S10.SS2.p50.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it D}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it D}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense connected manifold of a closed submanifold of <math id="S10.SS2.p50.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)=\operatorname{\sf Per}\left({\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)=\operatorname{\sf Per}\left({\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>,
we get that <math id="S10.SS2.p50.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense connected subset of the closed submanifold <math id="S10.SS2.p50.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}\left(Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)\right)\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}\left(Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)\right)\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S10.SS2.p50.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S10.SS2.p50.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p51" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p51.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p51.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.19 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p52" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p52.1" class="ltx_p">The group <math id="S10.SS2.p52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on the set of connected components of <math id="S10.SS2.p52.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S10.SS2.p52.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p53" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p53.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p53.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p54" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p54.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S10.SS2.p54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p54.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are connected components of <math id="S10.SS2.p54.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S10.SS2.p54.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. We need to show that there exists a symplectomorphism
in <math id="S10.SS2.p54.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> mapping <math id="S10.SS2.p54.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S10.SS2.p54.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p55" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p55.1" class="ltx_p">By <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>,</p>
<table id="S10.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S10.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\sf Per}\left({\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\right)=\operatorname{\sf Per}\left({\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{1}}(M,\omega)\right)={\it D}_{\omega}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\sf Per}\left({\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\right)=\operatorname{\sf Per}\left({\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{1}}(M,\omega)\right)={\it D}_{\omega}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S10.SS2.p55.2" class="ltx_p">Pick <math id="S10.SS2.p55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{0}\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{0}\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS2.p55.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{1}\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{1}}(M,\omega)\cap{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{1}\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{1}}(M,\omega)\cap{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that <math id="S10.SS2.p55.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\sf Per}([I_{0}])=\operatorname{\sf Per}([I_{1}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\sf Per}([I_{0}])=\operatorname{\sf Per}([I_{1}])</annotation></semantics></math>. By part B of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>, there exists <math id="S10.SS2.p55.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{H}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{H}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS2.p55.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}I_{1}=I_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}I_{1}=I_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S10.SS2.p55.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS2.p55.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> are cohomologous and compatible with the same complex structure <math id="S10.SS2.p55.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore the straight path in the space of 2-forms connecting these forms is formed by cohomologous symplectic forms. By Moser’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mos</a>]</cite>, this means that there exists <math id="S10.SS2.p55.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S10.SS2.p55.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi^{*}\phi^{*}\omega=\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{*}\phi^{*}\omega=\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S10.SS2.p55.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\psi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\psi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and moreover <math id="S10.SS2.p55.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi\psi)^{*}I_{1}=\psi^{*}\phi^{*}I_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi\psi)^{*}I_{1}=\psi^{*}\phi^{*}I_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the same connected component of <math id="S10.SS2.p55.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S10.SS2.p55.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{0}=\phi^{*}I_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{0}=\phi^{*}I_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> – i.e., in <math id="S10.SS2.p55.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p56" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p56.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, the symplectomorphism <math id="S10.SS2.p56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\psi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\psi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> maps <math id="S10.SS2.p56.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S10.SS2.p56.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S10.SS2.p56.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p57" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p57.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p57.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 10.20 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p58" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p58.1" class="ltx_p">The Kähler-type symplectic form <math id="S10.SS2.p58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with a Campana-simple complex structure on <math id="S10.SS2.p58.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
<math id="S10.SS2.p58.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational.
Moreover, in this case for any connected component <math id="S10.SS2.p58.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S10.SS2.p58.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S10.SS2.p58.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> the set
<math id="S10.SS2.p58.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
is a dense connected subset of <math id="S10.SS2.p58.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p59" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p59.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS2.p59.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p60" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p60.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S10.SS2.p60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is rational.
Then, by part 1 of
<a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, there are no Campana-simple complex structures compatible with <math id="S10.SS2.p60.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p61" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p61.1" class="ltx_p">Now assume <math id="S10.SS2.p61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational. Let us show that
the set
of Campana-simple structures lying in <math id="S10.SS2.p61.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
is a dense connected subset of <math id="S10.SS2.p61.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p62" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p62.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS2.p62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS2.p62.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S10.SS2.p62.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is not a real multiple of a rational cohomology class (and, in particular, not a real multiple of <math id="S10.SS2.p62.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="S10.SS2.p62.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}b^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}b^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}=20</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the space <math id="S10.SS2.p62.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left(b^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left(b^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> of positive oriented <math id="S10.SS2.p62.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional planes in
<math id="S10.SS2.p62.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}</annotation></semantics></math> has real codimension <math id="S10.SS2.p62.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S10.SS2.p62.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S10.SS2.p62.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(b,b)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(b,b)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and is empty otherwise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p63" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p63.1" class="ltx_p">As we have seen in the proof of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>, <math id="S10.SS2.p63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it D}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it D}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is the complement in <math id="S10.SS2.p63.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left([\omega]^{\bot}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> of an (at most) countable union of closed codimension-<math id="S10.SS2.p63.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> submanifolds <math id="S10.SS2.p63.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left(a^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left(a^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S10.SS2.p63.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-classes <math id="S10.SS2.p63.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS2.p63.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(a,[\omega])=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(a,[\omega])=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore the complement in <math id="S10.SS2.p63.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it D}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it D}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> to the countable union of codimension-2 submanifolds <math id="S10.SS2.p63.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}\left(b^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊥</mo></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}\left(b^{\bot}\cap[\omega]^{\bot}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS2.p63.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS2.p63.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, is a dense connected subset of <math id="S10.SS2.p63.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it D}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it D}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote the latter subset by <math id="S10.SS2.p63.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p64" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p64.1" class="ltx_p">By part A of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a> and <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>, <math id="S10.SS2.p64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense connected subset of <math id="S10.SS2.p64.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We claim that each <math id="S10.SS2.p64.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S10.SS2.p64.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}(A)\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}(A)\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is Campana-simple. Indeed, if <math id="S10.SS2.p64.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a proper positive-dimensional analytic subvariety – i.e., a (possibly singular) complex curve – then the cohomology class <math id="S10.SS2.p64.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS2.p64.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, Poincaré-dual to the fundamental class of the subvariety is of type <math id="S10.SS2.p64.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S10.SS2.p64.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Voi</a>, Vol. 1, Sec. 11]</cite>).
Therefore the 2-dimensional plane <math id="S10.SS2.p64.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\sf Per}([I])\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\sf Per}([I])\in A</annotation></semantics></math> is orthogonal to <math id="S10.SS2.p64.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S10.SS2.p64.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> which contradicts the definition of <math id="S10.SS2.p64.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p65" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS2.p65.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, <math id="S10.SS2.p65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap\operatorname{\sf Per}^{-1}(A)</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense connected subset of the set
<math id="S10.SS2.p65.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, <math id="S10.SS2.p65.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
is a dense connected subset of <math id="S10.SS2.p65.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S10.SS2.p65.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.3 </span>Kähler-type embeddings of balls into tori and K3 surfaces – the proofs</h3>

<div id="S10.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now we are ready to prove <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. For convenience
we restate it here.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 10.21 (<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=2n</annotation></semantics></math>, be either <math id="S10.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> or a smooth manifold (of real dimension <math id="S10.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>) underlying a complex K3 surface
and let <math id="S10.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler-type symplectic form on <math id="S10.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S10.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1},\ldots,r_{k}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">A. Kähler-type embeddings of <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S10.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">More precisely, assume that <math id="S10.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i}),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is
compatible with a Campana-simple complex structure <math id="S10.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Then
there exists an <math id="S10.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type embedding <math id="S10.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">B.
The group <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> acts transitively on the set of connected components of
<math id="S10.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S10.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">C.
Any two Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S10.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> (if they exist!) lie in the same orbit of the
<math id="S10.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-action. They lie in the same orbit of the <math id="S10.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Symp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}(M,\omega)\cap\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-action
if and only if they favor a common connected component of <math id="S10.SS3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. In the latter case there exists <math id="S10.SS3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt Teich}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt Teich}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
such that both embeddings are of <math id="S10.SS3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]</annotation></semantics></math>-Kähler-type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S10.SS3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S10.SS3" title="10.3 Kähler-type embeddings of balls into tori and K3 surfaces – the proofs ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.3</span></a> (=<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>):</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Let us first consider the torus case.
By <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>, in this case we may assume without loss of generality that <math id="S10.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}={\mathcal{C}}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}={\mathcal{C}}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now part A of the theorem follows from part (I) of <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>.
Part B of the theorem is exactly <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>.
Part C of the theorem follows from part B and from part (II) of <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and <a href="#S10.SS1" title="10.1 Campana-simple complex structures on tori ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.1</span></a>.
This finishes the proof in the torus case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Let us now prove the result in the K3 case.
Here part A of the theorem follows from part (I) of <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and
<a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>.
Part B is exactly <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>.
Part C of the theorem follows from part B and from part (II) of <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.
This finishes the proof in the K3 case.
<span id="S10.SS3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S11" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">11 </span>Tame
embeddings of arbitrary domains</h2>

<div id="S11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we will prove the results on
tame
embeddings into tori and K3 surfaces stated in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M=2n</annotation></semantics></math>, be a closed connected oriented manifold admitting Kähler structures (compatible with the orientation).</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S11.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{m}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{m}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the space of smooth differential <math id="S11.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-forms on <math id="S11.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=0,1,\ldots,2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=0,1,\ldots,2n</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S11.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{S}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{S}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the space of symplectic structures on <math id="S11.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S11.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{C}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{C}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the space of complex structures on <math id="S11.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
We equip these spaces with the <math id="S11.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-topologies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, be compact domains with piecewise-smooth boundary, so that their interiors contain the origin and <math id="S11.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(W_{i};{\mathbb{R}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(W_{i};{\mathbb{R}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote</p>
<table id="S11.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S11.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 11.1 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S11.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic form on <math id="S11.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S11.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a
tame
embedding holomorphic with respect to a complex structure <math id="S11.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> tamed by <math id="S11.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S11.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sufficiently small neighborhood of <math id="S11.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S11.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{C}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{C}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> so that each <math id="S11.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is tamed by <math id="S11.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Then there exists a neighborhood <math id="S11.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}={\mathcal{U}}({\mathcal{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}={\mathcal{U}}({\mathcal{V}})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S11.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S11.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{S}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{S}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> such that for each <math id="S11.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>
there exists a tame
embedding
<math id="S11.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>
holomorphic with respect to a complex structure <math id="S11.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> which is tamed by <math id="S11.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and isotopic to <math id="S11.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The following lemma is standard – it is proved by an easy modification of the proof of a similar result in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Lem. 9.3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 11.2 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\subset M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\subset M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}V=2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}V=2n</annotation></semantics></math>, be a compact
submanifold with a
piecewise-smooth boundary.
Let <math id="S11.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a neighborhood of zero in the space <math id="S11.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{1}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{1}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Then there exists a neighborhood <math id="S11.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of zero in the space <math id="S11.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega^{2}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega^{2}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that for each <math id="S11.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is exact on <math id="S11.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, one can
choose <math id="S11.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S11.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\sigma=\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\sigma=\eta</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S11.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that the tame embedding <math id="S11.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}f_{i}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}f_{i}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> extends (as a
tame embedding) to an embedding of an open neighborhood of
<math id="S11.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{U}}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}U_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕌</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{U}}=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}U_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S11.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, is a compact domain with a piecewise-smooth boundary containing <math id="S11.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in its interior and homotopy equivalent to <math id="S11.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that
<math id="S11.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic (on a neighborhood of <math id="S11.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>) with respect to <math id="S11.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Choose a neighborhood <math id="S11.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S11.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S11.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{S}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{S}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> so that for each <math id="S11.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> the following conditions are satisfied:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p15" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S11.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S11.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S11.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> the form <math id="S11.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega+t(\omega-\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega+t(\omega-\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectic.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S11.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is tamed by any <math id="S11.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S11.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Choose a neighborhood <math id="S11.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S11.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Id</annotation></semantics></math> in the group <math id="S11.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S11.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{*}I\in{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{*}I\in{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>
for each <math id="S11.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Choose a neighborhood <math id="S11.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S11.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> in the space of time-dependent smooth vector fields on <math id="S11.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, equipped with the <math id="S11.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-topology, so that for each vector field <math id="S11.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> the time <math id="S11.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>-flow of <math id="S11.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>
generates a diffeomorphism lying in <math id="S11.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> and such that
for each <math id="S11.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> the time-<math id="S11.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,t]</annotation></semantics></math> flow of <math id="S11.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> maps each <math id="S11.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(W_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(W_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S11.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(Int\,U_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(Int\,U_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Since for all <math id="S11.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S11.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(W_{i};{\mathbb{R}})=H^{2}(U_{i};{\mathbb{R}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(W_{i};{\mathbb{R}})=H^{2}(U_{i};{\mathbb{R}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, for each <math id="S11.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> the restriction of the form <math id="S11.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega-\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega-\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S11.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f({\mathbb{U}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕌</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f({\mathbb{U}})</annotation></semantics></math> is exact. Each <math id="S11.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in\Omega^{1}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in\Omega^{1}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfying <math id="S11.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\sigma=\omega-\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\sigma=\omega-\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> defines a smooth time-dependent vector field <math id="S11.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> by the formula</p>
<table id="S11.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\omega+t(\omega-\omega^{\prime})\right)(v_{t},\cdot)=\sigma(\cdot),\ t\in[0,1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\omega+t(\omega-\omega^{\prime})\right)(v_{t},\cdot)=\sigma(\cdot),\ t\in[0,1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p18.2" class="ltx_p">In view of <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, applied to <math id="S11.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f({\mathbb{U}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕌</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f({\mathbb{U}})</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a neighborhood <math id="S11.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}_{2}\subset{\mathcal{U}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}_{2}\subset{\mathcal{U}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S11.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S11.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> so that for each <math id="S11.p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> one can choose <math id="S11.p18.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> as above so that the corresponding <math id="S11.p18.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> lies in <math id="S11.p18.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S11.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}\}\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}\}\subset\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, be the time-<math id="S11.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,t]</annotation></semantics></math> flow of <math id="S11.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists an open neighborhood <math id="S11.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}\subset Int\,\,U_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}\subset Int\,\,U_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of each <math id="S11.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S11.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(Z_{i})\subset f(Int\,\,U_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(Z_{i})\subset f(Int\,\,U_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S11.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moser’s argument<span id="footnote1b" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          Let us note that in the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Prop. 9.1]</cite> we applied Moser’s argument to symplectic forms on the <span id="footnote1b.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">domain of <math id="footnote1b.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math></span>, while here we will apply it to symplectic forms on the <span id="footnote1b.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">target</span> of <math id="footnote1b.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> – i.e., on <math id="footnote1b.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mos</a>]</cite> shows that</p>
<table id="S11.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\phi_{1}^{*}\omega^{\prime}|_{f(Z_{i})}=\omega|_{f(Z_{i})},\ i=1,\ldots,k." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>ω</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}^{*}\omega^{\prime}|_{f(Z_{i})}=\omega|_{f(Z_{i})},\ i=1,\ldots,k.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p19.2" class="ltx_p">We claim that</p>
<table id="S11.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:=\phi_{1}\circ f=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}\phi_{1}\circ f_{i}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:=\phi_{1}\circ f=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}\phi_{1}\circ f_{i}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p19.3" class="ltx_p">is a tame embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, it is clearly a smooth embedding extending to a neighborhood <math id="S11.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S11.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S11.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(Z)\subset Int\,f({\mathbb{U}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕌</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(Z)\subset Int\,f({\mathbb{U}})</annotation></semantics></math>. It is also symplectic on <math id="S11.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S11.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}\omega^{\prime}=(\phi_{1}\circ f)^{*}\omega^{\prime}=f^{*}\phi_{1}^{*}\omega^{\prime}=f^{*}\omega=\omega_{0}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}\omega^{\prime}=(\phi_{1}\circ f)^{*}\omega^{\prime}=f^{*}\phi_{1}^{*}\omega^{\prime}=f^{*}\omega=\omega_{0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p20.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S11.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard symplectic form on <math id="S11.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that since the embedding <math id="S11.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic with respect to the complex structure <math id="S11.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, the embedding <math id="S11.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic with respect to the complex structure <math id="S11.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J:=(\phi_{1})_{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J:=(\phi_{1})_{*}I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p21.1" class="ltx_p">By our construction, <math id="S11.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}\in{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}\in{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S11.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\in{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\in{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S11.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{2}\subset{\mathcal{U}}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}_{2}\subset{\mathcal{U}}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the complex structure
<math id="S11.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is tamed by <math id="S11.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This shows that for <math id="S11.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}:={\mathcal{U}}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}:={\mathcal{U}}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S11.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a tame embedding
<math id="S11.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>
holomorphic with respect to the complex structure
<math id="S11.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> tamed by <math id="S11.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and isotopic to <math id="S11.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S11.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p22" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 11.3 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S11.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p24.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S11.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is a Kähler-type symplectic form on <math id="S11.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S11.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is an
<math id="S11.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame
embedding holomorphic with respect to a Kähler-type complex structure <math id="S11.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> tamed by <math id="S11.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p25.1" class="ltx_p">Then there exists a neighborhood <math id="S11.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S11.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S11.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> such that for each <math id="S11.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>
there exists an <math id="S11.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame
embedding
<math id="S11.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>
holomorphic with respect to a complex structure which is tamed by <math id="S11.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and isotopic to <math id="S11.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p26" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p27.1" class="ltx_p">Choose a neighborhood <math id="S11.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S11.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S11.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p28.1" class="ltx_p">Applying <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> to <math id="S11.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get a neighborhood <math id="S11.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S11.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S11.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{S}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{S}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
such that for each <math id="S11.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a tame
embedding <math id="S11.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> holomorphic with respect to a complex structure <math id="S11.p28.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>
tamed by <math id="S11.p28.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S11.p28.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}=\phi_{*}I\in{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}=\phi_{*}I\in{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S11.p28.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, for all <math id="S11.p28.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S11.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega^{\prime}]_{I^{\prime}}^{p,q}=[\omega^{\prime}]_{\phi_{*}I}^{p,q}=[\omega^{\prime}]_{I}^{p,q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega^{\prime}]_{I^{\prime}}^{p,q}=[\omega^{\prime}]_{\phi_{*}I}^{p,q}=[\omega^{\prime}]_{I}^{p,q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S11.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p29.1" class="ltx_p">The cohomology class <math id="S11.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> depends continuously on <math id="S11.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and the Hodge decomposition (with respect to <math id="S11.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>) of a cohomology class
depends continuously on that class. Also, the Kähler cone of <math id="S11.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is an open subset of <math id="S11.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
In view of these facts, we can choose, if necessary, a smaller neighborhood <math id="S11.p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> and assume without loss of generality that for any <math id="S11.p29.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p30" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S11.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S11.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The class
<math id="S11.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega^{\prime}]_{I^{\prime}}^{1,1}=[\omega^{\prime}]_{I}^{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega^{\prime}]_{I^{\prime}}^{1,1}=[\omega^{\prime}]_{I}^{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S11.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a:=[\omega]_{I^{\prime}}^{2,0}+[\omega]_{I^{\prime}}^{0,2}=[\omega^{\prime}]_{I}^{2,0}+[\omega^{\prime}]_{I}^{0,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a:=[\omega]_{I^{\prime}}^{2,0}+[\omega]_{I^{\prime}}^{0,2}=[\omega^{\prime}]_{I}^{2,0}+[\omega^{\prime}]_{I}^{0,2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S11.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\langle a^{n},[M]\rangle\right|&lt;\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\langle a^{n},[M]\rangle\right|&lt;\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S11.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p31.1" class="ltx_p">This yields that <math id="S11.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S11.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame symplectic embedding holomorphic with respect to the complex structure <math id="S11.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> which is tamed by <math id="S11.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and isotopic to <math id="S11.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S11.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p32" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 11.4</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p33.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S11.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is a Kähler-type symplectic form on <math id="S11.p33.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S11.p33.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a Kähler-type embedding
holomorphic with respect to a Kähler-type complex structure <math id="S11.p33.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p33.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible <math id="S11.p33.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p34.1" class="ltx_p">Then, for any <math id="S11.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
there exists a neighborhood <math id="S11.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S11.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S11.p34.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> such that for each <math id="S11.p34.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>
there exists an <math id="S11.p34.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame
embedding
<math id="S11.p34.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>
holomorphic with respect to a complex structure which is tamed by <math id="S11.p34.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and isotopic to <math id="S11.p34.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S11.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p35" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p35.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 11.5 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p36.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p37.1" class="ltx_p">Then the function <math id="S11.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\mapsto\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\mapsto\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{S}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{S}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is lower
semicontinuous with respect to the <math id="S11.p37.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-topology on <math id="S11.p37.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{S}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{S}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p38" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p38.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p38.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p39.1" class="ltx_p">Consider an arbitrary
<math id="S11.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in\mathit{S}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in\mathit{S}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let us prove that <math id="S11.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is lower semicontinuous
at <math id="S11.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p40.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta,\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta,\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume <math id="S11.p40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\lambda{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\lambda{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is an
<math id="S11.p40.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame
embedding holomorphic with respect to a Kähler-type complex structure <math id="S11.p40.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p40.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> tamed by <math id="S11.p40.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p41.1" class="ltx_p">By <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, there exists a neighborhood <math id="S11.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S11.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S11.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
such that for each <math id="S11.p41.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>
there exists an <math id="S11.p41.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame
embedding
<math id="S11.p41.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}:{\mathbb{W}}\to(M,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Choosing, if necessary, a smaller neighborhood <math id="S11.p41.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we may assume without loss of generality that</p>
<table id="S11.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{{\rm{Vol}\,}(\lambda{\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})}{{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega^{\prime})}&gt;\frac{{\rm{Vol}\,}(\lambda{\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})}{{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)}-\delta,\ \forall\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{{\rm{Vol}\,}(\lambda{\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})}{{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega^{\prime})}&gt;\frac{{\rm{Vol}\,}(\lambda{\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})}{{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)}-\delta,\ \forall\omega^{\prime}\in{\mathcal{U}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p41.2" class="ltx_p">This shows that <math id="S11.p41.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is lower semicontinuous
at <math id="S11.p41.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p42" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p42.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, <math id="S11.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is lower semicontinuous
at all <math id="S11.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in\mathit{S}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in\mathit{S}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that it is a lower semicontinuous function.
<span id="S11.p42.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p43.1" class="ltx_p">Let us now prove <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. For convenience, we restate it here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p44" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p44.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p44.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 11.6 (= <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p45.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be either a torus <math id="S11.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> or a smooth manifold underlying a K3 surface.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p46.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.p46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be
Kähler-type forms on <math id="S11.p46.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> so that
<math id="S11.p46.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{M}\omega_{1}^{n}=\int_{M}\omega_{2}^{n}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{M}\omega_{1}^{n}=\int_{M}\omega_{2}^{n}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and the
forms <math id="S11.p46.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p46.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are irrational.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p47.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.p47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, be compact domains with piecewise-smooth boundary whose interiors contain the origin.
Assume that <math id="S11.p47.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(W_{i};{\mathbb{R}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(W_{i};{\mathbb{R}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S11.p47.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S11.p47.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p48.1" class="ltx_p">Then, for
any <math id="S11.p48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S11.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})=\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})=\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S11.p49" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p49.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p49.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> (= <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>):</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p50" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p50.1" class="ltx_p">Without loss of generality, it is enough to proof the theorem in case <math id="S11.p50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{1})={\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{2})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{1})={\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{2})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p51" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p51.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S11.p51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the space of Kähler forms on <math id="S11.p51.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of total volume <math id="S11.p51.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Equip
<math id="S11.p51.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> with the <math id="S11.p51.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-topology. The group <math id="S11.p51.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> of
orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms of <math id="S11.p51.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S11.p51.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and the
function <math id="S11.p51.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\mapsto\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\mapsto\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> is clearly invariant under the action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p52" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p52.1" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Thm. 9.2]</cite> in the torus case and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV3</a>, Thm. 1.7]</cite> in the K3 case, the <math id="S11.p52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits of <math id="S11.p52.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.p52.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are dense in <math id="S11.p52.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}_{1}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. The function <math id="S11.p52.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\cdot,{\mathbb{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\cdot,{\mathbb{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> is constant
on both orbits. Together with the lower semicontinuity of <math id="S11.p52.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\cdot,{\mathbb{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\cdot,{\mathbb{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> proved in <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>,
this implies that</p>
<table id="S11.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})\leq\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})\leq\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p52.2" class="ltx_p">and, by the same token,</p>
<table id="S11.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})\geq\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})\geq\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p52.3" class="ltx_p">Thus,</p>
<table id="S11.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})=\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})=\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p52.4" class="ltx_p">as required. This proves the theorem.
<span id="S11.p52.4.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p53" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p53.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p53.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 11.7 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p54" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p54.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.p54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be either a torus <math id="S11.p54.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> or a smooth manifold underlying a K3 surface.
Let <math id="S11.p54.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p54.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math>, be compact domains with piecewise-smooth boundary whose interiors contain the origin.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p55" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p55.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S11.p55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(W_{i};{\mathbb{R}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(W_{i};{\mathbb{R}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S11.p55.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S11.p55.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S11.p55.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p56" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p56.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, assume that for any <math id="S11.p56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> there exists an irrational Kähler-type form
<math id="S11.p56.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p56.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.p56.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
so that <math id="S11.p56.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a Kähler-type embedding into <math id="S11.p56.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega_{\delta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega_{\delta})</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S11.p56.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}(\lambda{\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})/{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{\delta})\geq 1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}(\lambda{\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})/{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{\delta})\geq 1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p57" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p57.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p58" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p58.1" class="ltx_p">A. If <math id="S11.p58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\delta}=\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\delta}=\omega</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S11.p58.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.p58.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational, then there exists a dense <math id="S11.p58.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit – the <math id="S11.p58.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of <math id="S11.p58.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> – in the space of Kähler-type symplectic forms of volume <math id="S11.p58.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p58.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
such that for any <math id="S11.p58.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in this orbit we have
<math id="S11.p58.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{K}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{K}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p59" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p59.1" class="ltx_p">B. There exists a <math id="S11.p59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant open dense set of Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S11.p59.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on <math id="S11.p59.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.p59.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and containing, in particular, all irrational Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S11.p59.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that for each <math id="S11.p59.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in this set
<math id="S11.p59.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p60" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p60.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p60.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p61" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p61.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove part A.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p62" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p62.1" class="ltx_p">The hypothesis of part A implies that <math id="S11.p62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S11.p62.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Thm. 9.2]</cite> in the torus case and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV3</a>, Thm. 1.7]</cite> in the K3 case yield that the <math id="S11.p62.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of <math id="S11.p62.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is dense in the space of Kähler-type symplectic forms of volume <math id="S11.p62.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p62.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the function <math id="S11.p62.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\mapsto\nu_{K}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\mapsto\nu_{K}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S11.p62.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant and <math id="S11.p62.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have
<math id="S11.p62.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{K}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{K}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>
for any <math id="S11.p62.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S11.p62.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of <math id="S11.p62.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p63" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p63.1" class="ltx_p">This yields part A of the corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p64" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p64.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove part B.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p65" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p65.1" class="ltx_p">Rescaling, if necessary, the forms <math id="S11.p65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math>, we may assume without loss of generality that they all have the same volume – say, volume <math id="S11.p65.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the forms <math id="S11.p65.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> are all irrational, <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> implies then that <math id="S11.p65.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{\delta},{\mathbb{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{\delta},{\mathbb{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on <math id="S11.p65.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, the hypothesis of the corollary implies that <math id="S11.p65.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{\delta},{\mathbb{W}})\geq 1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{\delta},{\mathbb{W}})\geq 1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S11.p65.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S11.p65.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{\delta},{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{\delta},{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S11.p65.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Using again <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and rescaling the forms, we get that
<math id="S11.p65.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math> for any irrational Kähler-type form <math id="S11.p65.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p65.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, of any volume.
By <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, we get <math id="S11.p65.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega^{\prime\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega^{\prime\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>
also for any Kähler-type form <math id="S11.p65.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> lying in a sufficiently small neighborhood <math id="S11.p65.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> of any irrational form <math id="S11.p65.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S11.p65.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S11.p65.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S11.p65.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit of <math id="S11.p65.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{\omega^{\prime}}U(\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{\omega^{\prime}}U(\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, where the union is taken over all irrational <math id="S11.p65.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\in\mathit{SK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\in\mathit{SK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Clearly, <math id="S11.p65.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S11.p65.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant open dense subset of <math id="S11.p65.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, containing all the irrational forms in <math id="S11.p65.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{SK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑆𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{SK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S11.p65.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math> holds for any <math id="S11.p65.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}\in U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p66" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p66.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof of part B and of the corollary.
<span id="S11.p66.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p67" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p67.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p67.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 11.8 (= <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p68" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p68.1" class="ltx_p">Assume:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p69" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S11.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S11.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S11.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is either <math id="S11.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> or a smooth manifold underlying a K3 surface.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S11.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint union of <math id="S11.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> (possibly different) balls.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S11.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S11.p70" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p70.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p71" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p71.1" class="ltx_p">A.
For any irrational Kähler-type symplectic form <math id="S11.p71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p71.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S11.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{K}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p71.2" class="ltx_p">meaning that Kähler-type embeddings of <math id="S11.p71.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S11.p71.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are
unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p72" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p72.1" class="ltx_p">B. There exists a <math id="S11.p72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant open dense set of Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S11.p72.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on <math id="S11.p72.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.p72.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and containing, in particular, all irrational Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S11.p72.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that for each <math id="S11.p72.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> in this set
<math id="S11.p72.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega,{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math> – meaning that <math id="S11.p72.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame embeddings of <math id="S11.p72.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S11.p72.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> are
unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p73" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p73.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p73.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>
(=<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>):</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p74" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p74.1" class="ltx_p">Assume without loss of generality that
<math id="S11.p74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. (In the case <math id="S11.p74.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> the result is known – see <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p75" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p75.1" class="ltx_p">Part A of the corollary is equivalent to the existence claim proved in <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p76" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p76.1" class="ltx_p">Part B of the corollary follows then from part B of <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>.
<span id="S11.p76.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p77" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p77.1" class="ltx_p">Now let us prove <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. We recall it here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p78" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p78.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p78.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 11.9 (= <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p79" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p79.1" class="ltx_p">Assume:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p80" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S11.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S11.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S11.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M={\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M={\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S11.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is either a disjoint union of <math id="S11.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> identical copies of a <math id="S11.I4.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional polydisk</p>
<table id="S11.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n_{1}}(R_{1})\times\ldots\times B^{2n_{l}}(R_{l}),\ n_{1}+\ldots+n_{l}=n,\ R_{1},\ldots,R_{l}&gt;0,l&gt;1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n_{1}}(R_{1})\times\ldots\times B^{2n_{l}}(R_{l}),\ n_{1}+\ldots+n_{l}=n,\ R_{1},\ldots,R_{l}&gt;0,l&gt;1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.I4.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">or a disjoint union of <math id="S11.I4.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> identical copies of a parallelepiped</p>
<table id="S11.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}):=\left\{\sum_{j=1}^{2n}s_{j}e_{j},0\leq s_{j}\leq 1,j=1,\ldots,2n\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}):=\left\{\sum_{j=1}^{2n}s_{j}e_{j},0\leq s_{j}\leq 1,j=1,\ldots,2n\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.I4.i2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S11.I4.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a basis of the vector space <math id="S11.I4.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S11.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S11.p81" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p81.1" class="ltx_p">Then the following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p82" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p82.1" class="ltx_p">A.
For any positive volume there exists a dense <math id="S11.p82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit (of an irrational Kähler-type symplectic form depending on <math id="S11.p82.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>) in the space of Kähler-type symplectic forms of that volume on <math id="S11.p82.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that for any <math id="S11.p82.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in this orbit we have
<math id="S11.p82.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{K}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{K}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math> –
or, in other words, Kähler-type embeddings <math id="S11.p82.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p83" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p83.1" class="ltx_p">B. There exists a <math id="S11.p83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant open dense set of Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S11.p83.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on <math id="S11.p83.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.p83.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and containing, in particular, all irrational Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S11.p83.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that for each <math id="S11.p83.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in this set
<math id="S11.p83.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math> –
or, in other words, <math id="S11.p83.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame embeddings <math id="S11.p83.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p84" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p84.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p84.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> (=<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>):</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p85" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p85.1" class="ltx_p">Assume without loss of generality that
<math id="S11.p85.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. (In the case <math id="S11.p85.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> the result is known – see <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p86" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p86.1" class="ltx_p">Let us consider the case where <math id="S11.p86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the disjoint union of <math id="S11.p86.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> identical copies of the polydisk
<math id="S11.p86.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{2n_{1}}(R_{1})\times\ldots\times B^{2n_{l}}(R_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{2n_{1}}(R_{1})\times\ldots\times B^{2n_{l}}(R_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case the proof is the following straightforward modification of the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Cor. 3.3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p87" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p87.1" class="ltx_p">Assume, without loss of generality, that
<math id="S11.p87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each <math id="S11.p87.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote by <math id="S11.p87.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> the standard symplectic form on <math id="S11.p87.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each <math id="S11.p87.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,l</annotation></semantics></math> set</p>
<table id="S11.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}:={\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n_{i}}(R_{i}),\Omega_{n_{i}})=\pi^{n_{i}}n_{i}!R_{i}^{2n_{i}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}:={\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n_{i}}(R_{i}),\Omega_{n_{i}})=\pi^{n_{i}}n_{i}!R_{i}^{2n_{i}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p87.2" class="ltx_p">Note that</p>
<table id="S11.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{W}},\Omega_{2n})=k{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n_{1}}(R_{1})\times\ldots B^{2n_{l}}(R_{l}),\Omega_{2n})=kN\prod_{i=1}^{l}v_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>l</mi></munderover><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{W}},\Omega_{2n})=k{\rm{Vol}\,}(B^{2n_{1}}(R_{1})\times\ldots B^{2n_{l}}(R_{l}),\Omega_{2n})=kN\prod_{i=1}^{l}v_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p87.3" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S11.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N:=\frac{n!}{n_{1}!\cdot\ldots\cdot n_{l}!}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">!</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>!</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N:=\frac{n!}{n_{1}!\cdot\ldots\cdot n_{l}!}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p87.4" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S11.p87.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{W}},\omega_{0})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S11.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="kNv_{1}\cdot\ldots\cdot v_{l}=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">kNv_{1}\cdot\ldots\cdot v_{l}=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S11.p88" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p88.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S11.p88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.p88.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1},\ldots,w_{m}\in{\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1},\ldots,w_{m}\in{\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>, set</p>
<table id="S11.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\bf w}}:=(w_{1},\ldots,w_{m})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\bf w}}:=(w_{1},\ldots,w_{m})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S11.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\bar{\bf w}}:=m!w_{1}\cdot\ldots\cdot w_{m}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\bar{\bf w}}:=m!w_{1}\cdot\ldots\cdot w_{m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p88.2" class="ltx_p">and denote
by <math id="S11.p88.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\bar{\bf w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\bar{\bf w}}</annotation></semantics></math> the symplectic form</p>
<table id="S11.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\bar{\bf w}}:=\sum_{i=1}^{m}w_{i}dp_{i}\wedge dq_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\bar{\bf w}}:=\sum_{i=1}^{m}w_{i}dp_{i}\wedge dq_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p88.3" class="ltx_p">on the torus <math id="S11.p88.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2m}={\mathbb{R}}^{2m}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2m}={\mathbb{R}}^{2m}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that
<math id="S11.p88.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{2m},\omega_{\bar{\bf w}})=v_{\bar{\bf w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{2m},\omega_{\bar{\bf w}})=v_{\bar{\bf w}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p89" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p89.1" class="ltx_p">Set</p>
<table id="S11.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}:=k,k_{2}=\ldots=k_{l}:=1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}:=k,k_{2}=\ldots=k_{l}:=1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p89.2" class="ltx_p">so that</p>
<table id="S11.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=k_{1}\cdot\ldots\cdot k_{l}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=k_{1}\cdot\ldots\cdot k_{l}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S11.p90" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p90.1" class="ltx_p">One can choose <math id="S11.p90.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2n_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{2n_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S11.p90.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,l</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the following conditions
hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p91" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p91.1" class="ltx_p">(i) <math id="S11.p91.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{{{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}}}=k_{i}v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{{{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}}}=k_{i}v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S11.p91.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,l</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p92" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p92.1" class="ltx_p">(ii) The vector <math id="S11.p92.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\bf w}}:=({\bar{\bf w}}_{1},\ldots,{\bar{\bf w}}_{k})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\bf w}}:=({\bar{\bf w}}_{1},\ldots,{\bar{\bf w}}_{k})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is
not proportional to a vector with rational coordinates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p93" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p93.1" class="ltx_p">(iii) For each <math id="S11.p93.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,l</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S11.p93.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> the vector <math id="S11.p93.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is
not proportional to a vector with rational coordinates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p94" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p94.1" class="ltx_p">Conditions (i) and (iii) can be achieved since, by our assumption, <math id="S11.p94.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, and since for any <math id="S11.p94.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.p94.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>
the subset of the set</p>
<table id="S11.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{(w_{1},\ldots,w_{m})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m}\ |\ w_{1},\ldots,w_{m}&gt;0,w_{1}\cdot\ldots\cdot w_{m}=C\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.667em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(w_{1},\ldots,w_{m})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m}\ |\ w_{1},\ldots,w_{m}&gt;0,w_{1}\cdot\ldots\cdot w_{m}=C\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p94.2" class="ltx_p">formed by the vectors that are not
proportional to a vector with rational coordinates is dense.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p95" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p95.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the symplectic form <math id="S11.p95.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{{\bar{\bf w}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{{\bar{\bf w}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p95.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> – it is of Kähler type (since it is a linear symplectic form)
and irrational (because of condition (ii)).
Note that, by condition (i),</p>
<table id="S11.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}}\omega_{{\bar{\bf w}}}^{n}=N\prod_{i=1}^{l}v_{{{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}}}=N\prod_{i=1}^{l}k_{i}v_{i}=" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></msub><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>l</mi></munderover><msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>l</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}}\omega_{{\bar{\bf w}}}^{n}=N\prod_{i=1}^{l}v_{{{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}}}=N\prod_{i=1}^{l}k_{i}v_{i}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S11.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=Nk\prod_{i=1}^{l}k_{i}v_{i}=\int_{{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}}\omega^{n}=1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>l</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></msub><msup><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=Nk\prod_{i=1}^{l}k_{i}v_{i}=\int_{{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}}\omega^{n}=1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p95.2" class="ltx_p">Also note that, by condition (ii), for any <math id="S11.p95.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S11.p95.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S11.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n_{i}},\omega_{{{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}}})=v_{{{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}}}=k_{i}v_{i}&gt;\lambda k_{i}v_{i}={\rm{Vol}\,}\bigg(\lambda\bigsqcup_{k_{i}}(B^{2n_{i}}(R_{i}),\Omega_{n_{i}})\bigg)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨆</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n_{i}},\omega_{{{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}}})=v_{{{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}}}=k_{i}v_{i}&gt;\lambda k_{i}v_{i}={\rm{Vol}\,}\bigg(\lambda\bigsqcup_{k_{i}}(B^{2n_{i}}(R_{i}),\Omega_{n_{i}})\bigg),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p95.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S11.p95.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{k_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>⨆</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{k_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the disjoint union of <math id="S11.p95.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> equal copies
of the ball. By condition (iii), for each <math id="S11.p95.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,l</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S11.p95.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the form <math id="S11.p95.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{{{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{{{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p95.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational. Therefore, by <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> and <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, there exists a
Kähler-type embedding</p>
<table id="S11.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:\bigsqcup_{k_{i}}\left(\lambda B^{2n_{i}}(R_{i}),\Omega_{n_{i}}\right)\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n_{i}},\omega_{{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨆</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:\bigsqcup_{k_{i}}\left(\lambda B^{2n_{i}}(R_{i}),\Omega_{n_{i}}\right)\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n_{i}},\omega_{{\bar{\bf w}}_{i}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p95.4" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, the direct product of all such embeddings <math id="S11.p95.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S11.p95.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,l</annotation></semantics></math>, is a Kähler-type embedding</p>
<table id="S11.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(\lambda{\mathbb{W}},\Omega_{2n})\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega_{{\bar{\bf w}}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(\lambda{\mathbb{W}},\Omega_{2n})\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega_{{\bar{\bf w}}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p95.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S11.p95.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the disjoint union
of <math id="S11.p95.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}\cdot\ldots\cdot k_{l}=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}\cdot\ldots\cdot k_{l}=k</annotation></semantics></math>
copies of <math id="S11.p95.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\left(B^{2n_{1}}(R_{1})\times\ldots\times B^{2n_{l}}(R_{l})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\left(B^{2n_{1}}(R_{1})\times\ldots\times B^{2n_{l}}(R_{l})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, we have found an irrational Kähler-type form <math id="S11.p95.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{{\bar{\bf w}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{{\bar{\bf w}}}</annotation></semantics></math> of volume <math id="S11.p95.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p95.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
for any <math id="S11.p95.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a Kähler-type embedding of <math id="S11.p95.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda{\mathbb{W}},\Omega_{2n}=\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda{\mathbb{W}},\Omega_{2n}=\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> into
<math id="S11.p95.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega_{{\bar{\bf w}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega_{{\bar{\bf w}}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> yield the claims of parts A and B
of <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>
in the case where <math id="S11.p95.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the disjoint union of <math id="S11.p95.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> equal polydisks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p96" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p96.1" class="ltx_p">Now let us consider the case where <math id="S11.p96.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the disjoint union of <math id="S11.p96.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> identical copies of
a parallelepiped</p>
<table id="S11.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}):=\left\{\sum_{j=1}^{2n}s_{j}e_{j},0\leq s_{j}\leq 1,j=1,\ldots,2n\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}):=\left\{\sum_{j=1}^{2n}s_{j}e_{j},0\leq s_{j}\leq 1,j=1,\ldots,2n\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p96.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S11.p96.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a basis of the vector space <math id="S11.p96.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p97" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p97.1" class="ltx_p">Note that for any <math id="S11.p97.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, putting <math id="S11.p97.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S11.p97.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda P(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda P(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>, alongside each other in the <math id="S11.p97.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-direction we get a Kähler-type embedding of the disjoint union of <math id="S11.p97.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> identical copies of <math id="S11.p97.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda P(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda P(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S11.p97.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(ke_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(ke_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Considering the composition of this Kähler-type embedding with Kähler-type, or <math id="S11.p97.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame, embeddings of <math id="S11.p97.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(ke_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(ke_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>
into <math id="S11.p97.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we readily get that it suffices to prove the corollary for just one parallelepiped <math id="S11.p97.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>
(i.e., for the case where <math id="S11.p97.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p98" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p98.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore let us prove the corollary for one parallelepiped <math id="S11.p98.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p99" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p99.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the <math id="S11.p99.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n\times 2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n\times 2n</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix <math id="S11.p99.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> whose columns are
the vectors <math id="S11.p99.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> spanning <math id="S11.p99.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p100" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p100.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S11.p100.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in{\mathbb{Z}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> let <math id="S11.p100.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Mat}_{m}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Mat}_{m}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively <math id="S11.p100.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Mat}_{m}({\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Mat}_{m}({\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, be the space of real, respectively rational, <math id="S11.p100.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times m</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p101" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p101.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.p101.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>
be the set of matrices <math id="S11.p101.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S11.p101.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in{\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in{\mathbb{R}}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S11.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda Z^{t}\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;I_{n}\\
-I_{n}&amp;0\end{pmatrix}Z\notin{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{Q}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda Z^{t}\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;I_{n}\\
-I_{n}&amp;0\end{pmatrix}Z\notin{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{Q}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p101.2" class="ltx_p">(Here <math id="S11.p101.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity <math id="S11.p101.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix.)</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="S11.p102" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p102.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p102.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 11.10 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p103" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p103.1" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="S11.p103.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is dense in <math id="S11.p103.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p104" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p104.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p104.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p105" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p105.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S11.p105.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> has the form</p>
<table id="S11.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;B\\
C&amp;D\end{pmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>B</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>C</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>D</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{pmatrix}A&amp;B\\
C&amp;D\end{pmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p105.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S11.p105.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B,C,D\in{\it Mat}_{n}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B,C,D\in{\it Mat}_{n}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S11.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda Z^{t}\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;I_{n}\\
-I_{n}&amp;0\end{pmatrix}Z=\begin{pmatrix}A^{t}C-C^{t}A&amp;A^{t}D-C^{t}B\\
B^{t}C-D^{t}A&amp;B^{t}D-D^{t}B\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda Z^{t}\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;I_{n}\\
-I_{n}&amp;0\end{pmatrix}Z=\begin{pmatrix}A^{t}C-C^{t}A&amp;A^{t}D-C^{t}B\\
B^{t}C-D^{t}A&amp;B^{t}D-D^{t}B\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p105.3" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S11.p105.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the set of <math id="S11.p105.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S11.p105.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,C\in{\it Mat}_{n}({\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,C\in{\it Mat}_{n}({\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.p105.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{t}D-D^{t}B\notin{\it Mat}_{n}({\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{t}D-D^{t}B\notin{\it Mat}_{n}({\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>
is dense in <math id="S11.p105.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> and also lies in <math id="S11.p105.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S11.p105.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is dense in <math id="S11.p105.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p106" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p106.1" class="ltx_p">This proves the lemma.
<span id="S11.p106.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p107" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p107.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S11.p107.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset{\it Mat}_{2n}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense set,
it suffices to prove the corollary only in the case when <math id="S11.p107.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> lies in <math id="S11.p107.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p108" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p108.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, if <math id="S11.p108.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\lambda P\left(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}\right),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\lambda P\left(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}\right),\omega_{0}\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S11.p108.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and some Kähler-type form <math id="S11.p108.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p108.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>,
then there exists a basis <math id="S11.p108.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,e^{\prime}_{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,e^{\prime}_{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S11.p108.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S11.p108.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(e^{\prime}_{1},e^{\prime}_{2},\ldots,e^{\prime}_{2n})\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(e^{\prime}_{1},e^{\prime}_{2},\ldots,e^{\prime}_{2n})\in S</annotation></semantics></math>,
the parallelepiped <math id="S11.p108.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(e^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,e^{\prime}_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,e^{\prime}_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>
contains the parallelepiped <math id="S11.p108.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> and has only slightly bigger volume than it, so that
<math id="S11.p108.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}(\lambda P(e^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,e^{\prime}_{2n}),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}(\lambda P(e^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,e^{\prime}_{2n}),\omega_{0})&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S11.p108.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda P(e^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,e^{\prime}_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda P(e^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,e^{\prime}_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> admits a Kähler-type, or respectively an <math id="S11.p108.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame, embedding into <math id="S11.p108.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the composition of this embedding with the inclusion
<math id="S11.p108.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})\to\lambda P(e^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,e^{\prime}_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})\to\lambda P(e^{\prime}_{1},\ldots,e^{\prime}_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> is a Kähler-type, or respectively an <math id="S11.p108.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame, embedding of
<math id="S11.p108.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S11.p108.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p109" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p109.1" class="ltx_p">Now, in view of <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, it suffices to show that
for any <math id="S11.p109.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S11.p109.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})\in S</annotation></semantics></math>,
there exists an irrational
Kähler-type symplectic form <math id="S11.p109.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p109.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the following condition:
for any <math id="S11.p109.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S11.p109.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\lambda P\left(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}\right)\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\lambda P\left(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}\right)\right)&lt;{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>,
there exists a Kähler-type embedding <math id="S11.p109.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p110" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p110.1" class="ltx_p">Pick <math id="S11.p110.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S11.p110.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})\in S</annotation></semantics></math> and
let us construct such an <math id="S11.p110.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p111" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p111.1" class="ltx_p">Without loss of generality, assume that <math id="S11.p111.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\lambda P\left(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}\right),\omega_{0}\right)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\lambda P\left(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}\right),\omega_{0}\right)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p112" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p112.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the integral lattice <math id="S11.p112.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda:={{\rm Span}\,}_{\mathbb{Z}}\{e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}\}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Span</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda:={{\rm Span}\,}_{\mathbb{Z}}\{e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n}\}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The fundamental domain of <math id="S11.p112.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>
is exactly <math id="S11.p112.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The space <math id="S11.p112.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>
is a torus and the form <math id="S11.p112.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p112.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> induces
a Kähler-type symplectic form on <math id="S11.p112.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> of total volume <math id="S11.p112.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> that will be also denoted by <math id="S11.p112.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The matrix <math id="S11.p112.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})</annotation></semantics></math> induces a linear map <math id="S11.p112.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> mapping <math id="S11.p112.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S11.p112.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. This map induces
a diffeomorphism <math id="S11.p112.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}={\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/{\mathbb{Z}}^{2n}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S11.p112.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})\in S</annotation></semantics></math>,
the form <math id="S11.p112.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega:=F^{*}\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega:=F^{*}\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is an irrational Kähler-type form on <math id="S11.p112.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> of total volume <math id="S11.p112.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p113" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p113.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S11.p113.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> the inclusion <math id="S11.p113.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})\to({\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/\Lambda,\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})\to({\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/\Lambda,\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is a Kähler-type embedding.
This embedding is holomorphic with respect to the complex structure on <math id="S11.p113.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the standard complex structure <math id="S11.p113.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p113.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The composition of this embedding with <math id="S11.p113.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
is then a Kähler-type embedding <math id="S11.p113.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})\to({\mathbb{T}}^{2n},\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> holomorphic with respect to the linear complex
structure <math id="S11.p113.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{*}J_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{*}J_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p113.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p114" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p114.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, for any <math id="S11.p114.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a Kähler-type embedding <math id="S11.p114.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})\to({\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/\Lambda,\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{2n})\to({\mathbb{R}}^{2n}/\Lambda,\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>,
provided <math id="S11.p114.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(e_{1},e_{2},\ldots,e_{2n})\in S</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p115" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p115.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof of the corollary in the case where <math id="S11.p115.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the disjoint union of <math id="S11.p115.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> identical copies of
a parallelepiped.
<span id="S11.p115.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p116" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p116.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. We recall it here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p117" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p117.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p117.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 11.11 (= <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p118" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p118.1" class="ltx_p">Assume:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p119" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S11.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S11.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S11.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth manifold underlying a K3 surface.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S11.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕎</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨆</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}:=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint union of <math id="S11.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> identical copies of a parallelepiped</p>
<table id="S11.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4}):=\left\{\sum_{j=1}^{4}s_{j}e_{j},0\leq s_{j}\leq 1,j=1,\ldots,4\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>4</mn></munderover><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4}):=\left\{\sum_{j=1}^{4}s_{j}e_{j},0\leq s_{j}\leq 1,j=1,\ldots,4\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.I5.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S11.I5.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\ldots,e_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\ldots,e_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is a basis of the vector space <math id="S11.I5.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S11.I5.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S11.p120" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p120.1" class="ltx_p">Then there exists a <math id="S11.p120.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant open dense set of Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S11.p120.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on <math id="S11.p120.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕎</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.p120.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and containing, in particular, all irrational Kähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S11.p120.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that for each <math id="S11.p120.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in this set
<math id="S11.p120.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega^{\prime},{\mathbb{W}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that <math id="S11.p120.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame embeddings of <math id="S11.p120.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda{\mathbb{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕎</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda{\mathbb{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S11.p120.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are unobstructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p121" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p121.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p121.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> (=<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>):</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p122" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p122.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the integral lattice <math id="S11.p122.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda:={{\rm Span}\,}_{\mathbb{Z}}\{2e_{1},2e_{2},2e_{3},2e_{4}\}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Span</mi><mi>ℤ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda:={{\rm Span}\,}_{\mathbb{Z}}\{2e_{1},2e_{2},2e_{3},2e_{4}\}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. The parallelepiped <math id="S11.p122.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})</annotation></semantics></math> is the fundamental domain of the lattice <math id="S11.p122.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda/2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p123" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p123.1" class="ltx_p">Recall the following construction by Kummer of a K3 surface – see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bea2</a>, p.55]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p124" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p124.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the torus <math id="S11.p124.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{4}:={\mathbb{R}}^{4}/\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{4}:={\mathbb{R}}^{4}/\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S11.p124.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:{\mathbb{R}}^{4}\to{\mathbb{T}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:{\mathbb{R}}^{4}\to{\mathbb{T}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection.
Equip the torus <math id="S11.p124.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> with the complex structure <math id="S11.p124.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and the symplectic structure <math id="S11.p124.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the standard complex and symplectic structures on <math id="S11.p124.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The reflection with respect to the origin of <math id="S11.p124.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> defines an involution of <math id="S11.p124.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, which in turn defines a holomorphic and symplectic involution <math id="S11.p124.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:({\mathbb{T}}^{4},J_{0},\omega_{0})\to({\mathbb{T}}^{4},J_{0},\omega_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:({\mathbb{T}}^{4},J_{0},\omega_{0})\to({\mathbb{T}}^{4},J_{0},\omega_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The involution <math id="S11.p124.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S11.p124.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> fixed points – these are exactly the points of <math id="S11.p124.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> that are the images under <math id="S11.p124.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> of
the points in <math id="S11.p124.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> all of whose coordinates with respect to the basis <math id="S11.p124.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{2e_{1},2e_{2},\ldots,2e_{2n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{2e_{1},2e_{2},\ldots,2e_{2n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> are either <math id="S11.p124.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S11.p124.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S11.p124.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{{\mathbb{T}}}^{4},{\widetilde{J}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕋</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{{\mathbb{T}}}^{4},{\widetilde{J}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> be the complex blow-up of <math id="S11.p124.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{4},J_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{4},J_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>
at these 16 points.
The involution <math id="S11.p124.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to a holomorphic involution <math id="S11.p124.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{\iota}}:\tilde{{\mathbb{T}}}^{4}\to\tilde{{\mathbb{T}}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ι</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕋</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕋</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{\iota}}:\tilde{{\mathbb{T}}}^{4}\to\tilde{{\mathbb{T}}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S11.p124.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:=\tilde{{\mathbb{T}}}^{4}/{\widetilde{\iota}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝕋</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ι</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:=\tilde{{\mathbb{T}}}^{4}/{\widetilde{\iota}}</annotation></semantics></math>, equipped with the
complex structure induced by <math id="S11.p124.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{J}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{J}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, is
a complex manifold which is, in fact, a K3 surface (called a Kummer surface). Denote by <math id="S11.p124.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> the complex structure on <math id="S11.p124.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S11.p124.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\widetilde{J}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\widetilde{J}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p125" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p125.1" class="ltx_p">The complex manifold <math id="S11.p125.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> can also be obtained as follows: The space <math id="S11.p125.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex orbifold with <math id="S11.p125.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> isolated singular orbifold points <math id="S11.p125.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>16</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{16}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="S11.p125.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{J}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{J}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> the complex structure on <math id="S11.p125.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S11.p125.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S11.p125.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is the smooth complex manifold obtained by the complex blow-up of <math id="S11.p125.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota,{\overline{J}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota,{\overline{J}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S11.p125.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>16</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{16}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S11.p125.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pr:{\mathbb{T}}^{4}\to{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr:{\mathbb{T}}^{4}\to{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.p125.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi:M\to{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi:M\to{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota</annotation></semantics></math> be the natural projections. The form <math id="S11.p125.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p125.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S11.p125.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant and induces an orbifold Kähler form <math id="S11.p125.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\omega}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\omega}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on the complex orbifold <math id="S11.p125.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota</annotation></semantics></math> which is a smooth Kähler form outside the singularities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p126" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p126.1" class="ltx_p">The points <math id="S11.p126.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\ldots,x_{16}\in{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\ldots,x_{16}\in{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota</annotation></semantics></math> lie on the boundary of <math id="S11.p126.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(pr\circ\pi)\left(P\left(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(pr\circ\pi)\left(P\left(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. The interior <math id="S11.p126.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Int\,P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Int\,P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S11.p126.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})</annotation></semantics></math> projects diffeomorphically onto its image under <math id="S11.p126.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pr\circ\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr\circ\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and is an open dense subset of <math id="S11.p126.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, <math id="S11.p126.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="pr\circ\pi:Int\,P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})\to{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">pr\circ\pi:Int\,P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})\to{\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota</annotation></semantics></math> is an embedding which is
holomorphic with respect to <math id="S11.p126.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{J}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{J}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and symplectic with respect to <math id="S11.p126.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\overline{\omega}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\overline{\omega}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The map
<math id="S11.p126.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:=\Pi^{-1}\circ pr\circ\pi:Int\,P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:=\Pi^{-1}\circ pr\circ\pi:Int\,P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is then an <math id="S11.p126.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p127" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p127.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S11.p127.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Move <math id="S11.p127.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})</annotation></semantics></math> by a parallel translation into the interior of
<math id="S11.p127.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})</annotation></semantics></math>
and then map it by <math id="S11.p127.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S11.p127.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote the resulting map by <math id="S11.p127.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})\to M</annotation></semantics></math>. It is an <math id="S11.p127.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p128" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p128.1" class="ltx_p">Following the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV2</a>, Thm. 3.6]</cite> (or, alternatively, using <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Symplectic forms on complex blow-ups of 𝑀 ‣ 5 Kähler-type embeddings of balls and blow-ups ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>), one can find rational cohomology classes <math id="S11.p128.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}\in H^{2}(\Pi^{-1}(x_{i});{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}\in H^{2}(\Pi^{-1}(x_{i});{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p128.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,16</annotation></semantics></math>, so that
for any <math id="S11.p128.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and any sufficiently small <math id="S11.p128.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\epsilon_{1},\ldots,\epsilon_{16}&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\epsilon_{1},\ldots,\epsilon_{16}&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist
a Kähler form <math id="S11.p128.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S11.p128.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> and sufficiently small neighborhoods <math id="S11.p128.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{x_{i},\epsilon,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{x_{i},\epsilon,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S11.p128.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{-1}(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{-1}(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.p128.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,16</annotation></semantics></math>, in <math id="S11.p128.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
with the following properties:</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p129" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S11.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S11.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S11.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{x_{i},\epsilon,\lambda}\cap f\left(\lambda P\left(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4}\right)\right)=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{x_{i},\epsilon,\lambda}\cap f\left(\lambda P\left(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4}\right)\right)=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S11.I6.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,16</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The form <math id="S11.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with <math id="S11.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi^{*}{\overline{\omega}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi^{*}{\overline{\omega}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> outside <math id="S11.I6.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{i=1}^{16}U_{x_{i},\epsilon,\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>16</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{i=1}^{16}U_{x_{i},\epsilon,\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S11.I6.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega_{\epsilon}]=\Pi^{*}[{\overline{\omega}}_{0}]+\sum_{i=1}^{16}\epsilon_{i}b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>16</mn></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega_{\epsilon}]=\Pi^{*}[{\overline{\omega}}_{0}]+\sum_{i=1}^{16}\epsilon_{i}b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S11.I6.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S11.I6.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.I6.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S11.I6.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota,{\overline{\omega}}_{0})&gt;(1-\epsilon){\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota,{\overline{\omega}}_{0})&gt;(1-\epsilon){\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S11.p130" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p130.1" class="ltx_p">If the vector <math id="S11.p130.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon_{1},\ldots,\epsilon_{16})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>16</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon_{1},\ldots,\epsilon_{16})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{16}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a real multiple of a vector in <math id="S11.p130.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Q}}^{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℚ</mi><mn>16</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Q}}^{16}</annotation></semantics></math>, the cohomology class</p>
<table id="S11.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega_{\epsilon}]=\Pi^{*}[{\overline{\omega}}_{0}]+\sum_{i=1}^{16}\epsilon_{i}b_{i}\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>16</mn></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega_{\epsilon}]=\Pi^{*}[{\overline{\omega}}_{0}]+\sum_{i=1}^{16}\epsilon_{i}b_{i}\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p130.2" class="ltx_p">is a not a real multiple of a rational cohomology class, meaning that <math id="S11.p130.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p131" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p131.1" class="ltx_p">Our construction implies that <math id="S11.p131.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})\to(M,\omega_{\epsilon})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})\to(M,\omega_{\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math> is a Kähler-type embedding which is holomorphic with respect to <math id="S11.p131.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S11.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4}),\omega_{0}\right)=\lambda^{4}{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4}),\omega_{0}\right)=" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4}),\omega_{0}\right)=\lambda^{4}{\rm{Vol}\,}\left(P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4}),\omega_{0}\right)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S11.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\lambda^{4}{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota,{\overline{\omega}}_{0})&gt;\lambda^{4}(1-\epsilon){\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{\epsilon})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝕋</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\lambda^{4}{\rm{Vol}\,}({\mathbb{T}}^{4}/\iota,{\overline{\omega}}_{0})&gt;\lambda^{4}(1-\epsilon){\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{\epsilon}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S11.p132" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p132.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S11.p132.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we can choose
<math id="S11.p132.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently close to <math id="S11.p132.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S11.p132.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently small
so that <math id="S11.p132.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{4}(1-\epsilon)&gt;1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{4}(1-\epsilon)&gt;1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</p>
<table id="S11.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})\to(M,\omega_{\epsilon})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4})\to(M,\omega_{\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p132.2" class="ltx_p">is a Kähler-type embedding
and</p>
<table id="S11.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S11.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Vol}\,}(\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4}),\omega_{0})/{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{\epsilon})\geq 1-\delta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>Vol</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Vol}\,}(\lambda P(e_{1},\ldots,e_{4}),\omega_{0})/{\rm{Vol}\,}(M,\omega_{\epsilon})\geq 1-\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S11.p132.3" class="ltx_p">By <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, this implies the corollary.
<span id="S11.p132.3.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S12" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">12 </span>Hyperkähler case – proofs</h2>

<div id="S12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As we mentioned in Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 The IHS-hyperkähler case ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, the results of Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>
generalize to the IHS-hyperkähler case. In this section we
explain how the proofs of the results in the K3 case should be adjusted for general IHS-hyperkähler manifolds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S12.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed connected and simply-connected manifold carrying IHS-hyperkähler structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 The IHS-hyperkähler case ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> that in the IHS-hyperkähler case the words “Kähler-type” should be replaced everywhere by the words “IHS-hyperkähler-type”. <span id="S12.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accordingly, in this section we will use the same notation as in Section <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a> for IHS-hyperkähler-type objects as for Kähler-type objects. In particular, <math id="S12.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> will denote the space of IHS-hyperkähler-type complex structures, <math id="S12.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}(M)=\mathit{CK}(M)/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}(M)=\mathit{CK}(M)/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the Teichmüller space of IHS-hyperkähler-type complex structures etc.; all the symplectic forms and complex structures will be assumed to be of IHS-hyperkähler-type.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For general IHS-hyperkähler manifolds:</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S12.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S12.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S12.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Teichmüller space <math id="S12.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}(M)=\mathit{CK}(M)/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}(M)=\mathit{CK}(M)/\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> has the structure of a (non-Hausdorff) complex manifold for the same reasons as in Section <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cat</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S12.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S12.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of the intersection form one should consider the Bogomolov-Beauville-Fujiki (BBF) symmetric bilinear form <math id="S12.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S12.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>
(see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bea1</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu</a>]</cite>) – for K3 surfaces the intersection form and the BBF form coincide, up to a multiplicative factor. The BBF form is a primitive integral quadratic form of signature
<math id="S12.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,b_{2}-3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,b_{2}-3)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S12.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}=\dim_{\mathbb{R}}H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}=\dim_{\mathbb{R}}H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The space <math id="S12.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Gr_{++}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Gr_{++}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))</annotation></semantics></math> is defined then using the BBF form <math id="S12.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.I1.i2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.I1.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We will use <math id="S12.I1.i2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\perp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⟂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\perp</annotation></semantics></math> to denote orthogonal complements with respect to <math id="S12.I1.i2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S12.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S12.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Part A of the Torelli theorem (<a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a>) remains true for IHS-hyperkähler manifolds – see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ver6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S12.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S12.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the definition of <math id="S12.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S12.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-classes should be replaced by the so-called MBM cohomology classes in <math id="S12.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AmV1</a>]</cite>. With <math id="S12.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> defined in this way, <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a> remains true for general IHS-hyperkähler manifolds.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S12.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S12.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The analogues of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a> and <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a> remain true for general IHS-hyperkähler manifolds.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S12.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S12.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> be an IHS-hyperkähler-type symplectic form on <math id="S12.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="S12.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it D}_{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it D}_{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> is then defined literally as in Section <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a> with <math id="S12.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-classes being replaced by the MBM classes. <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a> and <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a> remain true in this case – in their proofs one should replace
<math id="S12.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>19</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S12.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}-3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S12.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}-4</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S12.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-classes by MBM classes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AmV1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Next, let us state the analogue of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a> in the IHS-hyperkähler case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 12.1 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The symplectic form <math id="S12.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is compatible with a Campana-simple IHS-hyperkähler-type complex structure on <math id="S12.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if
<math id="S12.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational. In this case, for any connected component <math id="S12.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S12.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S12.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> the set
<math id="S12.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> of Campana-simple structures lying in <math id="S12.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>
is a dense connected subset of <math id="S12.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of <a href="#S12" title="12 Hyperkähler case – proofs ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>
that we are going to give will not be a direct generalization of the proof of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a> and requires
a number of preliminaries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Consider an IHS-hyperkähler-type structure <math id="S12.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{h}}=\{I_{1},I_{2},I_{3},\omega_{1},\omega_{2},\omega_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔥</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{h}}=\{I_{1},I_{2},I_{3},\omega_{1},\omega_{2},\omega_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S12.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="S12.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,c\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,c\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the tensor <math id="S12.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="aI_{1}+bI_{2}+cI_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">aI_{1}+bI_{2}+cI_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a complex structure of hyperkähler type on <math id="S12.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. We will call
any such <math id="S12.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="aI_{1}+bI_{2}+cI_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">aI_{1}+bI_{2}+cI_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S12.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a complex structure induced by <math id="S12.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{h}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Clearly, all complex structures induced by <math id="S12.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in the same connected
component of <math id="S12.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> – we will say that this connected component is <span id="S12.p12.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">favored</span> by <math id="S12.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ver2</a>]</cite>,
we say that a closed subset <math id="S12.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset M</annotation></semantics></math> is
<span id="S12.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">trianalytic</span> (with respect to <math id="S12.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>), if <math id="S12.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>
is a complex
subvariety of <math id="S12.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> for each complex structure <math id="S12.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> induced by
<math id="S12.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathfrak{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔥</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathfrak{h}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 12.2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ver2</a>, Thm. 4.1]</cite></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S12.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S12.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is Poincaré-dual to the fundamental class <math id="S12.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Z]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Z]</annotation></semantics></math> of a trianalytic <math id="S12.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset M</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S12.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant with respect to the action of any induced complex structure on <math id="S12.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S12.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S12.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected component of <math id="S12.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S12.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed subvariety of <math id="S12.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> for a certain (IHS-hyperkähler-type) complex structure on <math id="S12.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> lying in <math id="S12.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kur</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx81" title="" class="ltx_ref">SolV</a>]</cite>, we say that <math id="S12.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S12.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">absolutely trianalytic</span>
(with respect to <math id="S12.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>)
if the cohomology class Poincaré-dual to the fundamental class of <math id="S12.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is
of type <math id="S12.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,p)</annotation></semantics></math> for all complex structures <math id="S12.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By <a href="#S12" title="12 Hyperkähler case – proofs ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, such a
manifold is trianalytic with respect to any IHS-hyperkähler structure favoring <math id="S12.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S12.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by
<math id="S12.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\subset{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\subset{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the set of all <math id="S12.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S12.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows e.g. from the Torelli theorem for hyperkähler manifolds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ver6</a>]</cite> that <math id="S12.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}</annotation></semantics></math> is an analytic submanifold of
<math id="S12.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\sf Per}({\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a})=Gr_{++}\left(a^{\bot}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖯𝖾𝗋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>⊥</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\sf Per}({\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a})=Gr_{++}\left(a^{\bot}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p20.1" class="ltx_p">For a real vector space <math id="S12.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with a non-degenerate indefinite
quadratic form we denote by <math id="S12.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO^{+}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO^{+}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> the identity component of <math id="S12.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO(W)</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, <math id="S12.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO^{+}(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO^{+}(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> stands for the identity component of the Lie group <math id="S12.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p21.1" class="ltx_p">For the proof of <a href="#S12" title="12 Hyperkähler case – proofs ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> we will need the following algebraic lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p22" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 12.3 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S12.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> be a real vector space equipped with a non-degenerate
quadratic form <math id="S12.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> of signature <math id="S12.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,l)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p24.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the set <math id="S12.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S12.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in SO^{+}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in SO^{+}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> fixing pointwise a linear subspace <math id="S12.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset W</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{R}}U=\dim_{\mathbb{R}}W-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{R}}U=\dim_{\mathbb{R}}W-2</annotation></semantics></math> whose orthogonal complement
is a positive 2-dimensional plane in <math id="S12.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p25.1" class="ltx_p">Then the set <math id="S12.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}(W)</annotation></semantics></math>
generates <math id="S12.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO^{+}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO^{+}(W)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p26" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S12" title="12 Hyperkähler case – proofs ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p27.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S12.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be the
subgroup of <math id="S12.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO^{+}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO^{+}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> generated by <math id="S12.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}(W)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Clearly, <math id="S12.p27.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is normal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p28.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S12.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\neq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\neq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the group
<math id="S12.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO^{+}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO^{+}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected simple Lie group (in the sense of Lie theory). Therefore it follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rag</a>]</cite> that any normal subgroup of <math id="S12.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO^{+}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO^{+}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> is either
the whole <math id="S12.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO^{+}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO^{+}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> or is contained in the center of <math id="S12.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO^{+}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO^{+}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> (which is discrete). Hence, <math id="S12.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=SO^{+}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=SO^{+}(W)</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that
<math id="S12.p28.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}(W)</annotation></semantics></math>
generates <math id="S12.p28.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO^{+}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO^{+}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S12.p28.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\neq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\neq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p29.1" class="ltx_p">Let us consider the case <math id="S12.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=2</annotation></semantics></math> – i.e., the case when <math id="S12.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> has signature <math id="S12.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Identify <math id="S12.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,q)</annotation></semantics></math> with the space <math id="S12.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Mat}_{2}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Mat}_{2}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> of real <math id="S12.p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math>-matrices equipped with the bilinear form <math id="S12.p29.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle A,B\rangle=-tr(AJBJ)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle A,B\rangle=-tr(AJBJ)</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S12.p29.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}0&amp;-1\\
1&amp;0\\
\end{array}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=\left(\begin{array}[]{cc}0&amp;-1\\
1&amp;0\\
\end{array}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the following action of <math id="S12.p29.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S12.p29.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\cong{\it Mat}_{2}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\cong{\it Mat}_{2}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>:
each <math id="S12.p29.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{1}\times h_{2}\in SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{1}\times h_{2}\in SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S12.p29.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in{\it Mat}_{2}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in{\it Mat}_{2}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="S12.p29.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\mapsto h_{1}Ah_{2}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\mapsto h_{1}Ah_{2}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This action preserves the bilinear form and induces a homomorphism <math id="S12.p29.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\to SO^{+}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\to SO^{+}(W)</annotation></semantics></math>
whose kernel is <math id="S12.p29.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\pm Id\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\pm Id\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The subgroup <math id="S12.p29.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{-1}(N)\subset SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{-1}(N)\subset SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is normal.
The Lie group <math id="S12.p29.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected semi-simple Lie group. Therefore it follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rag</a>]</cite> that any normal subgroup of <math id="S12.p29.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> which is not the whole <math id="S12.p29.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is contained either in <math id="S12.p29.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times\{\pm Id\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times\{\pm Id\}</annotation></semantics></math> or in
<math id="S12.p29.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\pm Id\}\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\pm Id\}\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p30.1" class="ltx_p">Let us show that <math id="S12.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{-1}\left({\mathcal{F}}(W)\right)\nsubseteq SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times\{\pm Id\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{-1}\left({\mathcal{F}}(W)\right)\nsubseteq SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times\{\pm Id\}</annotation></semantics></math> and consequently <math id="S12.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{-1}(N)\nsubseteq SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times\{\pm Id\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{-1}(N)\nsubseteq SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times\{\pm Id\}</annotation></semantics></math> (the claim <math id="S12.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{-1}(N)\nsubseteq\{\pm Id\}\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{-1}(N)\nsubseteq\{\pm Id\}\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is proved similarly). Indeed, if an element <math id="S12.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\times Id\in SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\times Id\in SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> is sent by <math id="S12.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S12.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}(W)</annotation></semantics></math>, then its action on <math id="S12.p30.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Mat}_{2}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Mat}_{2}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> fixes a two-dimensional subspace of <math id="S12.p30.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\it Mat}_{2}({\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑀𝑎𝑡</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\it Mat}_{2}({\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>. This easily implies that <math id="S12.p30.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> has a two-dimensional eigenspace for the eigenvalue <math id="S12.p30.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that <math id="S12.p30.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=Id</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S12.p30.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{-1}\left({\mathcal{F}}(W)\right)\nsubseteq SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times\{\pm Id\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{-1}\left({\mathcal{F}}(W)\right)\nsubseteq SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times\{\pm Id\}</annotation></semantics></math>
and consequently <math id="S12.p30.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{-1}(N)=SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{-1}(N)=SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})\times SL(2,{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.p30.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=SO^{+}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=SO^{+}(W)</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that
<math id="S12.p30.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}(W)</annotation></semantics></math>
generates <math id="S12.p30.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO^{+}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO^{+}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> also for <math id="S12.p30.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p31.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof of the lemma.
<span id="S12.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p32.1" class="ltx_p">Now we are ready to prove <a href="#S12" title="12 Hyperkähler case – proofs ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p33" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S12" title="12 Hyperkähler case – proofs ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p34.1" class="ltx_p">If the cohomology class <math id="S12.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is rational,
then by part 1 of
<a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, there are no Campana-simple complex structures compatible with <math id="S12.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p35.1" class="ltx_p">Assume that <math id="S12.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is irrational and <math id="S12.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected component of
<math id="S12.p35.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S12.p35.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p36.1" class="ltx_p">The analogue of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a> in the
IHS-hyperkähler case implies that <math id="S12.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense connected subset of the analytic subvariety
<math id="S12.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S12.p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, for each <math id="S12.p36.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.p36.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, there are only two possible options:</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p37" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p37.1" class="ltx_p">(1) <math id="S12.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}={\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}={\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math>, and consequently <math id="S12.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)={\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)={\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p38" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p38.1" class="ltx_p">(2) <math id="S12.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper (possibly empty) complex subvariety of <math id="S12.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math> and consequently <math id="S12.p38.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper complex subvariety of <math id="S12.p38.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p39" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p39.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p39.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 12.4 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p40.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S12.p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset M</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex subvariety of <math id="S12.p40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> (some complex structure <math id="S12.p40.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>) which is not absolutely trianalytic
with respect to <math id="S12.p40.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S12.p40.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{even}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{even}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> be the cohomology class Poincaré-dual to <math id="S12.p40.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Z]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Z]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p41.1" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S12.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}\nsubseteqq{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⫅̸</mo><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}\nsubseteqq{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math> (meaning that option (2) holds for <math id="S12.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p42" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p42.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S12.p42.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S12" title="12 Hyperkähler case – proofs ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p43.1" class="ltx_p">We start with a number of preparations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p44" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p44.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S12.p44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.p44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the Hodge rotation map <math id="S12.p44.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\to H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\to H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> that acts on each <math id="S12.p44.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{I}^{p,q}(M;{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{I}^{p,q}(M;{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math>
as multiplication by <math id="S12.p44.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\sqrt{-1}\&gt;(p-q)t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.220em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\sqrt{-1}\&gt;(p-q)t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a map preserves <math id="S12.p44.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> and its fixed point set is exactly the union of <math id="S12.p44.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{I}^{p,p}(M;{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{I}^{p,p}(M;{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S12.p44.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. We will view the action of this map on <math id="S12.p44.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> as an element of
<math id="S12.p44.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GL\left(H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GL\left(H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and denote it by <math id="S12.p44.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{I}(t):H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\to H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{I}(t):H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\to H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The restriction of each <math id="S12.p44.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{I}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{I}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S12.p44.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>
preserves the BBF form <math id="S12.p44.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and lies in <math id="S12.p44.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO^{+}\left(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO^{+}\left(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. The maps <math id="S12.p44.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{I}(t)|_{H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{I}(t)|_{H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.p44.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, are exactly the maps that act trivially on the subspace <math id="S12.p44.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>
and rotate its orthogonal complement, which is the positive 2-dimensional plane <math id="S12.p44.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{I}^{2,0}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\oplus H_{I}^{0,2}(M;{\mathbb{C}}))\cap H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{I}^{2,0}(M;{\mathbb{C}})\oplus H_{I}^{0,2}(M;{\mathbb{C}}))\cap H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p45.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S12.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\subset GL\left(H^{even}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\subset GL\left(H^{even}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math> the
subgroup generated by
<math id="S12.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{I}(t)|_{H^{even}(M;{\mathbb{R}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{I}(t)|_{H^{even}(M;{\mathbb{R}})}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S12.p45.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.p45.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
restriction to <math id="S12.p45.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> defines a homomorphism <math id="S12.p45.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\to SO^{+}\left(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\to SO^{+}\left(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ver3</a>, Thm. 2.2]</cite> and
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ver1</a>, Corollary 8.2]</cite>
that this homomorphism
is, in fact, an isomorphism.
(On <math id="S12.p45.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{odd}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{odd}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the group generated by <math id="S12.p45.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{I}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{I}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S12.p45.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.p45.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, acts as
the identity component of <math id="S12.p45.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spin}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Spin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spin}(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}}))</annotation></semantics></math>,
with the center of the spinor group acting as <math id="S12.p45.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-\operatorname{Id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\operatorname{Id}</annotation></semantics></math>.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p46.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S12.p46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{[\omega]}\subset G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{[\omega]}\subset G</annotation></semantics></math>
the subgroup generated by all Hodge rotation maps <math id="S12.p46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{I}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{I}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.p46.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.p46.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.p46.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each such <math id="S12.p46.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{I}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{I}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> fixes <math id="S12.p46.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> because <math id="S12.p46.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]\in H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]\in H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S12.p46.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed by <math id="S12.p46.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S12.p46.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math>
lies in the stabilizer of <math id="S12.p46.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S12.p46.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\cong SO^{+}\left(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\cong SO^{+}\left(H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. The latter stabilizer is isomorphic to <math id="S12.p46.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO^{+}([\omega]^{\perp})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO^{+}([\omega]^{\perp})</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that
the restriction of the BBF form <math id="S12.p46.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S12.p46.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]^{\perp}\subset H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]^{\perp}\subset H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> has signature <math id="S12.p46.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,b_{2}-3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,b_{2}-3)</annotation></semantics></math>. Now <a href="#S12" title="12 Hyperkähler case – proofs ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>
yields that the maps <math id="S12.p46.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{I}(t)|_{H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{I}(t)|_{H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{R}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.p46.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.p46.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math>, generate the stabilizer. In other words,
<math id="S12.p46.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with the stabilizer of <math id="S12.p46.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S12.p46.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p47.1" class="ltx_p">For the class <math id="S12.p47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{even}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{even}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> given in the hypothesis of the lemma, denote by <math id="S12.p47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> the stabilizer of
<math id="S12.p47.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S12.p47.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p48.1" class="ltx_p">Now we are ready to prove the lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p49" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p49.1" class="ltx_p">Assume, by contradiction, that <math id="S12.p49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}\subset{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}\subset{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning that option (1) above, and not (2), holds for <math id="S12.p49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S12.p49.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is of type <math id="S12.p49.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,p)</annotation></semantics></math> for all
complex structures <math id="S12.p49.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S12.p49.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, <math id="S12.p49.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed by <math id="S12.p49.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{I}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{I}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> for all such <math id="S12.p49.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="S12.p49.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, <math id="S12.p49.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math> fixes <math id="S12.p49.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> or, in other words,</p>
<table id="S12.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SO^{+}(2,b_{2}-3)\cong G_{[\omega]}\subset G_{a}\subset G\cong SO^{+}(3,b_{2}-3)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SO^{+}(2,b_{2}-3)\cong G_{[\omega]}\subset G_{a}\subset G\cong SO^{+}(3,b_{2}-3).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.p49.2" class="ltx_p">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Lem. 9.9]</cite>,
any intermediate subgroup
<math id="S12.p49.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{[\omega]}\subset H\subset G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{[\omega]}\subset H\subset G</annotation></semantics></math> is
equal either to <math id="S12.p49.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math> or to <math id="S12.p49.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
This implies that
<math id="S12.p49.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> equals
either <math id="S12.p49.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S12.p49.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S12.p49.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is not rational, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Lem. 9.10]</cite> yields that <math id="S12.p49.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{a}\neq G_{[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{a}\neq G_{[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S12.p49.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is not
absolutely trianalytic, there exists <math id="S12.p49.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S12.p49.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\notin H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\notin H_{I}^{1,1}(M;{\mathbb{R}})</annotation></semantics></math> and consequently
<math id="S12.p49.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is not fixed by <math id="S12.p49.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{I}(t)\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{I}(t)\in G</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S12.p49.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="S12.p49.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{I}(t)\notin G_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∉</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{I}(t)\notin G_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.p49.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{a}\neq G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{a}\neq G</annotation></semantics></math>.
This yields a contradiction and finishes the proof of the lemma.
<span id="S12.p49.2.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p50" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p50.1" class="ltx_p">Now let us finish the proof
of <a href="#S12" title="12 Hyperkähler case – proofs ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p51" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p51.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S12.p51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}\subset H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})\setminus\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}\subset H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})\setminus\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all <math id="S12.p51.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{*}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.p51.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, for
which <math id="S12.p51.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper (possibly empty) complex subvariety of <math id="S12.p51.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},[\omega]}</annotation></semantics></math> and consequently <math id="S12.p51.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper complex subvariety of <math id="S12.p51.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
By <a href="#S12" title="12 Hyperkähler case – proofs ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, <math id="S12.p51.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> includes, in particular, all <math id="S12.p51.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in H^{2}(M;{\mathbb{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> that are Poincaré-dual to the fundamental classes of
not absolutely trianalytic subvarieties (with respect to <math id="S12.p51.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p52" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p52.1" class="ltx_p">The set</p>
<table id="S12.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S12.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S:={\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\setminus\bigcup_{a\in{\mathcal{A}}}\left({\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S:={\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\setminus\bigcup_{a\in{\mathcal{A}}}\left({\tt T}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0},a}\cap{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S12.p52.2" class="ltx_p">is connected and dense in <math id="S12.p52.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. For any <math id="S12.p52.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{C}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S12.p52.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in S</annotation></semantics></math>, the complex manifold <math id="S12.p52.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> can contain only absolutely trianalytic subvarieties. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Lem. 7.9]</cite>, the union of all such subvarieties of <math id="S12.p52.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> is of measure zero, meaning that <math id="S12.p52.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is Campana-simple and <math id="S12.p52.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I]\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I]\in{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence,
<math id="S12.p52.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)\subset{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)\subset{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S12.p52.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is connected and dense in <math id="S12.p52.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, so is <math id="S12.p52.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝚃𝚎𝚒𝚌𝚑</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\tt Teich}_{{\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\omega)\cap{\tt Teich}^{s}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p53" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p53.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof of the proposition.
<span id="S12.p53.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p54" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p54.1" class="ltx_p">We are now ready to discuss the analogue of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>
in the IHS-hyperkähler case, as described in Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 The IHS-hyperkähler case ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p55" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p55.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of the claims of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>
in the IHS-hyperkähler case
(apart from the claim on the transitivity of the <math id="S12.p55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-action)
is the same as in the K3 case – one just needs
to use the generalizations of the results of Section <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a> to the IHS-hyperkähler case discussed above
(instead of the corresponding results for the K3 surfaces) and use <a href="#S9" title="9 Existence of Kähler-type embeddings of balls and Campana-simple complex structures ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>
in the simply-connected case involving Campana-simple complex structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p56" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p56.1" class="ltx_p">As for the transitivity of the action of <math id="S12.p56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, we cannot say anything in the IHS-hyperkähler case, as we have no analogue
of part B of <a href="#S10.SS2" title="10.2 Campana-simple complex structures on K3 surfaces ‣ 10 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.2</span></a> for IHS-hyperkähler manifolds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p57" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p57.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, let us discuss the results on tame embeddings into <math id="S12.p57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> for an IHS-hyperkähler-type form <math id="S12.p57.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p58" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p58.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from Section <a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5 The IHS-hyperkähler case ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> that there are two possibilities to define tame embeddings into such an <math id="S12.p58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>. One possibility is to use Kähler-type complex structures tamed by <math id="S12.p58.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Another possibility is to use instead
IHS-hyperkähler-type complex structures tamed by <math id="S12.p58.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S12.p59" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.p59.1" class="ltx_p">The claim <math id="S12.p59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})=\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕎</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{1},{\mathbb{W}})=\nu_{T,\varepsilon}(M,\omega_{2},{\mathbb{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 The case of tori and K3 surfaces ‣ 3 Main results ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> holds for both definitions of <math id="S12.p59.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, as long as
<math id="S12.p59.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S12.p59.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> lie in the same connected component of the space of IHS-hyperkähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S12.p59.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, it is easy to see that <a href="#S11" title="11 Tame embeddings of arbitrary domains ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> holds for both definitions of <math id="S12.p59.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{T,\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{T,\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The relevant claim about the density of the <math id="S12.p59.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Diff</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}^{+}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits of the form <math id="S12.p59.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S12.p59.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in the space of forms of the same volume in the same connected component of the space of IHS-hyperkähler-type symplectic forms on <math id="S12.p59.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Thm. 9.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S13" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">13 </span>Appendix</h2>

<div id="S13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we discuss various well-known facts concerning
the dependence of the Hodge decomposition on the complex structure,
the deformations of complex structures, and the Moser method, that we used in the paper and
for most of which we could not find a direct reference in the literature.</p>
</div>
<section id="S13.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">13.1 </span>Dependence of the Hodge decomposition on the complex structure</h3>

<div id="S13.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we prove two auxiliary facts about Kähler manifolds. These facts must have been known to the experts
but we have not been able to find a reference to them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S13.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n=\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></msub><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n=\dim_{\mathbb{R}}M</annotation></semantics></math>, is a closed manifold admitting Kähler structures – i.e., <math id="S13.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall that we use the <math id="S13.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-topology on <math id="S13.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S13.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=0,1,\ldots,2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=0,1,\ldots,2n</annotation></semantics></math> denote by <math id="S13.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{l}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{l}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> the space of smooth complex-valued differential <math id="S13.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-forms on <math id="S13.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each <math id="S13.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> let</p>
<table id="S13.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A^{l}(M)=\oplus_{p+q=l}A_{I}^{p,q}(M)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">⊕</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{l}(M)=\oplus_{p+q=l}A_{I}^{p,q}(M)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S13.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">be the <math id="S13.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>-decomposition of <math id="S13.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{l}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{l}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S13.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{I}^{1,0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{I}^{1,0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>
be the space of <math id="S13.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic 1-forms on <math id="S13.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, with respect to <math id="S13.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">By Hodge theory, <math id="S13.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{C}}H^{1}(M;{\mathbb{C}})=2\dim_{\mathbb{C}}H_{I}^{1,0}(M)=2\dim_{\mathbb{C}}H^{0}(\Omega_{I}^{1}(M))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{C}}H^{1}(M;{\mathbb{C}})=2\dim_{\mathbb{C}}H_{I}^{1,0}(M)=2\dim_{\mathbb{C}}H^{0}(\Omega_{I}^{1}(M))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S13.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:=\dim_{\mathbb{C}}H^{1}(M;{\mathbb{C}})/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:=\dim_{\mathbb{C}}H^{1}(M;{\mathbb{C}})/2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S13.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 13.1 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S13.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\in\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\in\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Then there exists a neighborhood <math id="S13.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S13.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S13.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> with the following property:</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S13.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> one can choose a basis <math id="S13.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{1}(I),\ldots,\theta_{m}(I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{1}(I),\ldots,\theta_{m}(I)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S13.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\Omega_{I}^{1}(M))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\Omega_{I}^{1}(M))</annotation></semantics></math>
continuously with respect to <math id="S13.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S13.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S13.SS1" title="13.1 Dependence of the Hodge decomposition on the complex structure ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.1</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Pick a <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant Riemannian metric <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a neighborhood <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> such that
for any <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently close to <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>
we have an <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant Riemannian metric <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g+I^{*}g)/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g+I^{*}g)/2</annotation></semantics></math> that depends continuously on <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and coincides with <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I=J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I=J</annotation></semantics></math>.
Together with <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> such an <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant Riemannian metric defines a Hermitian metric <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> that depends continuously on <math id="S13.SS1.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S13.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="S13.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=0,1,\ldots,2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=0,1,\ldots,2n</annotation></semantics></math> the Hermitian metric <math id="S13.SS1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> allows to define the Laplace-Beltrami operator on <math id="S13.SS1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{l}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{l}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> that will be
denoted <math id="S13.SS1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{I}^{l}:A^{l}(M)\to A^{l}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{I}^{l}:A^{l}(M)\to A^{l}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. This is an elliptic operator. Denote the kernel of <math id="S13.SS1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{I}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{I}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the space of harmonic <math id="S13.SS1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-forms with respect to <math id="S13.SS1.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{I}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{I}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math>) by <math id="S13.SS1.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{I}^{l}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{I}^{l}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S13.SS1.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim_{\mathbb{C}}{\mathcal{H}}_{I}^{l}(M)=\dim_{\mathbb{C}}H^{l}(M;{\mathbb{C}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℂ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim_{\mathbb{C}}{\mathcal{H}}_{I}^{l}(M)=\dim_{\mathbb{C}}H^{l}(M;{\mathbb{C}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Also, <math id="S13.SS1.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{I}^{1}(M)\cap A_{I}^{1,0}(M)=\Omega_{I}^{1,0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{I}^{1}(M)\cap A_{I}^{1,0}(M)=\Omega_{I}^{1,0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>.
These results follow from the classical
Hodge theory (see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wel</a>, Sec. IV.4, IV.5]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Thus we have obtained that for each <math id="S13.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> the operators <math id="S13.SS1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{I}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{I}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> form a family of elliptic operators depending
continuously on <math id="S13.SS1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>
so that the kernels of these operators all have the same dimension. Then these kernels – i.e., the spaces <math id="S13.SS1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}^{l}_{I}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}^{l}_{I}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> – form a continuous vector bundle over <math id="S13.SS1.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This
follows from a standard argument essentially contained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">At2</a>, Lem. 2.1]</cite>. Namely, one passes to a Hilbert completion of
<math id="S13.SS1.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{l}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{l}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and extends the continuous family of elliptic operators <math id="S13.SS1.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{I}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{I}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS1.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>, to a continuous family of Fredholm operators that, by elliptic regularity, have the same kernels, all of the same dimension. An easy functional analysis argument implies then that the kernels of these Fredholm operators form a continuous vector bundle (in fact, we only need the operators to be bounded with a closed image and these conditions are satisfied by any Fredholm operator).</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Since the spaces <math id="S13.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{H}}_{I}^{1}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{H}}_{I}^{1}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>, form a continuous vector bundle over <math id="S13.SS1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
so do their <math id="S13.SS1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant parts – i.e., the spaces <math id="S13.SS1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{I}^{1,0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>I</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{I}^{1,0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> – which are all of the same dimension <math id="S13.SS1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.
This readily implies the proposition.
<span id="S13.SS1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S13.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">13.2 </span>Deformation families of complex structures</h3>

<div id="S13.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S13.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> be a complex manifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S13.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> are connected (not necessarily Hausdorff)
smooth manifolds. Let <math id="S13.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}\in{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}\in{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a marked point.
Let <math id="S13.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth map.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S13.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper fiber bundle whose fibers are equipped with complex structures depending
smoothly on the fiber and the fiber over <math id="S13.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S13.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, we say that <math id="S13.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\to({\mathcal{B}},s_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\to({\mathcal{B}},s_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S13.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">smooth deformation family</span>, or a
<span id="S13.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">smooth deformation of <math id="S13.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S13.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> are complex manifolds and the map <math id="S13.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a proper holomorphic submersion, then it is a fiber bundle (by Ehresmann’s lemma, the fibration admits <span id="S13.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">smooth</span> local trivializations). The fibers of <math id="S13.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\to{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> are then closed
complex submanifolds of <math id="S13.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> that are diffeomorphic to <math id="S13.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote
by <math id="S13.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> the fiber over <math id="S13.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> and denote by <math id="S13.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> the complex
structure on <math id="S13.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the complex structure on <math id="S13.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume
that <math id="S13.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{s_{0}}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{s_{0}}=I</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case we say that <math id="S13.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{X}}\to({\mathcal{B}},s_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{X}}\to({\mathcal{B}},s_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is
a <span id="S13.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">complex-analytic deformation family</span>, or a <span id="S13.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">complex-analytic deformation of <math id="S13.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Given a complex structure <math id="S13.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S13.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>,
a smoothly trivial smooth (respectively complex-analytic) deformation family
<math id="S13.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times U\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times U\to U</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S13.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M,I)</annotation></semantics></math> over an open set <math id="S13.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset{\mathbb{C}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset{\mathbb{C}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> (respectively <math id="S13.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="S13.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}=0\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}=0\in U</annotation></semantics></math>,
is called a
<span id="S13.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">local smooth (respectively complex-analytic) deformation</span> of <math id="S13.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. It can be viewed as a smooth
(respectively complex analytic) family <math id="S13.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{I_{s}\}_{s\in U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{I_{s}\}_{s\in U}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{0}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{0}=I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{I_{s}\}_{s\in U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{I_{s}\}_{s\in U}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{0}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{0}=I</annotation></semantics></math>, is a smooth local deformation of a Kähler-type complex structure <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
The Kodaira-Spencer stability theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">KoS2</a>]</cite> says that for any symplectic form <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> compatible with <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a neighborhood <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and a smooth family <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\omega_{s}\}_{s\in U^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\omega_{s}\}_{s\in U^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{0}=\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{0}=\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, of symplectic forms <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
compatible with <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; in particular, each complex structure <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, is of Kähler type.
It follows from the Kodaira-Spencer stability theorem (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">EV1</a>, Thm. 5.6]</cite>) that there exists a neighborhood <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime\prime}\subset U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime\prime}\subset U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> so that for each <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in U^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in U^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> the cohomology class <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\omega]_{I_{s}}^{1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\omega]_{I_{s}}^{1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is Kähler with respect
to <math id="S13.SS2.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S13.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 13.2 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The space <math id="S13.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is locally <math id="S13.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-path-connected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S13.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S13.SS2" title="13.2 Deformation families of complex structures ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.2</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Kuranishi’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ku1</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ku2</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ku3</a>]</cite>
implies that the <math id="S13.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>-action on the space of complex structures on <math id="S13.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> admits local slices that are finite-dimensional analytic spaces. This implies that the space of complex structures on <math id="S13.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is locally path-connected.
Therefore,
since <math id="S13.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Diff</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> acts trivially on the cohomology and preserves <math id="S13.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math>, the Kodaira-Spencer stability theorem yields that the space <math id="S13.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathit{CK}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐶𝐾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathit{CK}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> is locally <math id="S13.SS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-path-connected.
This proves the proposition.
<span id="S13.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S13.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">13.3 </span>Relative version of Moser’s method</h3>

<div id="S13.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we recall the following (well-known) relative version of Moser’s method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mos</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S13.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 13.3 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S13.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed manifold.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">I. Assume that <math id="S13.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset N</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed domain with a piecewise-smooth boundary, such that <math id="S13.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(X;{\mathbb{R}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(X;{\mathbb{R}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S13.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Omega_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Omega_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, be a smooth family of cohomologous symplectic forms on <math id="S13.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, so that
all the forms <math id="S13.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, coincide on a neighborhood of <math id="S13.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Then there exists an isotopy <math id="S13.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}:N\to N\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}:N\to N\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}=Id</annotation></semantics></math>, such that for each <math id="S13.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> the diffeomorphism <math id="S13.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is identity on a neighborhood of <math id="S13.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S13.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}^{*}\Omega_{0}=\Omega_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}^{*}\Omega_{0}=\Omega_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">II. Assume that <math id="S13.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset N</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper submanifold. Let <math id="S13.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Omega_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Omega_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, be a smooth family of cohomologous symplectic forms on <math id="S13.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, so that
all the forms <math id="S13.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{t}|_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{t}|_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, are non-degenerate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Then there exists an isotopy <math id="S13.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}:N\to N\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}:N\to N\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}=Id</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S13.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(X)=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(X)=X</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S13.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}^{*}\Omega_{0}=\Omega_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}^{*}\Omega_{0}=\Omega_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>
for each <math id="S13.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">III. Assume that <math id="S13.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N={\mathbb{C}}P^{n}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N={\mathbb{C}}P^{n}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}:={\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\subset N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}:={\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}\times{\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\subset N</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}:={\mathbb{C}}P^{n}\times\textrm{pt}\subset N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>pt</mtext></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}:={\mathbb{C}}P^{n}\times\textrm{pt}\subset N</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S13.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Omega_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Omega_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, be a smooth family of cohomologous symplectic forms on <math id="S13.SS3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, so that
all the forms <math id="S13.SS3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{t}|_{X_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{t}|_{X_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, are non-degenerate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Then for every open neighborhood <math id="S13.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S13.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}\cap X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}\cap X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists an isotopy <math id="S13.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}:N\to N\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}:N\to N\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}=Id</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</p>
<ul id="S13.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S13.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S13.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S13.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(X_{1}\cup X_{2})=X_{1}\cup X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(X_{1}\cup X_{2})=X_{1}\cup X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S13.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S13.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S13.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}^{*}\Omega_{0}=\Omega_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}^{*}\Omega_{0}=\Omega_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> outside <math id="S13.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>
for each <math id="S13.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S13.SS3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S13.SS3" title="13.3 Relative version of Moser’s method ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.3</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove part I of the proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">The proof
follows the lines of the proof of the standard, absolute, version of the same result, as long as one can find a smooth family <math id="S13.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\lambda_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\lambda_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, of 1-forms on <math id="S13.SS3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> so that for all <math id="S13.SS3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S13.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\lambda_{t}=\frac{d}{dt}\Omega_{t}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\lambda_{t}=\frac{d}{dt}\Omega_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S13.SS3.p13.2" class="ltx_p">and all the forms <math id="S13.SS3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, vanish on a neighborhood of <math id="S13.SS3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">In order to find such a family, pick first a smooth family <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mu_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mu_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, of 1-forms on <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</p>
<table id="S13.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\mu_{t}=\frac{d}{dt}\Omega_{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\mu_{t}=\frac{d}{dt}\Omega_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S13.SS3.p14.2" class="ltx_p">for all <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> (this can be done e.g. using Hodge theory – see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDS</a>, Thm. 3.2.4]</cite>). Note that all the forms <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, vanish on a neighborhood of <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Now, since <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(X;{\mathbb{R}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(X;{\mathbb{R}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, it is easy to show that there exists a smooth family <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{F_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{F_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, of functions defined on a
neighborhood <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
so that <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dF_{t}=\mu_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dF_{t}=\mu_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Extend these functions from possibly a smaller neighborhood <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}\subset U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}\subset U</annotation></semantics></math> of the set <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
to a smooth family of functions on <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. By a slight abuse of notation, denote the latter family of functions on <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> also by <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{F_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{F_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m22" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{\lambda_{t}:=\mu_{t}-dF_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\lambda_{t}:=\mu_{t}-dF_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, is the wanted family of 1-forms on <math id="S13.SS3.p14.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.p15.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof of part I.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Part II is proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>, Cor. 4.1.B]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Part III is proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDP</a>, Prop. 4.1.C]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.p18.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof
of the proposition.
<span id="S13.SS3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S13.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">13.4 </span>Alexander’s trick</h3>

<div id="S13.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S13.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a compact domain with piecewise-smooth boundary that is starshaped with respect to the origin.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S13.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 13.4 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S13.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:W\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:W\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> is an embedding. Let <math id="S13.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:W\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:W\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the inclusion map.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The following claims hold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">I. Let <math id="S13.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> be an open neighborhood <math id="S13.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S13.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that the embedding <math id="S13.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is symplectic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S13.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S13.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math>,
then there exists a smooth family of symplectic embeddings <math id="S13.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{t}:W\to U\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{t}:W\to U\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S13.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}=\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}=\iota</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}=f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}=f</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, all <math id="S13.SS4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, can be made arbitrarily <math id="S13.SS4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math>, provided
<math id="S13.SS4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S13.SS4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">II.
Assume that the embedding <math id="S13.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is holomorphic, <math id="S13.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S13.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(W)\subset W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(W)\subset W</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there exists a smooth family of holomorphic embeddings <math id="S13.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{t}:W\to W\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{t}:W\to W\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S13.SS4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}=\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}=\iota</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}=f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}=f</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S13.SS4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S13.SS4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S13.SS4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S13.SS4" title="13.4 Alexander’s trick ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.4</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove part I.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S13.SS4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S13.SS4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math>,
then, composing <math id="S13.SS4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, if necessary, with a family of small affine symplectomorphisms staring at <math id="S13.SS4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Id</annotation></semantics></math>, we may assume without
loss of generality, that <math id="S13.SS4.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S13.SS4.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="df(0)=Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">df(0)=Id</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the following family of maps <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> defined on <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S13.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}(x):=g(tx)/t,\ \textrm{if}\ t\in(0,1]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}(x):=g(tx)/t,\ \textrm{if}\ t\in(0,1],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S13.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{0}=\iota." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{0}=\iota.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S13.SS4.p11.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math>,
then all the maps <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are symplectic embeddings taking values in <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. The family <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> may not be smooth at <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> but for an appropriate smooth function <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi:[0,1]\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi:[0,1]\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(1)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(1)=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
the family <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g_{\chi(t)}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g_{\chi(t)}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth family of symplectic embeddings <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to U</annotation></semantics></math> connecting <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math>.
The construction clearly shows that
all <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, can be made arbitrarily <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math>, provided
<math id="S13.SS4.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p11.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math>.
This finishes the proof of part I.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS4.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Let us prove part II.
For <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> consider the following family of maps <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> defined on <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S13.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}(z):=f(tz)/Ct,\ \textrm{if}\ t\in(0,1]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>if</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}(z):=f(tz)/Ct,\ \textrm{if}\ t\in(0,1],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S13.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S13.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{0}:=df(0)/C." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{0}:=df(0)/C.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S13.SS4.p12.2" class="ltx_p">For a sufficiently large <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm{Im}\,}g_{t}\subset W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm{Im}\,}g_{t}\subset W</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The family <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g_{t}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> may not be smooth at <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> but for an appropriate smooth function <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi:[0,1]\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi:[0,1]\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(1)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(1)=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
the family <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g_{\chi(t)}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g_{\chi(t)}\}_{0\leq t\leq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth family of holomorphic embeddings <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,0)\to(W,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,0)\to(W,0)</annotation></semantics></math> connecting <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}=f/C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}=f/C</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large, the linear holomorphic embedding <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be easily connected by a smooth family of holomorphic embeddings <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,0)\to(W,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,0)\to(W,0)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Id/C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Id/C</annotation></semantics></math> and the latter can be connected to <math id="S13.SS4.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Id</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S13.SS4.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS4.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S13.SS4.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 13.5 </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS4.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S13.SS4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a manifold and <math id="S13.SS4.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> a Kähler-type symplectic form on <math id="S13.SS4.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S13.SS4.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:W\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:W\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a Kähler-type/tame/<math id="S13.SS4.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame embedding (in the latter case we assume that <math id="S13.SS4.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is closed).</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS4.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Then any symplectic embedding <math id="S13.SS4.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}:W\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}:W\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently <math id="S13.SS4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is of Kähler-type/tame/<math id="S13.SS4.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame,
and there exists <math id="S13.SS4.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S13.SS4.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\circ f=f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\circ f=f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The symplectomorphism <math id="S13.SS4.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> can be made arbitrarily <math id="S13.SS4.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Id" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Id</annotation></semantics></math> provided <math id="S13.SS4.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S13.SS4.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS4.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S13.SS4.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof of <a href="#S13.SS4" title="13.4 Alexander’s trick ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.4</span></a>:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS4.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S13.SS4.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> extends to an <math id="S13.SS4.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>-holomorphic symplectic embedding <math id="S13.SS4.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:U\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:U\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S13.SS4.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is a neighborhood of <math id="S13.SS4.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S13.SS4.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S13.SS4.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is a complex structure on <math id="S13.SS4.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> with the relevant properties as needed for a Kähler-type/tame/<math id="S13.SS4.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS4.p18.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}:W\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}:W\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> be a symplectic embedding (extending to a symplectic embedding of an open neighborhood of <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>). If <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}(W)\subset f(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}(W)\subset f(U)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S13.SS4.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}\circ f^{\prime}:W\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}\circ f^{\prime}:W\to U</annotation></semantics></math> is a symplectic embedding that can be made arbitrarily <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to the identity. By part I of <a href="#S13.SS4" title="13.4 Alexander’s trick ‣ 13 Appendix ‣ Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13.4</span></a>, it can be connected to the inclusion <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota:W\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota:W\to U</annotation></semantics></math> by a smooth family of symplectic
embeddings <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to U</annotation></semantics></math> arbitrarily <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to the identity, provided <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. Composing these embeddings with <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, we get a smooth family of symplectic embeddings <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}:W\to(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}:W\to(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,
connecting <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. These embeddings can be made arbitrarily <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, provided <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p18.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS4.p19.1" class="ltx_p">By a standard result concerning extension of symplectic isotopies (proved as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDS</a>, Thm. 3.3.1]</cite>),
there exists <math id="S13.SS4.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Symp</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in\mathrm{Symp}_{0}(M,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S13.SS4.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\circ f=f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\circ f=f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The symplectomorphism <math id="S13.SS4.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> can be chosen to be arbitrarily <math id="S13.SS4.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to the identity, if the symplectic
embeddings <math id="S13.SS4.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S13.SS4.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, are sufficiently <math id="S13.SS4.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, which is true if <math id="S13.SS4.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently <math id="S13.SS4.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="S13.SS4.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.
The symplectic embedding <math id="S13.SS4.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is of Kähler-type/tame/<math id="S13.SS4.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-tame because it is holomorphic with respect to the
complex structure <math id="S13.SS4.p19.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{*}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{*}I</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S13.SS4.p19.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying the relevant properties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS4.p20.1" class="ltx_p">This finishes the proof of the corollary.
<span id="S13.SS4.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.0pt;height:5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S13.SS4.p21" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S13.SS4.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S13.SS4.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgements:</span> We are grateful to Y.Karshon, D.Novikov, H.Nuer, L.Polterovich and M.Prokhorova for useful discussions. We thank L.Polterovich and D.Salamon for comments on the preliminary version of this paper, and
the anonymous referee for numerous comments and corrections.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
